32 hours. Further study is needed to determine the role of aluminum and chlorine on the densification and conductivity of LLZTO by using a zirconium salt that does not contain chlorine, using different levels of aluminum by adding Al salt in the co-precipitation process, and sintering with aluminum-free crucibles."}, {"context": [{"line": 6, "text": ""}, {"line": 7, "text": "# Introduction"}, {"line": 8, "text": ""}, {"line": 9, "text": "Interest in Li-ion batteries (LIBs) is constantly growing from portable electronics; to electric vehicles; to flexible batteries in wearable electronics.1–3 Along with this increasing demand for LIBs, substitution of conventional flammable and volatile liquid electrolytes with inorganic solid-state electrolytes (SSEs) is essential to improve the safety and capabilities of the batteries.4 Thin film based all-solid-state batteries (ASSBs) facilitate miniaturization of microelectronic devices and flexible batteries with higher safety and energy densities.2 In addition to the chemical and electrochemical stability, mechanical stability in particular at the interface of SSE/electrode of ASSBs is also essential for the batteries performances. On one hand, microcracks can form due to elevated internal and/or external stresses, leading to the crack propagation, delamination, and fragmentation of electrodes and SSEs/electrode interfaces. For example, electrode volume changes during charging/discharging increases internal stresses and introduces bending/ stretching to the SSE which eventually aggregate the formation of microcracks.5–9 On the other hand, Li dendrite formation and growth introduces stress at the SSE/Li electrode interface that results in mechanical deformation which is the very complex phenomena because all chemical, electrochemical, and mechanical factors are involved.6,10 Two main types of SSEs are polymer and inorganic electrolytes. Polymer SSEs are believed to facilitate the interfacial contact issues due to their soft and flexible nature, however the volume changes of electrodes especially Li anode, introduce partial delamination at the interface.11 Inorganic ceramic SSEs are also fabricated because of their higher strength and stability in air, while their poor interface stability remains as an issue.12 Among thin film fabrication techniques, atomic layer deposition (ALD) enables uniform, pinhole-free ultra-thin films which provides good interfacial contact between inorganic SSEs/ electrodes.13–15 Despite such contact establishment, the contact needs to be mechanically stable. Previous research has suggested that elastic deformation of SSEs materials accompanied by some degree of ductility is desirable to maintain good interfacial contact with an electrode during cycling in order to preserve the battery cycling performance.13,14,16 Low stiffness sulfide SSEs with a low range of Young’s modulus (E) (14–25 GPa for Li2S-P2S5) 8,17,18 were considered as favorable SSEs for the bulk battery design to accommodate volume changes of the electrodes.8 However, Bucci et al.19 developed a cohesive zone model to simulate damage evaluation and presented quantitative conditions that facture would occur. They indicated that electrolytes with E ∼ 15 GPa were more prone to micro-cracking in compare to SSEs with higher E. Based on this model for SSEs with E = 15 GPa, fracture was prevented when the electrode expansion was below 7.5%, and the fracture energy of SSEs was greater than Gc = 4 J m−2 . In addition, oxide electrolytes exhibiting high E (77, 150, and 192 GPa in amorphous LLZO, LIPON, and Li La TiO , respectively)16,20,21 have been reported; however, the impact of inorganic SSEs mechanical behavior on battery performance is unknown. Moreover, there are a few models that incorporated electro-chemo-mechanical behavior of solid electrolytes to address interface stability in contact with Li metal. Li nucleation in the grain boundary of ceramic electrolyte was modeled by Raj and Wolfenstine22 with coupling mechanical stresses and the electrical potential. They showed that critical current above Li nucleation depends on ionic conductivity and fracture strength of the SSE. Monroe and Newman23,24 modeled the lithium deposition in a polymer electrolyte and considered the contribution of bulk and surface stresses to Li deposition electrochemical reactions. They concluded that deposition stability increased by increasing electrolyte shear modulus because of more contribution of bulk pressure and surface stresses. Based on Monroe and Newman model,24 researchers constructed hybrid SSEs with high modulus (elastic of shear modulus) to suppress dendrite formation and growth, however it has been found that Li dendrite can grow and penetrate both polymer and rigid ceramics.25–27 It is still unclear which model can explain the mechanisms of Li dendrite growth. The SSE modulus is not the only mechanical factor that limits the Li dendrite growth and other mechanical properties such as tensile strength, fracture toughness, and flexure strength of brittle inorganic SSEs require more attention. Recently organic, inorganic and hybrid coating layers have shown potentials for suppressing Li dendrite at the interface of SSE/ Li metal.15 Fu et al.,28 designed and developed a hybrid system of polymer embedded with nano-sized ceramic domains called as “soft ceramic” structure as dendrite-suppressing SSE by applying a universal chemomechanical model that can assess fundamentally pressure- or density-driven dendrite suppressing. In addition to all the efforts on modelling and designing compatible interfaces, a study by LePage et al. 29 showed that “creep” is a dominant deformation mechanism for Li metal in batteries which further complicates the interfacial mechanical stability issues. These studies show that better understanding of mechanical behavior of nanoscale organic and inorganic SSEs are essential prior to investigate their theoretical and experimental electro-chemomechanical implications."}, {"line": 10, "text": ""}, {"line": 11, "text": "Previously studied amorphous LPO and LTO thin films with moderate ionic conductivity (∼10−8 S cm−1 at 25 °C) have been used as SSEs for ASSBs,30,31 and as coating layers on the electrodes of LIBs to significantly improve the battery performance.31–35 A summary of electrochemical studies of the impact of LTO and LPO coatings on electrode materials in LIBs cyclic performance is presented in Table 1. Both LPO and LTO exhibited electrochemical and chemical stability, and ionic conductivity for coating cathode materials as well as facilitating better contact between electrode materials and electrolytes as shown in Table 1.31–34 The mechanism behind preserving the structural degradation of electrode material is not well understood and a more detailed understanding of the mechanical stability of SSEs in ASSBs against internal and/or external stresses during charging/discharging is needed.14,15 In this study, the elastic and fracture behavior of ALD prepared LTO, and LPO SSEs thin films were studied and compared for the first time. The mechanical behavior of a range of ultra-thin ALD prepared suspended SSEs thin films (5 nm–30 nm thickness) were studied using an atomic force microscope (AFM) film deflection technique. The film under AFM deflection emulates stress levels present in the SSE at the nanoscale, and the results will help to identify the potential influence of SSEs mechanical performances for on their operation in flexible ASSBs."}, {"line": 12, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ _ 10.1039_c9nr02176k", "line": 9, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> _ 10.1039_c9nr02176k.md", "submatches": [{"end": 5081, "start": 3142}], "text": "Interest in Li-ion batteries (LIBs) is constantly growing from portable electronics; to electric vehicles; to flexible batteries in wearable electronics.1–3 Along with this increasing demand for LIBs, substitution of conventional flammable and volatile liquid electrolytes with inorganic solid-state electrolytes (SSEs) is essential to improve the safety and capabilities of the batteries.4 Thin film based all-solid-state batteries (ASSBs) facilitate miniaturization of microelectronic devices and flexible batteries with higher safety and energy densities.2 In addition to the chemical and electrochemical stability, mechanical stability in particular at the interface of SSE/electrode of ASSBs is also essential for the batteries performances. On one hand, microcracks can form due to elevated internal and/or external stresses, leading to the crack propagation, delamination, and fragmentation of electrodes and SSEs/electrode interfaces. For example, electrode volume changes during charging/discharging increases internal stresses and introduces bending/ stretching to the SSE which eventually aggregate the formation of microcracks.5–9 On the other hand, Li dendrite formation and growth introduces stress at the SSE/Li electrode interface that results in mechanical deformation which is the very complex phenomena because all chemical, electrochemical, and mechanical factors are involved.6,10 Two main types of SSEs are polymer and inorganic electrolytes. Polymer SSEs are believed to facilitate the interfacial contact issues due to their soft and flexible nature, however the volume changes of electrodes especially Li anode, introduce partial delamination at the interface.11 Inorganic ceramic SSEs are also fabricated because of their higher strength and stability in air, while their poor interface stability remains as an issue.12 Among thin film fabrication techniques, atomic layer deposition (ALD) enables uniform, pinhole-free ultra-thin films which provides good interfacial contact between inorganic SSEs/ electrodes.13–15 Despite such contact establishment, the contact needs to be mechanically stable. Previous research has suggested that elastic deformation of SSEs materials accompanied by some degree of ductility is desirable to maintain good interfacial contact with an electrode during cycling in order to preserve the battery cycling performance.13,14,16 Low stiffness sulfide SSEs with a low range of Young’s modulus (E) (14–25 GPa for Li2S-P2S5) 8,17,18 were considered as favorable SSEs for the bulk battery design to accommodate volume changes of the electrodes.8 However, Bucci et al.19 developed a cohesive zone model to simulate damage evaluation and presented quantitative conditions that facture would occur. They indicated that electrolytes with E ∼ 15 GPa were more prone to micro-cracking in compare to SSEs with higher E. Based on this model for SSEs with E = 15 GPa, fracture was prevented when the electrode expansion was below 7.5%, and the fracture energy of SSEs was greater than Gc = 4 J m−2 . In addition, oxide electrolytes exhibiting high E (77, 150, and 192 GPa in amorphous LLZO, LIPON, and Li La TiO , respectively)16,20,21 have been reported; however, the impact of inorganic SSEs mechanical behavior on battery performance is unknown. Moreover, there are a few models that incorporated electro-chemo-mechanical behavior of solid electrolytes to address interface stability in contact with Li metal. Li nucleation in the grain boundary of ceramic electrolyte was modeled by Raj and Wolfenstine22 with coupling mechanical stresses and the electrical potential. They showed that critical current above Li nucleation depends on ionic conductivity and fracture strength of the SSE. Monroe and Newman23,24 modeled the lithium deposition in a polymer electrolyte and considered the contribution of bulk and surface stresses to Li deposition electrochemical reactions. They concluded that deposition stability increased by increasing electrolyte shear modulus because of more contribution of bulk pressure and surface stresses. Based on Monroe and Newman model,24 researchers constructed hybrid SSEs with high modulus (elastic of shear modulus) to suppress dendrite formation and growth, however it has been found that Li dendrite can grow and penetrate both polymer and rigid ceramics.25–27 It is still unclear which model can explain the mechanisms of Li dendrite growth. The SSE modulus is not the only mechanical factor that limits the Li dendrite growth and other mechanical properties such as tensile strength, fracture toughness, and flexure strength of brittle inorganic SSEs require more attention. Recently organic, inorganic and hybrid coating layers have shown potentials for suppressing Li dendrite at the interface of SSE/ Li metal.15 Fu et al.,28 designed and developed a hybrid system of polymer embedded with nano-sized ceramic domains called as “soft ceramic” structure as dendrite-suppressing SSE by applying a universal chemomechanical model that can assess fundamentally pressure- or density-driven dendrite suppressing. In addition to all the efforts on modelling and designing compatible interfaces, a study by LePage et al. 29 showed that “creep” is a dominant deformation mechanism for Li metal in batteries which further complicates the interfacial mechanical stability issues. These studies show that better understanding of mechanical behavior of nanoscale organic and inorganic SSEs are essential prior to investigate their theoretical and experimental electro-chemomechanical implications."}], "ok": true, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO).*(grain boundary|microstructure).*(conductivity|density)", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": true, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 13, "truncated": false}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:09:22.800121+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:15:{"duration_ms": 45031.884, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 1, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 20, "query": "(?i)(LLZO|Li7La3Zr2O12).{0,120}(co[- ]?precipitation|coprecipitation)", "regex": true, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 25, "text": ""}, {"line": 26, "text": "In this work, Ta-doped LLZO (LLZTO) was synthesized through a co-precipitation method with a low sintering temperature of 1100°C. The effects of a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which is created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder, on the phase stabilization, densification and Li+ conductivity were examined. LiOH was chosen as the additive since it is one of the starting materials."}, {"line": 27, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 26, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 78, "start": 23}], "text": "In this work, Ta-doped LLZO (LLZTO) was synthesized through a co-precipitation method with a low sintering temperature of 1100°C. The effects of a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which is created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder, on the phase stabilization, densification and Li+ conductivity were examined. LiOH was chosen as the additive since it is one of the starting materials."}, {"context": [{"line": 111, "text": "10. Yoshida, T., Honda, A., and Sato, Y., Aluminiumdoped Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte and process for producing the same, EEC Patent no. EP 2159867 A1, 2010.   "}, {"line": 112, "text": "11. Shao, C., Liu, H., Yu, Z., Zheng, Z., Sun, N., and Diao, C., Structure and ionic conductivity of cubic Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by chemical co-precipitation method, Solid State Ionics, 2016, vol. 287, p. 13.   "}, {"line": 113, "text": "12. Zhao, P., Cao, G., Jin, Z., Ming, H., Wen, Y., Xu, Y., Zhu, X., Xiang, Y., and Zhang, S., Self-consolidation mechanism and its application in the preparation of Aldoped cubic Li7La3Zr2O12, Mater. Des., 2018, vol. 139, p. 65.   "}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132", "line": 112, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132.md", "submatches": [{"end": 175, "start": 107}], "text": "11. Shao, C., Liu, H., Yu, Z., Zheng, Z., Sun, N., and Diao, C., Structure and ionic conductivity of cubic Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by chemical co-precipitation method, Solid State Ionics, 2016, vol. 287, p. 13.   "}], "ok": true, "query": "(?i)(LLZO|Li7La3Zr2O12).{0,120}(co[- ]?precipitation|coprecipitation)", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": true, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 2, "truncated": false}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:09:28.334109+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:16:{"duration_ms": 26371.115, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 3, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 30, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO).*(ionic conductivity|total conductivity)", "regex": true, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 5, "text": "1Materials Research and Education Center, Auburn University, Auburn, Alabama 36849, USA   "}, {"line": 6, "text": "2Department of Chemical Engineering, Auburn University, Auburn, Alabama 36849, USA"}, {"line": 7, "text": ""}, {"line": 8, "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}, {"line": 9, "text": ""}, {"line": 10, "text": "© The Author(s) 2019. Published by ECS. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License (CC BY, http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted reuse of the work in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. [DOI: 10.1149/2.1031915jes]"}, {"line": 11, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 8, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 722, "start": 26}], "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}, {"context": [{"line": 11, "text": ""}, {"line": 12, "text": "Manuscript submitted September 6, 2019; revised manuscript received October 28, 2019. Published November 13, 2019."}, {"line": 13, "text": ""}, {"line": 14, "text": "Using a solid-state electrolyte instead of the commonly used organic liquid electrolyte to produce an all-solid-state lithium-ion battery is regarded as one promising way to overcome safety issues, such as flammability and leakage of electrolyte.1–4 A large number of solid-state electrolytes have been reported, such as solid polymer electrolytes using different Li salts of LiClO4, LiBF4, or LiN(CF3SO2)2,5,6 and ceramic electrolytes including perovskite-type titanates (LLTO),7,8 NASICON-type (sodium super ionic conductor) oxide electrolytes,9,10 sulfide-type and garnet-type materials.11 The cubic garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention due to its high chemical stability and high ionic conductivity (8.7 10−4 S/cm at 25°C).12–15"}, {"line": 15, "text": ""}, {"line": 16, "text": "Besides the cubic structure, garnet-type LLZO can also exist with a tetragonal crystal structure with a Li+ conductivity of around 2 × 10−6 S/cm,16,17 which is two orders of magnitude lower than the conductivity of the cubic phase structure. In the cubic crystal structure, Li ions occupy two types of crystallographic sites. A tetrahedral site, Li(1), and a distorted octahedral site, Li(2).16 In the tetragonal phase structure, Li atoms occupy three types of crystallographic sites: a tetrahedral site, Li(1), and two distorted octahedral sites, Li(2) and Li(3).17 The lower conductivity of the tetragonal crystal structure has been attributed to the ordering of Li atoms and Li vacancies in the tetrahedral and octahedral sites, as the tetrahedral or distorted octahedral sites are either full or empty.18 In the cubic phase, however, the Li sub-lattice is disordered, so both types of Li sites are partially occupied and the Li ion vacancies are also not ordered.18–20 In the tetragonal phase, all Li sites are fully occupied (g 1), while a reduced site occupancy of Li(1) site (g 0.94) and Li(2) site (g 0.35) were found in the cubic phase.17,21 Thus, compared to the tetragonal phase, in the cubic phase the larger number of vacancies and the disorder in the Li sub-lattice enable a lower activation energy for Li motion, which enhance the Li-ion conductivity in the cubic phase.22"}, {"line": 17, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 14, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 728, "start": 631}], "text": "Using a solid-state electrolyte instead of the commonly used organic liquid electrolyte to produce an all-solid-state lithium-ion battery is regarded as one promising way to overcome safety issues, such as flammability and leakage of electrolyte.1–4 A large number of solid-state electrolytes have been reported, such as solid polymer electrolytes using different Li salts of LiClO4, LiBF4, or LiN(CF3SO2)2,5,6 and ceramic electrolytes including perovskite-type titanates (LLTO),7,8 NASICON-type (sodium super ionic conductor) oxide electrolytes,9,10 sulfide-type and garnet-type materials.11 The cubic garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention due to its high chemical stability and high ionic conductivity (8.7 10−4 S/cm at 25°C).12–15"}, {"context": [{"line": 17, "text": ""}, {"line": 18, "text": "Doping elements are usually used to stabilize the cubic phase from the tetragonal phase, which is performed by substituting cations for Li, La or Zr.18,23 Substitution of Al, Ga, Fe for Li,24–28 Rb, Nd, Ca, Ba for La,29–31 and/or Nb, Ta, Y for Zr30,32–35 have been reported. Aluminum contamination from the crucible has been found to be helpful in stabilizing the cubic phase.25 The reaction between Al2O3 from the crucible and LiOH or Li2CO3 has resulted in the production of an Al-containing phase, which plays two roles. A small amount of Al in the garnet phase could stabilize the cubic structure relative to the tetragonal phase.25 Secondly, a liquid phase introduced by the reaction between Li2O and Al2O3 could facilitate the sintering.27 According to the Li2O-Al2O3 phase diagram, a eutectic liquid forms at 23 mol% Al2O3, which has a melting point of 1055°C.36 This Al-containing phase has also been reported in other studies, and is typically located between grains.13,27,37"}, {"line": 19, "text": ""}, {"line": 20, "text": "The phase stability and the conductivity are affected by the Al incorporation, as the substitution mechanism of one Al atom for three Li atoms leads to a reduction of the Li content and an increase in the Li vacancies.25 Although the stabilization of cubic phase by Al doping could help to obtain high conductivity,12,16,17 an undesired Li5AlO4 phase forming at higher doping levels, has lower Li+ conductivity than LLZO.38,39 In addition, it has been reported that the substitutional Al ions sit in the conductive pathway of Li, lead to lower conductivity.25,40 The substitutional Ta ions occupy Li sites, which will not hinder the Li-ion motion.12 The addition of Al into Ta-doped LLZO has been shown to lead to a decrease in the ionic conductivity from 8.7 × 10−4 S/cm to 3.7 × 10−4 S/cm.12 The conductivity of Al-doped LLZO has been reported as 4 10−4 S/cm, while Ta-doped LLZO could reach 8.7 10−4 S/cm.12,41 In addition to the higher ionic conductivity, Ta-doped LLZO was also found to be stable with lithium metal electrode.34,40 While in Al-doped LLZO, intergranular Li metal was observed to propagate through the grain boundaries.42 Therefore, Ta was chosen as the doping element in this work."}, {"line": 21, "text": ""}, {"line": 22, "text": "A dense microstructure is also needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities (e.g. 8.9 10−6 S/cm at 25°C).43 Hot-pressing can be used to obtain an almost dense microstructure.41,44,45 Other methods like cold isostatic pressing,46–48 field assisted sintering technology,49 flowing oxygen sintering,50 sintering with microwave hybrid furnace,37 have also been reported to be effective methods to increase the density."}, {"line": 23, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 20, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 968, "start": 416}], "text": "The phase stability and the conductivity are affected by the Al incorporation, as the substitution mechanism of one Al atom for three Li atoms leads to a reduction of the Li content and an increase in the Li vacancies.25 Although the stabilization of cubic phase by Al doping could help to obtain high conductivity,12,16,17 an undesired Li5AlO4 phase forming at higher doping levels, has lower Li+ conductivity than LLZO.38,39 In addition, it has been reported that the substitutional Al ions sit in the conductive pathway of Li, lead to lower conductivity.25,40 The substitutional Ta ions occupy Li sites, which will not hinder the Li-ion motion.12 The addition of Al into Ta-doped LLZO has been shown to lead to a decrease in the ionic conductivity from 8.7 × 10−4 S/cm to 3.7 × 10−4 S/cm.12 The conductivity of Al-doped LLZO has been reported as 4 10−4 S/cm, while Ta-doped LLZO could reach 8.7 10−4 S/cm.12,41 In addition to the higher ionic conductivity, Ta-doped LLZO was also found to be stable with lithium metal electrode.34,40 While in Al-doped LLZO, intergranular Li metal was observed to propagate through the grain boundaries.42 Therefore, Ta was chosen as the doping element in this work."}, {"context": [{"line": 61, "text": ""}, {"line": 62, "text": "SEM images of fracture cross sections of 30Li and 65Li are shown in Figure 3. After sintering with 15Li cover powder, large pores were observed in Figures 3a–3d. When the cover powder contained LiOH, fewer pores were observed as shown in Figures 3e–3h. The fracture surface of the pellets sintered with 15Li cover powder consisted of smooth rounded surfaces (Figures 3b–3d), but in the sample with 15Li + LiOH cover powder, flat surface indicative of brittle or intergranular facture surface were observed (Figures 3f–3h) indicating that the grains are more connected when LiOH was added in the cover powder. These results indicate that LiOH in the cover powder helped with the sintering of LLZTO. An interparticle phase between grains was observed in Figures 3f–3h, which is circled in the figures and found from EDS analysis to contain aluminum as shown in Figure 4."}, {"line": 63, "text": ""}, {"line": 64, "text": "Room temperature impedance spectra for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with cover powders of 15Li or 15Li LiOH are shown in Figure 5. As shown in the typical Nyquist plot for LLZTO pellets in Figure 5, the spectra were fit to an equivalent circuit of L(RtCPEt)(ReCPEe), where L, R and CPE are the inductor, resistor and the constant phase element, and the subscript t and e refer to the total and electrode contribution, respectively. The inductive contribution is attributed to the wires connected to the sample and is not used for the sample properties. The first semicircle is attributed to the total resistance (Rt). The total conductivity is calculated as σtotal t / (A Rt), where t is the sample thickness and A is the cross-sectional area of the sample. The grain and grain boundary contributions overlapped with each other and cannot be separated, which is consistent with other works.53,56,57 The RT total Li+ conductivity for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with 15Li or 15Li LiOH cover powder are tabulated in Table II. The RT total conductivity of 30Li with 15Li cover powders is 2.0 10−5 S/cm. It is significantly lower than that sintered with 15Li LiOH, which is 6.9 10−4 S/cm. Compared to the 30 wt% LiOH samples, samples with higher lithium content (65Li) showed a lower RT σtotal when the cover was 15Li, but similar RT σtotal when the cover contained LiOH. 75Li also showed a similar RT σtotal with 30wt% and 65 wt% LiOH samples when the cover contained LiOH, which indicates that the amount of Li content in the pellets does not affect RT σtotal once it is over 30 wt%. The higher conductivity is consistent with the higher relative density in the samples sintered with 15Li + LiOH cover."}, {"line": 65, "text": ""}, {"line": 66, "text": "Arrhenius plots of total Li+ conductivities for 30Li and 65Li sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with different cover powders are shown in Figure 6 and the corresponding activation energies (Ea) are tabulated in Table II. The activation energy for all samples is from 0.20 to 0.39 eV, but the conductivities of samples sintered with 15Li LiOH cover are significantly higher than those sintered with a 15Li cover powder. The activation energy of 0.2 eV for 30Li sintered with 15Li LiOH cover is lower than other reported values which are in the range of 0.3–0.4 eV.16,32,35,40,58,59 The EIS tests were performed in a narrow temperature range (20–40°C). Further EIS study is needed to figure out the reason for this low activation energy by obtaining more data points in the EIS tests on 30Li samples, which could be realized by performing the EIS test from a lower temperatures (below 20°C) to 40°C or in a shorter time interval between 20 and 40°C, like 5°C interval time instead of currently used 10°C."}, {"line": 67, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 64, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1103, "start": 198}], "text": "Room temperature impedance spectra for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with cover powders of 15Li or 15Li LiOH are shown in Figure 5. As shown in the typical Nyquist plot for LLZTO pellets in Figure 5, the spectra were fit to an equivalent circuit of L(RtCPEt)(ReCPEe), where L, R and CPE are the inductor, resistor and the constant phase element, and the subscript t and e refer to the total and electrode contribution, respectively. The inductive contribution is attributed to the wires connected to the sample and is not used for the sample properties. The first semicircle is attributed to the total resistance (Rt). The total conductivity is calculated as σtotal t / (A Rt), where t is the sample thickness and A is the cross-sectional area of the sample. The grain and grain boundary contributions overlapped with each other and cannot be separated, which is consistent with other works.53,56,57 The RT total Li+ conductivity for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with 15Li or 15Li LiOH cover powder are tabulated in Table II. The RT total conductivity of 30Li with 15Li cover powders is 2.0 10−5 S/cm. It is significantly lower than that sintered with 15Li LiOH, which is 6.9 10−4 S/cm. Compared to the 30 wt% LiOH samples, samples with higher lithium content (65Li) showed a lower RT σtotal when the cover was 15Li, but similar RT σtotal when the cover contained LiOH. 75Li also showed a similar RT σtotal with 30wt% and 65 wt% LiOH samples when the cover contained LiOH, which indicates that the amount of Li content in the pellets does not affect RT σtotal once it is over 30 wt%. The higher conductivity is consistent with the higher relative density in the samples sintered with 15Li + LiOH cover."}, {"context": [{"line": 12, "text": ""}, {"line": 13, "text": "Recently, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been widely used as power sources for portable electronic devices, such as mobile phones, laptops and for electric vehicles owing to their high energy and power density [1–3]. However, common LIBs are based on flammable organic liquid electrolytes, which carry the inherent risks of leakage, fire, and explosion. For this reason, solid-state electrolytes with fast Li-ion diffusion were recognized as promising alternatives [4,5]. Thus, extensive efforts have been made to develop lithium-ion conductive inorganic materials, including NASICON, perovskite, and sulfide glasses, for use as solid electrolytes, an important component of all-solid lithium-ion batteries [6–10]."}, {"line": 14, "text": ""}, {"line": 15, "text": "In general, the solid electrolytes that have been studied extensively are mainly composed of sulfides and oxides; Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is probably one of the most promising candidates to date for use in all-solid-state Liion batteries on account of its excellent chemical and electrochemical stabilities while exhibiting high Li-ion conductivity compared to those of other garnet structure conductors such as Li5La3M2O12 (M = Nb, Ta) [5,11– 13]. LLZO is a garnet-type material that has two stable phases: tetragonal and cubic. Although the tetragonal phase can be obtained at lower synthesis temperatures than the cubic phase, the cubic phase has a bulk conductivity of ∼ 10−3 S/cm compared to ∼ 10−6 S/cm for tetragonal LLZO [14]. For high ionic conductivity, the LLZO solid electrolytes should maintain a stable cubic structure, which usually require several calcination steps and long sintering times at temperatures greater than 1000 - 1200 ◦C [15–17]. However, during this hightemperature thermal process, significant Li loss unavoidably occurs, which often results in a structural collapse of LLZO accompanied by the formation of second phases, thereby reducing the ionic conductivity [18]. Doping with external elements is a well-known method to stabilize the cubic phase at lower temperatures compared to the conventional 1000 - 1200 ◦C range and increase Li+ conductivity by introducing additional Li-ion vacancies, which balance the ionic charge [19–24]. However, doping has the disadvantage of being difficult to control; therefore, finding other ways to stabilize the cubic phase at room temperature is of at most importance."}, {"line": 16, "text": ""}, {"line": 17, "text": "In this research, cubic LLZO was synthesized through a solution-based synthesis method without any extrinsic dopants at temperatures lower than 1000 ◦C. Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) was used as the chelating agent, and the cubic phase was confirmed by using XRay diffraction. Finally, AC impedance measurements were conducted to evaluate the electrochemical performance of the synthesized material."}, {"line": 18, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 15, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1203, "start": 128}], "text": "In general, the solid electrolytes that have been studied extensively are mainly composed of sulfides and oxides; Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is probably one of the most promising candidates to date for use in all-solid-state Liion batteries on account of its excellent chemical and electrochemical stabilities while exhibiting high Li-ion conductivity compared to those of other garnet structure conductors such as Li5La3M2O12 (M = Nb, Ta) [5,11– 13]. LLZO is a garnet-type material that has two stable phases: tetragonal and cubic. Although the tetragonal phase can be obtained at lower synthesis temperatures than the cubic phase, the cubic phase has a bulk conductivity of ∼ 10−3 S/cm compared to ∼ 10−6 S/cm for tetragonal LLZO [14]. For high ionic conductivity, the LLZO solid electrolytes should maintain a stable cubic structure, which usually require several calcination steps and long sintering times at temperatures greater than 1000 - 1200 ◦C [15–17]. However, during this hightemperature thermal process, significant Li loss unavoidably occurs, which often results in a structural collapse of LLZO accompanied by the formation of second phases, thereby reducing the ionic conductivity [18]. Doping with external elements is a well-known method to stabilize the cubic phase at lower temperatures compared to the conventional 1000 - 1200 ◦C range and increase Li+ conductivity by introducing additional Li-ion vacancies, which balance the ionic charge [19–24]. However, doping has the disadvantage of being difficult to control; therefore, finding other ways to stabilize the cubic phase at room temperature is of at most importance."}, {"context": [{"line": 32, "text": ""}, {"line": 33, "text": "The microstructure of the Li7La3Zr2O12 powder and the cross-section of the pellet observed using SEM and AFM are shown in Fig. 3. As one can observe in the images, the particles are uneven, and the pellet has large particles and larger voids. The voids were revealed in the SEM image of the pellet (Fig. 3(c)), and the height deviation is large due to the voids seen in the AFM image (Fig. 3(d)). These voids inside the pellet reduce the density of the material and increase its resistance, thereby reducing its ionic conductivity [17,20]."}, {"line": 34, "text": ""}, {"line": 35, "text": "Figure 4(a) shows the Nyquist plots obtained from AC impedance spectroscopy analyses of the LLZO pellets. The ratios of the bulk and the grain boundary resistances to the total resistance were determined by fitting the experimental data to the equivalent circuit shown in Fig. 4(b). The ionic conductivity of the sample was calculated as follows: σ = L(A R) , where L is the length of the pellet, A is the surface area of the pellet, and R is the Ohmic resistance determined from the AC impedance. The bulk, grain boundary and total ionic conductivities determined in this fashion are 2.91 × 10−5 S/cm, 1.13 × 10−6 S/cm and 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, respectively. The equivalent circuit was of the form RB/CB+RGB/CGB+Wo, where RB is the bulk resistance, RGB is the grain boundary resistance, and Wo is the Warburg element [5,25]. The calculated total Li+ conductivity of LLZO is 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, which is higher than that reported by Kokal et al. [26] (3.12 10−7 S/cm), but is lower than the value for other cubic materials [15, 17]. This is a result of the impurity phase and the many voids observed from the XRD results and the SEM images."}, {"line": 36, "text": ""}, {"line": 37, "text": "![](images/b9d84ce182b242e458ca97cfeae70c23cc3a7444a5414059cbf716aa975aa5c8.jpg)  "}, {"line": 38, "text": "Fig. 3. (Color online) Scanning electron microscopy images of (a) Li7La3Zr2O12 powder and (c) a cross-section of a Li7La3Zr2O12 pellet; (b) and (d) the corresponding atomic force microscopy images."}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 35, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 305, "start": 92}], "text": "Figure 4(a) shows the Nyquist plots obtained from AC impedance spectroscopy analyses of the LLZO pellets. The ratios of the bulk and the grain boundary resistances to the total resistance were determined by fitting the experimental data to the equivalent circuit shown in Fig. 4(b). The ionic conductivity of the sample was calculated as follows: σ = L(A R) , where L is the length of the pellet, A is the surface area of the pellet, and R is the Ohmic resistance determined from the AC impedance. The bulk, grain boundary and total ionic conductivities determined in this fashion are 2.91 × 10−5 S/cm, 1.13 × 10−6 S/cm and 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, respectively. The equivalent circuit was of the form RB/CB+RGB/CGB+Wo, where RB is the bulk resistance, RGB is the grain boundary resistance, and Wo is the Warburg element [5,25]. The calculated total Li+ conductivity of LLZO is 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, which is higher than that reported by Kokal et al. [26] (3.12 10−7 S/cm), but is lower than the value for other cubic materials [15, 17]. This is a result of the impurity phase and the many voids observed from the XRD results and the SEM images."}, {"context": [{"line": 40, "text": "![](images/d239f1334560e93fd35c3a8e1bc19c61a05874c8969666d1ec96d3c528cdb362.jpg)  "}, {"line": 41, "text": "Fig. 4. (Color online) (a) Nyquist plots of Li7La3Zr2O12 measured at 25 ◦C, (b) the equivalent circuit model used to fit the impedance data and (c) Arrhenius plot for the total conductivity of Li7La3Zr2O12."}, {"line": 42, "text": ""}, {"line": 43, "text": "Figure 4(c) shows the temperature dependence of the total ionic conductivity (Arrhenius plot) of the LLZO pellets at temperatures of 25 - 70 ◦C, where the Arrhenius equation is given by σ = AT exp - −EakBT \u0003 , where σ is the ionic conductivity, T is the absolute temperature, A is the pre-exponential constant, kB is the Boltzmann constant and Ea is the activation energy for the ionic conductivity. The activation energy determined from the slope of the log (σT ) versus 1/T plot for the total conductivity of the sample is 0.53 eV. The activation energy for the LLZO material is lower (0.53 eV) than that for the reference LLZO material (0.67 eV) [26]. The lower activation energy is due to a transition from the tetragonal to the cubic phase, and the lower activation energy can improve the conductivity by facilitating the movement of lithium ions [27]."}, {"line": 44, "text": ""}, {"line": 45, "text": "# III. CONCLUSION"}, {"line": 46, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 43, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 514, "start": 101}], "text": "Figure 4(c) shows the temperature dependence of the total ionic conductivity (Arrhenius plot) of the LLZO pellets at temperatures of 25 - 70 ◦C, where the Arrhenius equation is given by σ = AT exp - −EakBT \u0003 , where σ is the ionic conductivity, T is the absolute temperature, A is the pre-exponential constant, kB is the Boltzmann constant and Ea is the activation energy for the ionic conductivity. The activation energy determined from the slope of the log (σT ) versus 1/T plot for the total conductivity of the sample is 0.53 eV. The activation energy for the LLZO material is lower (0.53 eV) than that for the reference LLZO material (0.67 eV) [26]. The lower activation energy is due to a transition from the tetragonal to the cubic phase, and the lower activation energy can improve the conductivity by facilitating the movement of lithium ions [27]."}, {"context": [{"line": 44, "text": ""}, {"line": 45, "text": "# III. CONCLUSION"}, {"line": 46, "text": ""}, {"line": 47, "text": "An LLZO electrolyte was synthesized by using a solution-based method. The sample formed a Li+ conducting cubic phase without any extrinsic dopants at 900 ◦C. Remarkably, cubic materials were synthesized at 900 ◦C, which is lower than the conventional synthesis temperatures of 1000 - 1200 ◦C range, by using a solution-based method. The synthesized LLZO pellet revealed relatively non-uniform particles as a result of impurities and reduced density. As a result, the total ionic conductivity of LLZO was found to be 1.09 10−6 S/cm."}, {"line": 48, "text": ""}, {"line": 49, "text": "# ACKNOWLEDGMENTS"}, {"line": 50, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 47, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 497, "start": 3}], "text": "An LLZO electrolyte was synthesized by using a solution-based method. The sample formed a Li+ conducting cubic phase without any extrinsic dopants at 900 ◦C. Remarkably, cubic materials were synthesized at 900 ◦C, which is lower than the conventional synthesis temperatures of 1000 - 1200 ◦C range, by using a solution-based method. The synthesized LLZO pellet revealed relatively non-uniform particles as a result of impurities and reduced density. As a result, the total ionic conductivity of LLZO was found to be 1.09 10−6 S/cm."}, {"context": [{"line": 178, "text": "Table 5. Investigation of recently reported diffusivity in electrode materials.   "}, {"line": 179, "text": "![](images/44f9a84e0a5757e530f8b8ad9a973426833c798dc64ae92da1efdb475bb9f7b5.jpg)"}, {"line": 180, "text": ""}, {"line": 181, "text": "The investigation of ionic conductivity in solid electrolytes at room temperature reported in the last two years is shown as the histogram in Figure 6 (The highest/lowest ionic conductivity of the various structural families counted here does not represent the limits they can achieve, but only based on the studies reported during this period). We have made a simple classification of the structural family, including Garnet type represented by Li7La3Zr2O12,[57] NASICON-like represented by Li1+xAlxTi2-x(PO4)3 (LATP),[58] Lithium-Sulfide represented by Li3PS4 (LISICON-like) and Li2S-P2S5 glass ceramics,[59] Perovskite represented by Li3xLa2/3−x□1/3−xTiO3 (LLTO),[60] and various composite polymer electrolytes (CPEs).[61] For the Na+ solid-state electrolytes, the ionic conductivity of sodium sulfide[59i,62] and various structures (NASICON type, borohydride, etc.)[61c,63] are mainly counted. The K+ solid-state electrolytes are no longer listed here due to the lack of reports. In the Garnet type structure, the ionic conductivity can be largely regulated by cation doping, the highly self-textured garnet-type lithium-ion conducting solid electrolyte of LLZO-Ga exhibits an extremely high ionic conductivity (2.06 × 10-3 S cm-1 at room temperature).[57c] Similarly, in NASICON-like structure, Li1+xTi2-xScx(PO4)3 doped with Sc exhibits an ion conductivity of up to 2.5 × 10-3 S cm-1 (X = 0.2),[58a] This is comparable to or even better than the LATP[58d,58e] reported in the literature. In Lithium"}, {"line": 182, "text": ""}, {"line": 183, "text": "Sulfides, Li10GeP2S12 with LISICON-like structure has the highest ionic conductivity reported in this kind of materials (more than 10- 2 S cm-1 at room temperature[59e]). The ionic conductivity can be improved by adjusting the ratio of the two phases in the Li2S-P2S5 glass ceramic, the highest ionic conductivity reaches 4.8×10-3 S cm-1 after the doping of MoS2.[59i] The ionic conductivity of the Perovskite structure itself is only about 10-4 S cm-1,[60b,60d] but it could be greatly improved after compounding with other materials.[60a,60c] Studies on solid polymer electrolytes have focused on complexing with metal oxide nanoparticles, LLZO, and various polymers.[61] In the study of Na+ solid-state electrolytes, the equivalent substitution of Na3PS4 showed a significant increase in ionic conductivity,[59i,62a,62b] up to 3.7 × 10-3 S cm-1.[59i] However, the sodium sulfide Na10GeP2S12 with similar structure of Li10GeP2S12 does not show commensurately high ionic conductivity of Na+.[62e] The Na1+nZr2SinP3-nO12 possessing a NASICON type structure achieves ionic conductivity of up to 3.4 × 10-3 S cm-1, and even reaches 1.4 × 10-2 S cm-1 at 80 °C. The ionic conductivities of different structural families are strongly dependent on the internal crystal structure and conduction machanisim, which will be discussed in section 3.3.1. The latest computational studies will also be statistically discussed in the following section."}, {"line": 184, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Recent research on strategies to improve ion conduction in alkali metal‐ion batteries -", "line": 181, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> research on strategies to improve ion conduction in alkali metal‐ion batteries -.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1220, "start": 1167}], "text": "The investigation of ionic conductivity in solid electrolytes at room temperature reported in the last two years is shown as the histogram in Figure 6 (The highest/lowest ionic conductivity of the various structural families counted here does not represent the limits they can achieve, but only based on the studies reported during this period). We have made a simple classification of the structural family, including Garnet type represented by Li7La3Zr2O12,[57] NASICON-like represented by Li1+xAlxTi2-x(PO4)3 (LATP),[58] Lithium-Sulfide represented by Li3PS4 (LISICON-like) and Li2S-P2S5 glass ceramics,[59] Perovskite represented by Li3xLa2/3−x□1/3−xTiO3 (LLTO),[60] and various composite polymer electrolytes (CPEs).[61] For the Na+ solid-state electrolytes, the ionic conductivity of sodium sulfide[59i,62] and various structures (NASICON type, borohydride, etc.)[61c,63] are mainly counted. The K+ solid-state electrolytes are no longer listed here due to the lack of reports. In the Garnet type structure, the ionic conductivity can be largely regulated by cation doping, the highly self-textured garnet-type lithium-ion conducting solid electrolyte of LLZO-Ga exhibits an extremely high ionic conductivity (2.06 × 10-3 S cm-1 at room temperature).[57c] Similarly, in NASICON-like structure, Li1+xTi2-xScx(PO4)3 doped with Sc exhibits an ion conductivity of up to 2.5 × 10-3 S cm-1 (X = 0.2),[58a] This is comparable to or even better than the LATP[58d,58e] reported in the literature. In Lithium"}, {"context": [{"line": 180, "text": ""}, {"line": 181, "text": "The investigation of ionic conductivity in solid electrolytes at room temperature reported in the last two years is shown as the histogram in Figure 6 (The highest/lowest ionic conductivity of the various structural families counted here does not represent the limits they can achieve, but only based on the studies reported during this period). We have made a simple classification of the structural family, including Garnet type represented by Li7La3Zr2O12,[57] NASICON-like represented by Li1+xAlxTi2-x(PO4)3 (LATP),[58] Lithium-Sulfide represented by Li3PS4 (LISICON-like) and Li2S-P2S5 glass ceramics,[59] Perovskite represented by Li3xLa2/3−x□1/3−xTiO3 (LLTO),[60] and various composite polymer electrolytes (CPEs).[61] For the Na+ solid-state electrolytes, the ionic conductivity of sodium sulfide[59i,62] and various structures (NASICON type, borohydride, etc.)[61c,63] are mainly counted. The K+ solid-state electrolytes are no longer listed here due to the lack of reports. In the Garnet type structure, the ionic conductivity can be largely regulated by cation doping, the highly self-textured garnet-type lithium-ion conducting solid electrolyte of LLZO-Ga exhibits an extremely high ionic conductivity (2.06 × 10-3 S cm-1 at room temperature).[57c] Similarly, in NASICON-like structure, Li1+xTi2-xScx(PO4)3 doped with Sc exhibits an ion conductivity of up to 2.5 × 10-3 S cm-1 (X = 0.2),[58a] This is comparable to or even better than the LATP[58d,58e] reported in the literature. In Lithium"}, {"line": 182, "text": ""}, {"line": 183, "text": "Sulfides, Li10GeP2S12 with LISICON-like structure has the highest ionic conductivity reported in this kind of materials (more than 10- 2 S cm-1 at room temperature[59e]). The ionic conductivity can be improved by adjusting the ratio of the two phases in the Li2S-P2S5 glass ceramic, the highest ionic conductivity reaches 4.8×10-3 S cm-1 after the doping of MoS2.[59i] The ionic conductivity of the Perovskite structure itself is only about 10-4 S cm-1,[60b,60d] but it could be greatly improved after compounding with other materials.[60a,60c] Studies on solid polymer electrolytes have focused on complexing with metal oxide nanoparticles, LLZO, and various polymers.[61] In the study of Na+ solid-state electrolytes, the equivalent substitution of Na3PS4 showed a significant increase in ionic conductivity,[59i,62a,62b] up to 3.7 × 10-3 S cm-1.[59i] However, the sodium sulfide Na10GeP2S12 with similar structure of Li10GeP2S12 does not show commensurately high ionic conductivity of Na+.[62e] The Na1+nZr2SinP3-nO12 possessing a NASICON type structure achieves ionic conductivity of up to 3.4 × 10-3 S cm-1, and even reaches 1.4 × 10-2 S cm-1 at 80 °C. The ionic conductivities of different structural families are strongly dependent on the internal crystal structure and conduction machanisim, which will be discussed in section 3.3.1. The latest computational studies will also be statistically discussed in the following section."}, {"line": 184, "text": ""}, {"line": 185, "text": "![](images/4deda5f876ad8fda3f7bcc755d6fda872eb303bb4394603f3de66bf489e769e6.jpg)  "}, {"line": 186, "text": "Figure 6. Investigation of recently (2017-2018) reported ionic conductivity in solid-state electrolytes at room temperature, including Garnet,[57] NASICON-like,[58] LiSulfide,[59] Perovskite,[60] Composite polymer electrolyte CPE,[61] Na-Sulfide,[59i,62] Others.[61c,63]"}], "document_id": "Recent research on strategies to improve ion conduction in alkali metal‐ion batteries -", "line": 183, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> research on strategies to improve ion conduction in alkali metal‐ion batteries -.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1086, "start": 643}], "text": "Sulfides, Li10GeP2S12 with LISICON-like structure has the highest ionic conductivity reported in this kind of materials (more than 10- 2 S cm-1 at room temperature[59e]). The ionic conductivity can be improved by adjusting the ratio of the two phases in the Li2S-P2S5 glass ceramic, the highest ionic conductivity reaches 4.8×10-3 S cm-1 after the doping of MoS2.[59i] The ionic conductivity of the Perovskite structure itself is only about 10-4 S cm-1,[60b,60d] but it could be greatly improved after compounding with other materials.[60a,60c] Studies on solid polymer electrolytes have focused on complexing with metal oxide nanoparticles, LLZO, and various polymers.[61] In the study of Na+ solid-state electrolytes, the equivalent substitution of Na3PS4 showed a significant increase in ionic conductivity,[59i,62a,62b] up to 3.7 × 10-3 S cm-1.[59i] However, the sodium sulfide Na10GeP2S12 with similar structure of Li10GeP2S12 does not show commensurately high ionic conductivity of Na+.[62e] The Na1+nZr2SinP3-nO12 possessing a NASICON type structure achieves ionic conductivity of up to 3.4 × 10-3 S cm-1, and even reaches 1.4 × 10-2 S cm-1 at 80 °C. The ionic conductivities of different structural families are strongly dependent on the internal crystal structure and conduction machanisim, which will be discussed in section 3.3.1. The latest computational studies will also be statistically discussed in the following section."}, {"context": [{"line": 221, "text": ""}, {"line": 222, "text": "Diffusion of an alkali metal ion in the crystalline material is strongly dependent upon the interaction potential between the alkali metal ions and the host material structure. Figure 10 shows a lot of representative crystal structures.[98] According to the diffusion path of ions in the crystal, various structure types can be classified, such as materials with 1D channel (e.g. LiFePO4, Figure 10a), 2D layer structure(e.g. LiCoO2 and graphite, Figure 10b,e), and 3D diffusion channel (e.g. LiMn2O4, Figure 10c). However, such classification is not entirely applicable. The research on polymorphic LiFeSO4F (tavorite and triplite)[99] shows that, although the calculated migration energy proves that both isomorphs are Li+ conductors, the Li+ in triplite type LiFeSO4F move in a restricted migration path (one or two dimensions). while the tavorite type has continuous three-dimensional conductive network for Li+. Similarly, Li10GeP2S12 can be classified into a 3D conductor or a pseudo 1D conductor."}, {"line": 223, "text": ""}, {"line": 224, "text": "The conduction path and mechanism of ions in inorganic solid-state electrolytes (Figure 10f-j) is more complicated. The Garnet type (Figure 10f) is derived from the ideal garnet structure with the general formula A3B2(XO4)3, Li+ ions usually occupy the tetrahedral positions of X-site which is 4-fold coordinated. Meier et al.[79] identified the mechanisms (Figure 11a) responsible for the considerable difference in ionic conductivity between the tetragonal and the cubic phases of LLZO (Li7La3Zr2O12). In tetragonal LLZO, the motion of Li ions is of fully collective nature or synchronous, which requires a higher activation energy. However, in cubic LLZO, the asynchronous collective motion of Li ions caused by single ion hopping results in lower activation energy due to the presence of unoccupied Li sites. Substitution of Zr[100] with Ta, Sb, etc. can improve the distribution of Li ions in the crystal structure of LLZO and improve the ionic conductivity. Recent reports[101] indicate that when stabilizing the garnetstructured LLZO with Ga3+, it shows superior properties compared to LLZO stabilized with supervalent cations (such as Al3+). In the NASICON-like LiTi2(PO4)3 (Figure 10g) framework, Li ions occupy two sites of M1 and M2 (Figure 11b),[102] and lithium migration occurs through the jump between these two sites (Figure 11c). The partial occupancy of Li+ ions at these two sites is critical for fast Li+ ion conduction. The Li+ ion conduction activation energy decreases linearly with the bottleneck size between the M1 and M2 sites.[103] Li ions are introduced for charge compensation by substitution of a trivalent cation such as Al3+, but these additional Li ions are trapped near the trivalent ions, resulting in a limited increase in ion conductivity. The LISICON-like Li10GeP2S12 (LGPS) (Figure 10h) has the highest Li+ ion conductivity[104] reported so far because it forms a one-dimensional channel with very high Li mobility. The conduction path of Li+ ions in the b direction and the c direction is shown in Figure 11d,[105] and the recent theoretical calculation[106] shows that it is also possible for Li+ to diffuse in the ab plane. In the latest study, Paulus et al.[105] used spin-aligned echo (SAE) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) techniques to directly detect the slow Li+ ion transitions with correlation times as low as about 10-5 s, which provides a new way to understand the mobility of Li+ in three dimensions. By introducing Li ions into the perovskite structure by heterovalent ion doping, a composition of Li3xLa2/3−x□1/3−xTiO3 (Figure 10i) can be obtained. The introduction of Li+ ions changes the concentration of lithium and vacancies, and its interaction[107] can significantly affect ionic conductivity. LLTO has a high volume ionic conductivity, but due to the high electrical resistance at the grain boundaries, its total conductivity tends to be two orders of magnitude lower than the volume ion conductivity."}, {"line": 225, "text": ""}, {"line": 226, "text": "![](images/55abbcdd568690c0eee5c94959c48945d9f41e1935a348db9ee958612f974045.jpg)  "}, {"line": 227, "text": "Figure 10. Some typical crystal structures of battery materials: (a) Olivine LiFePO4, (b) Layered LiCoO2, (c) Spinel LiMn2O4, (d) Tavorite LiFeSO4F, (e) Graphite, (f) Garnet Li7La3Zr2O12, (g) NASICON-like Na3Zr2Si2PO12, (h) LISICON-like Li10GeP2S12, (i) Perovskite Li3xLa2/3−x□1/3−xTiO3 and(j) Antiperovskite Li3OCl. [98]"}], "document_id": "Recent research on strategies to improve ion conduction in alkali metal‐ion batteries -", "line": 224, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> research on strategies to improve ion conduction in alkali metal‐ion batteries -.md", "submatches": [{"end": 2894, "start": 483}], "text": "The conduction path and mechanism of ions in inorganic solid-state electrolytes (Figure 10f-j) is more complicated. The Garnet type (Figure 10f) is derived from the ideal garnet structure with the general formula A3B2(XO4)3, Li+ ions usually occupy the tetrahedral positions of X-site which is 4-fold coordinated. Meier et al.[79] identified the mechanisms (Figure 11a) responsible for the considerable difference in ionic conductivity between the tetragonal and the cubic phases of LLZO (Li7La3Zr2O12). In tetragonal LLZO, the motion of Li ions is of fully collective nature or synchronous, which requires a higher activation energy. However, in cubic LLZO, the asynchronous collective motion of Li ions caused by single ion hopping results in lower activation energy due to the presence of unoccupied Li sites. Substitution of Zr[100] with Ta, Sb, etc. can improve the distribution of Li ions in the crystal structure of LLZO and improve the ionic conductivity. Recent reports[101] indicate that when stabilizing the garnetstructured LLZO with Ga3+, it shows superior properties compared to LLZO stabilized with supervalent cations (such as Al3+). In the NASICON-like LiTi2(PO4)3 (Figure 10g) framework, Li ions occupy two sites of M1 and M2 (Figure 11b),[102] and lithium migration occurs through the jump between these two sites (Figure 11c). The partial occupancy of Li+ ions at these two sites is critical for fast Li+ ion conduction. The Li+ ion conduction activation energy decreases linearly with the bottleneck size between the M1 and M2 sites.[103] Li ions are introduced for charge compensation by substitution of a trivalent cation such as Al3+, but these additional Li ions are trapped near the trivalent ions, resulting in a limited increase in ion conductivity. The LISICON-like Li10GeP2S12 (LGPS) (Figure 10h) has the highest Li+ ion conductivity[104] reported so far because it forms a one-dimensional channel with very high Li mobility. The conduction path of Li+ ions in the b direction and the c direction is shown in Figure 11d,[105] and the recent theoretical calculation[106] shows that it is also possible for Li+ to diffuse in the ab plane. In the latest study, Paulus et al.[105] used spin-aligned echo (SAE) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) techniques to directly detect the slow Li+ ion transitions with correlation times as low as about 10-5 s, which provides a new way to understand the mobility of Li+ in three dimensions. By introducing Li ions into the perovskite structure by heterovalent ion doping, a composition of Li3xLa2/3−x□1/3−xTiO3 (Figure 10i) can be obtained. The introduction of Li+ ions changes the concentration of lithium and vacancies, and its interaction[107] can significantly affect ionic conductivity. LLTO has a high volume ionic conductivity, but due to the high electrical resistance at the grain boundaries, its total conductivity tends to be two orders of magnitude lower than the volume ion conductivity."}, {"context": [{"line": 245, "text": "![](images/7d404a0ea5d9c6655fbdaed446416019bae8e8b8cb92a3bdfa825c8efde1efee.jpg)  "}, {"line": 246, "text": "Figure 13. (a) Nyquist plots of the Na3V2-xMgx(PO4)3/C (x = 0, 0.01, 0.03, 0.05, 0.07 and 0.1; inset: the equivalent circuit used to fit the EIS). (b) The relationship between Zre and ω-1/2 at low frequencies. (c) Sodium ion diffusion coefficients in Na3V2-xMgx(PO4)3/C (x = 0, 0.01, 0.03, 0.05, 0.07, and 0.1). (d) Electronic conductivity of Na3V2-xMgx(PO4)3/C (x = 0, 0.01, 0.03, 0.05, 0.07, and 0.1), which was taken using a RTS-4 linear four-point probe measurement system.[41e] Reprinted (adapted) with permission from ref. 41e. Copyright (2015) Royal Society of Chemistry."}, {"line": 247, "text": ""}, {"line": 248, "text": "Since different ions have different ionic radii, the introduction of other elements usually produces a change in lattice parameters[116] or phase transitions[101]. Figure 14a[117] shows the doping concentration dependence of the unit cell volume of Li3K3Ce2(BH4)12 and Li3K3La2(BH4)12 for different divalent cation (Ca2+, Sr2+, Eu2+) substitutions. For Sr2+, Eu2+ cations, the unit cell volume increases significantly as the doping concentration increases, and as a result, the Li+ ionic conductivities of the samples doped with divalent Sr2+ and Eu2+ are increased by an order of magnitude (Figure 14b/c) over the entire temperature range. So how does the change in lattice volume caused by doping of different elements affect the ionic conductivity? The key is that the increase in lattice volume can effectively reduce the activation energy within a certain range. Bachman et al.[25] made detailed statistics on change of activation energy and ionic conductivity changes caused by lattice volume. The study by Lang et al.[102] also proves that the substitution of Ti atoms in LTP by various trivalent, tetravalent and pentavalent cations X (LXTP) leads to structural changes, thus affecting Li mobility. The minimum energy path (MEP) of the Li jump between adjacent M1 sites is obtained by the nudged elastic band (NEB) method (as shown in Figure 14d), and the calculated activation energy of the migration vacancy is between 0.29 eV and 0.75 eV, which is related to the size of the LiO6 octahedron in the structure. Figure 14e/f further shows that the structures with a vacant Li position inside a larger polyhedron are more stable, this is because O atoms bind the Li+ ion more tightly in smaller polyhedral cages than in larger ones due to the 1/r dependence of the electrostatic Coulomb interaction. Shin et al.[118] demonstrated that the multidoping of additional Ta dopants in Al-doped LLZO shifts the most energetic favorable position of Al in the crystal structure from 24 d to 96 h Li sites, thereby it provides more open space for Li ion transportation. This synergistic effect makes the ion conductivity (6.14 × 10-4 S cm-1) of multi-doped LLZO approximately three times higher than that of single-doped LLZO. The substitution of elements can affect the lattice volume, while the direct doping of compounds can also significantly modify the ion conduction behavior. For example, Subbu et al.[119] observed the improvement of ionic conductivity in polymer electrolytes prepared by complexation of lithium salts with polyethylene oxide (PEO) and polyvinylidene chloride-co-acrylonitrile (PVdC-co-AN), and among the various lithium salts studied, the lithium trifluoromethanesulfonate [LiN(CF3SO2)2]-based electrolyte exhibits the highest ionic conductivity at room temperature of"}, {"line": 249, "text": ""}, {"line": 250, "text": "0.265 × 10-5 S cm-1. Iranipour et al.[120] recently reported studies on the equilibrium phase behavior and the effect of lithium salt (lithium tetrafluoroborate) on the organic ionic plastic crystal (OIPC), N-ethyl-N-methylpyrrolidinium tetrafluoroborate [C2mpyr] [BF4]. In addition to the equilibrium phase transformations of the pure OIPC, new transitions are observed after the addition of the lithium salt (10 mol% and 20 mol% LiBF4), which significantly alters the ion conduction behavior of the material. The doping of these polymers usually has an important relationship with their concentration, but due to the lack of systematic theoretical research on the conduction in polymers, further exploration of its influencing factors is needed."}, {"line": 251, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Recent research on strategies to improve ion conduction in alkali metal‐ion batteries -", "line": 248, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> research on strategies to improve ion conduction in alkali metal‐ion batteries -.md", "submatches": [{"end": 2769, "start": 1895}], "text": "Since different ions have different ionic radii, the introduction of other elements usually produces a change in lattice parameters[116] or phase transitions[101]. Figure 14a[117] shows the doping concentration dependence of the unit cell volume of Li3K3Ce2(BH4)12 and Li3K3La2(BH4)12 for different divalent cation (Ca2+, Sr2+, Eu2+) substitutions. For Sr2+, Eu2+ cations, the unit cell volume increases significantly as the doping concentration increases, and as a result, the Li+ ionic conductivities of the samples doped with divalent Sr2+ and Eu2+ are increased by an order of magnitude (Figure 14b/c) over the entire temperature range. So how does the change in lattice volume caused by doping of different elements affect the ionic conductivity? The key is that the increase in lattice volume can effectively reduce the activation energy within a certain range. Bachman et al.[25] made detailed statistics on change of activation energy and ionic conductivity changes caused by lattice volume. The study by Lang et al.[102] also proves that the substitution of Ti atoms in LTP by various trivalent, tetravalent and pentavalent cations X (LXTP) leads to structural changes, thus affecting Li mobility. The minimum energy path (MEP) of the Li jump between adjacent M1 sites is obtained by the nudged elastic band (NEB) method (as shown in Figure 14d), and the calculated activation energy of the migration vacancy is between 0.29 eV and 0.75 eV, which is related to the size of the LiO6 octahedron in the structure. Figure 14e/f further shows that the structures with a vacant Li position inside a larger polyhedron are more stable, this is because O atoms bind the Li+ ion more tightly in smaller polyhedral cages than in larger ones due to the 1/r dependence of the electrostatic Coulomb interaction. Shin et al.[118] demonstrated that the multidoping of additional Ta dopants in Al-doped LLZO shifts the most energetic favorable position of Al in the crystal structure from 24 d to 96 h Li sites, thereby it provides more open space for Li ion transportation. This synergistic effect makes the ion conductivity (6.14 × 10-4 S cm-1) of multi-doped LLZO approximately three times higher than that of single-doped LLZO. The substitution of elements can affect the lattice volume, while the direct doping of compounds can also significantly modify the ion conduction behavior. For example, Subbu et al.[119] observed the improvement of ionic conductivity in polymer electrolytes prepared by complexation of lithium salts with polyethylene oxide (PEO) and polyvinylidene chloride-co-acrylonitrile (PVdC-co-AN), and among the various lithium salts studied, the lithium trifluoromethanesulfonate [LiN(CF3SO2)2]-based electrolyte exhibits the highest ionic conductivity at room temperature of"}, {"context": [{"line": 14, "text": ""}, {"line": 15, "text": "Recently, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been widely used as power sources for portable electronic devices, such as mobile phones, laptops and for electric vehicles owing to their high energy and power density [1–3]. However, common LIBs are based on flammable organic liquid electrolytes, which carry the inherent risks of leakage, fire, and explosion. For this reason, solid-state electrolytes with fast Li-ion diffusion were recognized as promising alternatives [4,5]. Thus, extensive efforts have been made to develop lithium-ion conductive inorganic materials, including NASICON, perovskite, and sulfide glasses, for use as solid electrolytes, an important component of all-solid lithium-ion batteries [6–10]."}, {"line": 16, "text": ""}, {"line": 17, "text": "In general, the solid electrolytes that have been studied extensively are mainly composed of sulfides and oxides; Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is probably one of the most promising candidates to date for use in all-solid-state Liion batteries on account of its excellent chemical and electrochemical stabilities while exhibiting high Li-ion conductivity compared to those of other garnet structure conductors such as Li5La3M2O12 (M = Nb, Ta) [5,11– 13]. LLZO is a garnet-type material that has two stable phases: tetragonal and cubic. Although the tetragonal phase can be obtained at lower synthesis temperatures than the cubic phase, the cubic phase has a bulk conductivity of ∼ 10−3 S/cm compared to ∼ 10−6 S/cm for tetragonal LLZO [14]. For high ionic conductivity, the LLZO solid electrolytes should maintain a stable cubic structure, which usually require several calcination steps and long sintering times at temperatures greater than 1000 - 1200 ◦C [15–17]. However, during this hightemperature thermal process, significant Li loss unavoidably occurs, which often results in a structural collapse of LLZO accompanied by the formation of second phases, thereby reducing the ionic conductivity [18]. Doping with external elements is a well-known method to stabilize the cubic phase at lower temperatures compared to the conventional 1000 - 1200 ◦C range and increase Li+ conductivity by introducing additional Li-ion vacancies, which balance the ionic charge [19–24]. However, doping has the disadvantage of being difficult to control; therefore, finding other ways to stabilize the cubic phase at room temperature is of at most importance."}, {"line": 18, "text": ""}, {"line": 19, "text": "In this research, cubic LLZO was synthesized through a solution-based synthesis method without any extrinsic dopants at temperatures lower than 1000 ◦C. Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) was used as the chelating agent, and the cubic phase was confirmed by using XRay diffraction. Finally, AC impedance measurements were conducted to evaluate the electrochemical performance of the synthesized material."}, {"line": 20, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 17, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1203, "start": 128}], "text": "In general, the solid electrolytes that have been studied extensively are mainly composed of sulfides and oxides; Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is probably one of the most promising candidates to date for use in all-solid-state Liion batteries on account of its excellent chemical and electrochemical stabilities while exhibiting high Li-ion conductivity compared to those of other garnet structure conductors such as Li5La3M2O12 (M = Nb, Ta) [5,11– 13]. LLZO is a garnet-type material that has two stable phases: tetragonal and cubic. Although the tetragonal phase can be obtained at lower synthesis temperatures than the cubic phase, the cubic phase has a bulk conductivity of ∼ 10−3 S/cm compared to ∼ 10−6 S/cm for tetragonal LLZO [14]. For high ionic conductivity, the LLZO solid electrolytes should maintain a stable cubic structure, which usually require several calcination steps and long sintering times at temperatures greater than 1000 - 1200 ◦C [15–17]. However, during this hightemperature thermal process, significant Li loss unavoidably occurs, which often results in a structural collapse of LLZO accompanied by the formation of second phases, thereby reducing the ionic conductivity [18]. Doping with external elements is a well-known method to stabilize the cubic phase at lower temperatures compared to the conventional 1000 - 1200 ◦C range and increase Li+ conductivity by introducing additional Li-ion vacancies, which balance the ionic charge [19–24]. However, doping has the disadvantage of being difficult to control; therefore, finding other ways to stabilize the cubic phase at room temperature is of at most importance."}, {"context": [{"line": 34, "text": ""}, {"line": 35, "text": "The microstructure of the Li7La3Zr2O12 powder and the cross-section of the pellet observed using SEM and AFM are shown in Fig. 3. As one can observe in the images, the particles are uneven, and the pellet has large particles and larger voids. The voids were revealed in the SEM image of the pellet (Fig. 3(c)), and the height deviation is large due to the voids seen in the AFM image (Fig. 3(d)). These voids inside the pellet reduce the density of the material and increase its resistance, thereby reducing its ionic conductivity [17,20]."}, {"line": 36, "text": ""}, {"line": 37, "text": "Figure 4(a) shows the Nyquist plots obtained from AC impedance spectroscopy analyses of the LLZO pellets. The ratios of the bulk and the grain boundary resistances to the total resistance were determined by fitting the experimental data to the equivalent circuit shown in Fig. 4(b). The ionic conductivity of the sample was calculated as follows: σ = (ALR), where L is the length of the pellet, A is the surface area of the pellet, and R is the Ohmic resistance determined from the AC impedance. The bulk, grain boundary and total ionic conductivities determined in this fashion are 2.91 10−5 S/cm, 1.13 10−6 S/cm and 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, respectively. The equivalent circuit was of the form RB/CB+RGB/CGB+Wo, where RB is the bulk resistance, RGB is the grain boundary resistance, and Wo is the Warburg element [5,25]. The calculated total Li+ conductivity of LLZO is 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, which is higher than that reported by Kokal et al. [26] (3.12 10−7 S/cm), but is lower than the value for other cubic materials [15, 17]. This is a result of the impurity phase and the many voids observed from the XRD results and the SEM images."}, {"line": 38, "text": ""}, {"line": 39, "text": "![](images/86a714b04b155447369b6752be40989e7ca351d5e8c553944d4383956044baca.jpg)  "}, {"line": 40, "text": "Fig. 3. (Color online) Scanning electron microscopy images of (a) Li7La3Zr2O12 powder and (c) a cross-section of a Li7La3Zr2O12 pellet; (b) and (d) the corresponding atomic force microscopy images."}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 37, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 305, "start": 92}], "text": "Figure 4(a) shows the Nyquist plots obtained from AC impedance spectroscopy analyses of the LLZO pellets. The ratios of the bulk and the grain boundary resistances to the total resistance were determined by fitting the experimental data to the equivalent circuit shown in Fig. 4(b). The ionic conductivity of the sample was calculated as follows: σ = (ALR), where L is the length of the pellet, A is the surface area of the pellet, and R is the Ohmic resistance determined from the AC impedance. The bulk, grain boundary and total ionic conductivities determined in this fashion are 2.91 10−5 S/cm, 1.13 10−6 S/cm and 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, respectively. The equivalent circuit was of the form RB/CB+RGB/CGB+Wo, where RB is the bulk resistance, RGB is the grain boundary resistance, and Wo is the Warburg element [5,25]. The calculated total Li+ conductivity of LLZO is 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, which is higher than that reported by Kokal et al. [26] (3.12 10−7 S/cm), but is lower than the value for other cubic materials [15, 17]. This is a result of the impurity phase and the many voids observed from the XRD results and the SEM images."}, {"context": [{"line": 42, "text": "![](images/fc3f22f79a7179d472a82d8dcdd088876ed7de3dec807618430257673c8e6f17.jpg)  "}, {"line": 43, "text": "Fig. 4. (Color online) (a) Nyquist plots of Li7La3Zr2O12 measured at 25 ◦C, (b) the equivalent circuit model used to fit the impedance data and (c) Arrhenius plot for the total conductivity of Li7La3Zr2O12."}, {"line": 44, "text": ""}, {"line": 45, "text": "Figure 4(c) shows the temperature dependence of the total ionic conductivity (Arrhenius plot) of the LLZO pellets at temperatures of 25 - 70 ◦C, where the Arrhenius equation is given by σ = TA exp \u0002 k−BETa \u0003, where σ is the ionic conductivity, T is the absolute temperature, A is the pre-exponential constant, kB is the Boltzmann constant and Ea is the activation energy for the ionic conductivity. The activation energy determined from the slope of the log (σT ) versus 1/T plot for the total conductivity of the sample is 0.53 eV. The activation energy for the LLZO material is lower (0.53 eV) than that for the reference LLZO material (0.67 eV) [26]. The lower activation energy is due to a transition from the tetragonal to the cubic phase, and the lower activation energy can improve the conductivity by facilitating the movement of lithium ions [27]."}, {"line": 46, "text": ""}, {"line": 47, "text": "# III. CONCLUSION"}, {"line": 48, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 45, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 513, "start": 101}], "text": "Figure 4(c) shows the temperature dependence of the total ionic conductivity (Arrhenius plot) of the LLZO pellets at temperatures of 25 - 70 ◦C, where the Arrhenius equation is given by σ = TA exp \u0002 k−BETa \u0003, where σ is the ionic conductivity, T is the absolute temperature, A is the pre-exponential constant, kB is the Boltzmann constant and Ea is the activation energy for the ionic conductivity. The activation energy determined from the slope of the log (σT ) versus 1/T plot for the total conductivity of the sample is 0.53 eV. The activation energy for the LLZO material is lower (0.53 eV) than that for the reference LLZO material (0.67 eV) [26]. The lower activation energy is due to a transition from the tetragonal to the cubic phase, and the lower activation energy can improve the conductivity by facilitating the movement of lithium ions [27]."}, {"context": [{"line": 46, "text": ""}, {"line": 47, "text": "# III. CONCLUSION"}, {"line": 48, "text": ""}, {"line": 49, "text": "An LLZO electrolyte was synthesized by using a solution-based method. The sample formed a Li+ conducting cubic phase without any extrinsic dopants at 900 ◦C. Remarkably, cubic materials were synthesized at 900 ◦C, which is lower than the conventional synthesis temperatures of 1000 - 1200 ◦C range, by using a solution-based method. The synthesized LLZO pellet revealed relatively non-uniform particles as a result of impurities and reduced density. As a result, the total ionic conductivity of LLZO was found to be 1.09 10−6 S/cm."}, {"line": 50, "text": ""}, {"line": 51, "text": "# ACKNOWLEDGMENTS"}, {"line": 52, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 49, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 497, "start": 3}], "text": "An LLZO electrolyte was synthesized by using a solution-based method. The sample formed a Li+ conducting cubic phase without any extrinsic dopants at 900 ◦C. Remarkably, cubic materials were synthesized at 900 ◦C, which is lower than the conventional synthesis temperatures of 1000 - 1200 ◦C range, by using a solution-based method. The synthesized LLZO pellet revealed relatively non-uniform particles as a result of impurities and reduced density. As a result, the total ionic conductivity of LLZO was found to be 1.09 10−6 S/cm."}, {"context": [{"line": 16, "text": ""}, {"line": 17, "text": "This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 18, "text": ""}, {"line": 19, "text": "Garnet-type metal oxide such as Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is considered as one of the most promising solid electrolytes among the inorganic solid electrolytes4, for it shows high Li+ ionic conductivity, high stability with lithium metal and wide electrochemical window5. The sintering process6, doping strategy7-9, microstructure10 and composite design11 have been widely investigated to enhance its performance. The LLZO solid electrolyte can be synthesized by solid state reaction12, sol-gel method13, spark plasma sintering14 and hot-press sintering method15. Solid state reaction at high temperature is preferred because the cost is relatively low and the procedure is simple, which is suitable for large-scale industrial producing. The LLZO electrolyte sample prepared by solid state reaction has a moderate ionic conductivity in the order of 10-4 S/cm. Doping various elements into LLZO can increase the Li-ion conductivity and the Ta doped sample, Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12(x=0.6), has an ionic conductivity of about 1×10-3 S/cm 16, 17. However, because of the high volatility nature of Li element, Li is easy to lose during the high temperature sintering which leads to form the undesirable La2Zr2O7 as an impure phase. In order to prepare the stoichiometric LLZO, the lost Li should be compensated and the most common means is to cover the tablet samples up with the mother powder 18. The mother powder with the same chemistry composition as the samples acts as the sacrificial component to ensure the sample to form the right garnet phase. The amount of the sacrificed mother powder usually reaches the ratio 1:1 in weight to the samples and after sintering, the mother powder cannot be reused. That is the problem, as we know that the mother powder contains the rare earth element La and the element Ta that are expensive. Therefore, the conventional solid state reaction with mother powder protecting method is not economical. We noticed that the effect of the This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 20, "text": ""}, {"line": 21, "text": "mother powder is merely providing another Li source to compensate the lost Li. We proposed an alternative method to compensate the lost Li where the mother powder is replaced by Li2CO3. In this method, Li2CO3 is separated from the sample and it will melt and decompound above 730ºC. The decomposed Li2CO3 provides a Li2O atmosphere and ensures the sample to form the pure garnet phase. The method is also suitable for other oxide solid electrolytes. Li2CO3 is much cheaper than the mother powder and this method will lower the synthesis cost of LLZO as well as other oxide solid electrolytes, which is important for the large-scale application of the solid state batteries in the future."}, {"line": 22, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &", "line": 19, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1002, "start": 46}], "text": "Garnet-type metal oxide such as Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is considered as one of the most promising solid electrolytes among the inorganic solid electrolytes4, for it shows high Li+ ionic conductivity, high stability with lithium metal and wide electrochemical window5. The sintering process6, doping strategy7-9, microstructure10 and composite design11 have been widely investigated to enhance its performance. The LLZO solid electrolyte can be synthesized by solid state reaction12, sol-gel method13, spark plasma sintering14 and hot-press sintering method15. Solid state reaction at high temperature is preferred because the cost is relatively low and the procedure is simple, which is suitable for large-scale industrial producing. The LLZO electrolyte sample prepared by solid state reaction has a moderate ionic conductivity in the order of 10-4 S/cm. Doping various elements into LLZO can increase the Li-ion conductivity and the Ta doped sample, Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12(x=0.6), has an ionic conductivity of about 1×10-3 S/cm 16, 17. However, because of the high volatility nature of Li element, Li is easy to lose during the high temperature sintering which leads to form the undesirable La2Zr2O7 as an impure phase. In order to prepare the stoichiometric LLZO, the lost Li should be compensated and the most common means is to cover the tablet samples up with the mother powder 18. The mother powder with the same chemistry composition as the samples acts as the sacrificial component to ensure the sample to form the right garnet phase. The amount of the sacrificed mother powder usually reaches the ratio 1:1 in weight to the samples and after sintering, the mother powder cannot be reused. That is the problem, as we know that the mother powder contains the rare earth element La and the element Ta that are expensive. Therefore, the conventional solid state reaction with mother powder protecting method is not economical. We noticed that the effect of the This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"context": [{"line": 40, "text": "compensate the loss of lithium before the garnet phase is formed.   "}, {"line": 41, "text": "This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 42, "text": ""}, {"line": 43, "text": "LLZO is usually prepared by solid-state reaction at high temperature above 1100 °C. In the experiment, Al2O3 was added to the preheated powders as a sintering aid that will form a liquid of LiAlO2 and Li5AlO4 eutectic 19. As Li2CO3 is much more excessive, the sintering temperature might be lowered. We prepared the LLZTO samples with 1g Li2CO3 providing the protective atmosphere (L1 sample) at different temperatures. Fig.3 (a) shows the XRD patterns of L1 samples prepared at 1060°C, 1100°C and 1140°C respectively. All these samples have LLZTO as the main phase and there exists LiAlO2 phase. As it can be seen in the patterns, the intensity of LiAlO2 increases as temperature increasing and the samples prepared at 1140°C contain considerable amount of LiAlO2. The evidence can also be seen in the SEM images of these samples as shown in Fig.4. In the SEM images of LLZTO, the dark areas represent the LiAlO2 phase. Fig.4(a) shows the micro-morphology of LLZTO sample prepared at 1060°C, there are some pores that maybe due to the temperature is not high enough to form the necessary eutectic liquid of LiAlO2 and Li5AlO4. As the XRD pattern of 1060°C shows, there is little LiAlO2. The LLZTO samples prepared at 1100°C and 1140°C are nearly free of pores. It is obvious that samples of 1140°C contain much more LiAlO2 phase than that of 1100°C, which is in accord with the result of XRD. Because LiAlO2 can block the lithium ions transfer and thus decreases the ionic conductivity, the samples with excessive LiAlO2 may not be suitable for solid electrolyte. For these reasons, we select 1100°C as the sintering temperature to synthesize LLZTO."}, {"line": 44, "text": ""}, {"line": 45, "text": "At 1100°C, the LLZTO tablet samples were prepared with different amount of Li2CO3 added to provide protective atmosphere. Fig.3(b) shows the XRD patterns of L0, L0.5, L1 and L1.5 samples, and the diffraction peaks of Li5LaNb2O12 (ICSD PDF #45-0109) which has This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 46, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &", "line": 43, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1497, "start": 0}], "text": "LLZO is usually prepared by solid-state reaction at high temperature above 1100 °C. In the experiment, Al2O3 was added to the preheated powders as a sintering aid that will form a liquid of LiAlO2 and Li5AlO4 eutectic 19. As Li2CO3 is much more excessive, the sintering temperature might be lowered. We prepared the LLZTO samples with 1g Li2CO3 providing the protective atmosphere (L1 sample) at different temperatures. Fig.3 (a) shows the XRD patterns of L1 samples prepared at 1060°C, 1100°C and 1140°C respectively. All these samples have LLZTO as the main phase and there exists LiAlO2 phase. As it can be seen in the patterns, the intensity of LiAlO2 increases as temperature increasing and the samples prepared at 1140°C contain considerable amount of LiAlO2. The evidence can also be seen in the SEM images of these samples as shown in Fig.4. In the SEM images of LLZTO, the dark areas represent the LiAlO2 phase. Fig.4(a) shows the micro-morphology of LLZTO sample prepared at 1060°C, there are some pores that maybe due to the temperature is not high enough to form the necessary eutectic liquid of LiAlO2 and Li5AlO4. As the XRD pattern of 1060°C shows, there is little LiAlO2. The LLZTO samples prepared at 1100°C and 1140°C are nearly free of pores. It is obvious that samples of 1140°C contain much more LiAlO2 phase than that of 1100°C, which is in accord with the result of XRD. Because LiAlO2 can block the lithium ions transfer and thus decreases the ionic conductivity, the samples with excessive LiAlO2 may not be suitable for solid electrolyte. For these reasons, we select 1100°C as the sintering temperature to synthesize LLZTO."}, {"context": [{"line": 5, "text": "1Materials Research and Education Center, Auburn University, Auburn, Alabama 36849, USA   "}, {"line": 6, "text": "2Department of Chemical Engineering, Auburn University, Auburn, Alabama 36849, USA"}, {"line": 7, "text": ""}, {"line": 8, "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}, {"line": 9, "text": ""}, {"line": 10, "text": "© The Author(s) 2019. Published by ECS. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License (CC BY, http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted reuse of the work in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. [DOI: 10.1149/2.1031915jes]"}, {"line": 11, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 8, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 722, "start": 26}], "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}, {"context": [{"line": 11, "text": ""}, {"line": 12, "text": "Manuscript submitted September 6, 2019; revised manuscript received October 28, 2019. Published November 13, 2019."}, {"line": 13, "text": ""}, {"line": 14, "text": "Using a solid-state electrolyte instead of the commonly used organic liquid electrolyte to produce an all-solid-state lithium-ion battery is regarded as one promising way to overcome safety issues, such as flammability and leakage of electrolyte.1–4 A large number of solid-state electrolytes have been reported, such as solid polymer electrolytes using different Li salts of LiClO4, LiBF4, or LiN(CF3SO2)2, 5,6 and ceramic electrolytes including perovskite-type titanates (LLTO),7,8 NASICON-type (sodium super ionic conductor) oxide electrolytes,9,10 sulfide-type and garnet-type materials.11 The cubic garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention due to its high chemical stability and high ionic conductivity (8.7 × 10−4 S/cm at 25°C).12–15"}, {"line": 15, "text": ""}, {"line": 16, "text": "Besides the cubic structure, garnet-type LLZO can also exist with a tetragonal crystal structure with a Li+ conductivity of around 2 10−6 S/cm,16,17 which is two orders of magnitude lower than the conductivity of the cubic phase structure. In the cubic crystal structure, Li ions occupy two types of crystallographic sites. A tetrahedral site, Li(1), and a distorted octahedral site, Li(2).16 In the tetragonal phase structure, Li atoms occupy three types of crystallographic sites: a tetrahedral site, Li(1), and two distorted octahedral sites, Li(2) and Li(3).17 The lower conductivity of the tetragonal crystal structure has been attributed to the ordering of Li atoms and Li vacancies in the tetrahedral and octahedral sites, as the tetrahedral or distorted octahedral sites are either full or empty.18 In the cubic phase, however, the Li sub-lattice is disordered, so both types of Li sites are partially occupied and the Li ion vacancies are also not ordered.18–20 In the tetragonal phase, all Li sites are fully occupied (g  1), while a reduced site occupancy of Li(1) site (g  0.94) and Li(2) site (g  0.35) were found in the cubic phase.17,21 Thus, compared to the tetragonal phase, in the cubic phase the larger number of vacancies and the disorder in the Li sub-lattice enable a lower activation energy for Li motion, which enhance the Li-ion conductivity in the cubic phase.22"}, {"line": 17, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 14, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 729, "start": 632}], "text": "Using a solid-state electrolyte instead of the commonly used organic liquid electrolyte to produce an all-solid-state lithium-ion battery is regarded as one promising way to overcome safety issues, such as flammability and leakage of electrolyte.1–4 A large number of solid-state electrolytes have been reported, such as solid polymer electrolytes using different Li salts of LiClO4, LiBF4, or LiN(CF3SO2)2, 5,6 and ceramic electrolytes including perovskite-type titanates (LLTO),7,8 NASICON-type (sodium super ionic conductor) oxide electrolytes,9,10 sulfide-type and garnet-type materials.11 The cubic garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention due to its high chemical stability and high ionic conductivity (8.7 × 10−4 S/cm at 25°C).12–15"}, {"context": [{"line": 17, "text": ""}, {"line": 18, "text": "Doping elements are usually used to stabilize the cubic phase from the tetragonal phase, which is performed by substituting cations for Li, La or Zr.18,23 Substitution of Al, Ga, Fe for Li,24–28 Rb, Nd, Ca, Ba for La,29–31 and/or Nb, Ta, Y for Zr30,32–35 have been reported. Aluminum contamination from the crucible has been found to be helpful in stabilizing the cubic phase.25 The reaction between Al2O3 from the crucible and LiOH or Li2CO3 has resulted in the production of an Al-containing phase, which plays two roles. A small amount of Al in the garnet phase could stabilize the cubic structure relative to the tetragonal phase.25 Secondly, a liquid phase introduced by the reaction between Li2O and Al2O3 could facilitate the sintering.27 According to the Li2O-Al2O3 phase diagram, a eutectic liquid forms at 23 mol% Al2O3, which has a melting point of 1055°C.36 This Al-containing phase has also been reported in other studies, and is typically located between grains.13,27,37"}, {"line": 19, "text": ""}, {"line": 20, "text": "The phase stability and the conductivity are affected by the Al incorporation, as the substitution mechanism of one Al atom for three Li atoms leads to a reduction of the Li content and an increase in the Li vacancies.25 Although the stabilization of cubic phase by Al doping could help to obtain high conductivity,12,16,17 an undesired Li5AlO4 phase forming at higher doping levels, has lower Li+ conductivity than LLZO.38,39 In addition, it has been reported that the substitutional Al ions sit in the conductive pathway of Li, lead to lower conductivity.25,40 The substitutional Ta ions occupy Li sites, which will not hinder the Li-ion motion.12 The addition of Al into Ta-doped LLZO has been shown to lead to a decrease in the ionic conductivity from 8.7 × 10−4 S/cm to 3.7 × 10−4 S/cm.12 The conductivity of Al-doped LLZO has been reported as 4 10−4 S/cm, while Ta-doped LLZO could reach 8.7 × 10−4 S/cm.12,41 In addition to the higher ionic conductivity, Ta-doped LLZO was also found to be stable with lithium metal electrode.34,40 While in Al-doped LLZO, intergranular Li metal was observed to propagate through the grain boundaries.42 Therefore, Ta was chosen as the doping element in this work."}, {"line": 21, "text": ""}, {"line": 22, "text": "A dense microstructure is also needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities (e.g. 8.9 × 10−6 S/cm at 25°C).43 Hot-pressing can be used to obtain an almost dense microstructure.41,44,45 Other methods like cold isostatic pressing,46–48 field assisted sintering technology,49 flowing oxygen sintering,50 sintering with microwave hybrid furnace,37 have also been reported to be effective methods to increase the density."}, {"line": 23, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 20, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 971, "start": 416}], "text": "The phase stability and the conductivity are affected by the Al incorporation, as the substitution mechanism of one Al atom for three Li atoms leads to a reduction of the Li content and an increase in the Li vacancies.25 Although the stabilization of cubic phase by Al doping could help to obtain high conductivity,12,16,17 an undesired Li5AlO4 phase forming at higher doping levels, has lower Li+ conductivity than LLZO.38,39 In addition, it has been reported that the substitutional Al ions sit in the conductive pathway of Li, lead to lower conductivity.25,40 The substitutional Ta ions occupy Li sites, which will not hinder the Li-ion motion.12 The addition of Al into Ta-doped LLZO has been shown to lead to a decrease in the ionic conductivity from 8.7 × 10−4 S/cm to 3.7 × 10−4 S/cm.12 The conductivity of Al-doped LLZO has been reported as 4 10−4 S/cm, while Ta-doped LLZO could reach 8.7 × 10−4 S/cm.12,41 In addition to the higher ionic conductivity, Ta-doped LLZO was also found to be stable with lithium metal electrode.34,40 While in Al-doped LLZO, intergranular Li metal was observed to propagate through the grain boundaries.42 Therefore, Ta was chosen as the doping element in this work."}, {"context": [{"line": 61, "text": ""}, {"line": 62, "text": "SEM images of fracture cross sections of 30Li and 65Li are shown in Figure 3. After sintering with 15Li cover powder, large pores were observed in Figures 3a–3d. When the cover powder contained LiOH, fewer pores were observed as shown in Figures 3e–3h. The fracture surface of the pellets sintered with 15Li cover powder consisted of smooth rounded surfaces (Figures 3b–3d), but in the sample with 15Li LiOH cover powder, flat surface indicative of brittle or intergranular facture surface were observed (Figures 3f–3h) indicating that the grains are more connected when LiOH was added in the cover powder. These results indicate that LiOH in the cover powder helped with the sintering of LLZTO. An interparticle phase between grains was observed in Figures 3f–3h, which is circled in the figures and found from EDS analysis to contain aluminum as shown in Figure 4."}, {"line": 63, "text": ""}, {"line": 64, "text": "Room temperature impedance spectra for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with cover powders of 15Li or 15Li LiOH are shown in Figure 5. As shown in the typical Nyquist plot for LLZTO pellets in Figure 5, the spectra were fit to an equivalent circuit of L(R CPE )(R CPE ), where L, R and CPE are the inductor, resistor and the constant phase element, and the subscript t and e refer to the total and electrode contribution, respectively. The inductive contribution is attributed to the wires connected to the sample and is not used for the sample properties. The first semicircle is attributed to the total resistance (Rt). The total conductivity is calculated as σtotal t / (A × Rt), where t is the sample thickness and A is the cross-sectional area of the sample. The grain and grain boundary contributions overlapped with each other and cannot be separated, which is consistent with other works.53,56,57 The RT total Li+ conductivity for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with 15Li or 15Li LiOH cover powder are tabulated in Table II. The RT total conductivity of 30Li with 15Li cover powders is 2.0 × 10−5 S/cm. It is significantly lower than that sintered with 15Li + LiOH, which is 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm. Compared to the 30 wt% LiOH samples, samples with higher lithium content (65Li) showed a lower RT σtotal when the cover was 15Li, but similar RT σtotal when the cover contained LiOH. 75Li also showed a similar RT σtotal with 30wt% and 65 wt% LiOH samples when the cover contained LiOH, which indicates that the amount of Li content in the pellets does not affect RT σtotal once it is over 30 wt%. The higher conductivity is consistent with the higher relative density in the samples sintered with 15Li  LiOH cover."}, {"line": 65, "text": ""}, {"line": 66, "text": "Arrhenius plots of total Li+ conductivities for 30Li and 65Li sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with different cover powders are shown in Figure 6 and the corresponding activation energies (Ea) are tabulated in Table II. The activation energy for all samples is from 0.20 to 0.39 eV, but the conductivities of samples sintered with 15Li LiOH cover are significantly higher than those sintered with a 15Li cover powder. The activation energy of 0.2 eV for 30Li sintered with 15Li  LiOH cover is lower than other reported values which are in the range of 0.3–0.4 eV.16,32,35,40,58,59 The EIS tests were performed in a narrow temperature range (20–40°C). Further EIS study is needed to figure out the reason for this low activation energy by obtaining more data points in the EIS tests on 30Li samples, which could be realized by performing the EIS test from a lower temperatures (below 20°C) to 40°C or in a shorter time interval between 20 and 40°C, like 5°C interval time instead of currently used 10°C."}, {"line": 67, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 64, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1106, "start": 198}], "text": "Room temperature impedance spectra for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with cover powders of 15Li or 15Li LiOH are shown in Figure 5. As shown in the typical Nyquist plot for LLZTO pellets in Figure 5, the spectra were fit to an equivalent circuit of L(R CPE )(R CPE ), where L, R and CPE are the inductor, resistor and the constant phase element, and the subscript t and e refer to the total and electrode contribution, respectively. The inductive contribution is attributed to the wires connected to the sample and is not used for the sample properties. The first semicircle is attributed to the total resistance (Rt). The total conductivity is calculated as σtotal t / (A × Rt), where t is the sample thickness and A is the cross-sectional area of the sample. The grain and grain boundary contributions overlapped with each other and cannot be separated, which is consistent with other works.53,56,57 The RT total Li+ conductivity for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with 15Li or 15Li LiOH cover powder are tabulated in Table II. The RT total conductivity of 30Li with 15Li cover powders is 2.0 × 10−5 S/cm. It is significantly lower than that sintered with 15Li + LiOH, which is 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm. Compared to the 30 wt% LiOH samples, samples with higher lithium content (65Li) showed a lower RT σtotal when the cover was 15Li, but similar RT σtotal when the cover contained LiOH. 75Li also showed a similar RT σtotal with 30wt% and 65 wt% LiOH samples when the cover contained LiOH, which indicates that the amount of Li content in the pellets does not affect RT σtotal once it is over 30 wt%. The higher conductivity is consistent with the higher relative density in the samples sintered with 15Li  LiOH cover."}, {"context": [{"line": 10, "text": ""}, {"line": 11, "text": "Rechargeable lithium ion batteries have been widely used as energy sources for electronic mobile devices and hybrid electric vehicles (HEVs) because of their high energy densities. Recently, some researchers have tried to solve safety concerns by eliminating the liquid electrolyte and building an entirely solid-state battery that uses a ceramic electrolyte to separate the lithium anode from the cathode.1–3 The properties of the interface between the solid electrode and the solid electrolyte are one of the most important factors in fabricating an all-solid-state lithium ion battery."}, {"line": 12, "text": ""}, {"line": 13, "text": "Many types of solid-state electrolytes have been explored, including perovskite titanates, NASICON phosphates, LISICON sulfides, and cubic garnets. Among these materials, Li La Zr O (LLZO) with a cubic garnet structure has an ionic conductivity of above approximately 10−4 S/cm at room temperature and good thermal and chemical stability against prospective electrodes such as Li metal.4\u0001 5 Thus, the challenge has been to stabilize the cubic form because the conductivity of the tetragonal phase at room temperature is about 2 orders of magnitude lower than that of the cubic phase.5\u0001 6 Long sintering at a high temperature has been thought to be the key factor for controlling the transformation from tetragonal to cubic.7–10 However, improvement of its ionic conductivity is hindered by the high porosity related to the severe volatilization of the lithium components during high temperature sintering. Therefore, the most effective method for forming the cubic phase has been to dope some elements such as Al,11–13 Ta,14\u0001 15 Nb,10\u0001 16 and Sb17 during the preparation process."}, {"line": 14, "text": ""}, {"line": 15, "text": "The most famous method for fabricating a LLZO powder source has been the solid-state reaction because of its simplicity; however, the calcination temperature is so high that there exists unstable stoichiometry resulting from lithium volatilization and processing difficulty.7\u0001 8\u0001 10–12\u0001 18 Additionally, there has been much interest in the sol–gel synthesis of LLZO powder because of its many advantages in preparing a ceramic source, including a relatively low processing temperature, and the possibility of excellent control of the stoichiometry and homogeneous materials when using large amounts.19\u0001 20 To the best of the authors’ knowledge, few studies have been done directly comparing the two promising methods in a similar system and conditions."}, {"line": 16, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ _ 10.1166_jnn.2016.13570", "line": 13, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> _ 10.1166_jnn.2016.13570.md", "submatches": [{"end": 778, "start": 183}], "text": "Many types of solid-state electrolytes have been explored, including perovskite titanates, NASICON phosphates, LISICON sulfides, and cubic garnets. Among these materials, Li La Zr O (LLZO) with a cubic garnet structure has an ionic conductivity of above approximately 10−4 S/cm at room temperature and good thermal and chemical stability against prospective electrodes such as Li metal.4\u0001 5 Thus, the challenge has been to stabilize the cubic form because the conductivity of the tetragonal phase at room temperature is about 2 orders of magnitude lower than that of the cubic phase.5\u0001 6 Long sintering at a high temperature has been thought to be the key factor for controlling the transformation from tetragonal to cubic.7–10 However, improvement of its ionic conductivity is hindered by the high porosity related to the severe volatilization of the lithium components during high temperature sintering. Therefore, the most effective method for forming the cubic phase has been to dope some elements such as Al,11–13 Ta,14\u0001 15 Nb,10\u0001 16 and Sb17 during the preparation process."}, {"context": [{"line": 6, "text": ""}, {"line": 7, "text": "Department of Mechanical Engineering, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, Michigan 48109-2125"}, {"line": 8, "text": ""}, {"line": 9, "text": "The effect of relative density on the hardness and fracture toughness of Al-substituted cubic garnet Li6.19Al0.27La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) was investigated. Polycrystalline LLZO was made using solid-state synthesis and hot-pressing. The relative density was controlled by varying the densification time at fixed temperature (1050°C) and pressure (62 MPa). After hotpressing, the average grain size varied from approximately 2.7–3.7 lm for the 85% and 98% relative density samples, respectively. Examination of fracture surfaces revealed a transition from inter- to intragranular fracture as the relative density increased. The Vickers hardness increased with relative density up to 96%, above which the hardness was constant. At 98% relative density, the Vickers hardness was equal to the hardness measured by nanoindentation 9.1 GPa, which is estimated as the single-crystal hardness value. An inverse correlation between relative density and fracture toughness was observed. The fracture toughness increased linearly from 0.97 to 2.37 MPa√m for the 98% and 85% relative density samples, respectively. It is suggested that crack deflection along grain boundaries can explain the increase in fracture toughness with decreasing relative density. It was also observed that the total ionic conductivity increased from 0.0094 to 0.34 mS/cm for the 85%–98% relative density samples, respectively. The results of this study suggest that the microstructure of LLZO must be optimized to maximize mechanical integrity and ionic conductivity."}, {"line": 10, "text": ""}, {"line": 11, "text": "# I. Introduction"}, {"line": 12, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ The Effect of Relative Density on the Mechanical Properties of Hot-Pressed Cubic Li7 La3 Zr2 O12 .", "line": 9, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> The Effect of Relative Density on the Mechanical Properties of Hot-Pressed Cubic Li7 La3 Zr2 O12 ..md", "submatches": [{"end": 1529, "start": 124}], "text": "The effect of relative density on the hardness and fracture toughness of Al-substituted cubic garnet Li6.19Al0.27La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) was investigated. Polycrystalline LLZO was made using solid-state synthesis and hot-pressing. The relative density was controlled by varying the densification time at fixed temperature (1050°C) and pressure (62 MPa). After hotpressing, the average grain size varied from approximately 2.7–3.7 lm for the 85% and 98% relative density samples, respectively. Examination of fracture surfaces revealed a transition from inter- to intragranular fracture as the relative density increased. The Vickers hardness increased with relative density up to 96%, above which the hardness was constant. At 98% relative density, the Vickers hardness was equal to the hardness measured by nanoindentation 9.1 GPa, which is estimated as the single-crystal hardness value. An inverse correlation between relative density and fracture toughness was observed. The fracture toughness increased linearly from 0.97 to 2.37 MPa√m for the 98% and 85% relative density samples, respectively. It is suggested that crack deflection along grain boundaries can explain the increase in fracture toughness with decreasing relative density. It was also observed that the total ionic conductivity increased from 0.0094 to 0.34 mS/cm for the 85%–98% relative density samples, respectively. The results of this study suggest that the microstructure of LLZO must be optimized to maximize mechanical integrity and ionic conductivity."}, {"context": [{"line": 14, "text": ""}, {"line": 15, "text": "candidate owing to its high ionic conductivity (\\~10-4 –10-3 S/ cm)7–12 and stability with Li.13,14 While there have been numerous studies that focus on characterizing and improving the conductivity of LLZO,7–12,15–21 there have been relatively few investigations that correlate microstructural features with mechanical properties.22,23"}, {"line": 16, "text": ""}, {"line": 17, "text": "The purpose of this work was to investigate the effect of relative density on the hardness and fracture toughness of hot-pressed Al-substituted LLZO. Though not the focus, the ionic conductivity was also measured as a function of relative density. We believe the results of this work could facilitate the development of LLZO membrane technology and its integration into solid-state batteries."}, {"line": 18, "text": ""}, {"line": 19, "text": "# II. Experimental Procedures"}, {"line": 20, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ The Effect of Relative Density on the Mechanical Properties of Hot-Pressed Cubic Li7 La3 Zr2 O12 .", "line": 17, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> The Effect of Relative Density on the Mechanical Properties of Hot-Pressed Cubic Li7 La3 Zr2 O12 ..md", "submatches": [{"end": 194, "start": 144}], "text": "The purpose of this work was to investigate the effect of relative density on the hardness and fracture toughness of hot-pressed Al-substituted LLZO. Though not the focus, the ionic conductivity was also measured as a function of relative density. We believe the results of this work could facilitate the development of LLZO membrane technology and its integration into solid-state batteries."}, {"context": [{"line": 137, "text": ""}, {"line": 138, "text": "# (6) Ionic Conductivity"}, {"line": 139, "text": ""}, {"line": 140, "text": "The logarithm of total ionic conductivity of hot-pressed LLZO as a function of relative density is shown in Fig. 9. From Fig. 9, it is observed that the total ionic conductivity increases with increasing relative density. This trend is typically observed in LLZO and is usually associated with a decrease in the grain-boundary resistance.15,16,19 The decrease in the grain-boundary resistance component could be a result of a change in the nature of the grain boundary as suggested by David et al.15 At 85% relative density, the total ionic conductivity is 0.0094 mS/cm and increases to 0.34 mS/cm for the 98% relative density sample. The value of 0.34 mS/ cm is in good agreement with the upper values of total conductivity for Al-substituted LLZO of similar composition (0.02–0.5 mS/cm).9,15,17,49 Extrapolation of the curve in Fig. 9 yields a total ionic conductivity of \\~0.4 mS/cm, which is in excellent agreement with previously reported bulk conductivity values.9,15,21"}, {"line": 141, "text": ""}, {"line": 142, "text": "![](images/b9ff5f1de146ef61f895be7d881ca4a5133778214a8e6e1a21e184d06a79919f.jpg)  "}, {"line": 143, "text": "Fig. 9. Total ionic conductivity of Li6.19Al0.27La3Zr2O12 as a function of relative density."}], "document_id": "_ The Effect of Relative Density on the Mechanical Properties of Hot-Pressed Cubic Li7 La3 Zr2 O12 .", "line": 140, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> The Effect of Relative Density on the Mechanical Properties of Hot-Pressed Cubic Li7 La3 Zr2 O12 ..md", "submatches": [{"end": 872, "start": 57}], "text": "The logarithm of total ionic conductivity of hot-pressed LLZO as a function of relative density is shown in Fig. 9. From Fig. 9, it is observed that the total ionic conductivity increases with increasing relative density. This trend is typically observed in LLZO and is usually associated with a decrease in the grain-boundary resistance.15,16,19 The decrease in the grain-boundary resistance component could be a result of a change in the nature of the grain boundary as suggested by David et al.15 At 85% relative density, the total ionic conductivity is 0.0094 mS/cm and increases to 0.34 mS/cm for the 98% relative density sample. The value of 0.34 mS/ cm is in good agreement with the upper values of total conductivity for Al-substituted LLZO of similar composition (0.02–0.5 mS/cm).9,15,17,49 Extrapolation of the curve in Fig. 9 yields a total ionic conductivity of \\~0.4 mS/cm, which is in excellent agreement with previously reported bulk conductivity values.9,15,21"}, {"context": [{"line": 156, "text": ""}, {"line": 157, "text": "The total ionic conductivity increased with increasing relative density. This increase is associated with an increase in the grain-boundary conductivity as a result of the change in the nature of the grain boundaries with the increasing relative density. At a relative density of 85%, the total ionic conductivity was 0.0094 mS/cm and increased to 0.34 mS/cm for the sample with a relative density of 98%."}, {"line": 158, "text": ""}, {"line": 159, "text": "An interesting correlation between ionic conductivity and K was observed. As the relative density increased, the ionic conductivity increased while the K decreased. This correlation suggests that if one desires a LLZO material with high ionic conductivity, a sacrifice in KIC will occur."}, {"line": 160, "text": ""}, {"line": 161, "text": "# Acknowledgments"}, {"line": 162, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ The Effect of Relative Density on the Mechanical Properties of Hot-Pressed Cubic Li7 La3 Zr2 O12 .", "line": 159, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> The Effect of Relative Density on the Mechanical Properties of Hot-Pressed Cubic Li7 La3 Zr2 O12 ..md", "submatches": [{"end": 255, "start": 213}], "text": "An interesting correlation between ionic conductivity and K was observed. As the relative density increased, the ionic conductivity increased while the K decreased. This correlation suggests that if one desires a LLZO material with high ionic conductivity, a sacrifice in KIC will occur."}, {"context": [{"line": 45, "text": ""}, {"line": 46, "text": "Figure 3 shows the impedance spectra of as-obtained LSTP thin films. The ionic conductivity of as-obtained LSTP films is calculated by the following formula [5, 8, 16] :"}, {"line": 47, "text": ""}, {"line": 48, "text": "where d is the thickness of the film, A is the area of the Ag electrode, and R is illustrated in AC impedance spectroscopy. Meanwhile, the area of all the film is about 0.27 cm2. Unlike the crystallized bulk solid electrolytes, whose AC impedance spectroscopy displays two semicircles represented grain resistance and grain boundary resistance, respectively, only one complete semicircle can be found in the spectra, which is mainly due to the amorphous structure of LSTP thin film electrolyte without grain boundary. This result is agreed with the previous studies focused on the thin film electrolyte with amorphous structure [9, 16]. Owing to that of uniform particles compacted in arrangement, proper stoichiometric ratio to the powder target as well as sufficient Li-ion for diffusion, the LSTP thin films prepared with the sputtering power of 160 W hold a considerable ionic conductivity of about 4.12 × 10−6 S·cm−1. This result is better than that of amorphous LLZO with a ionic conductivity of 4 × 10−7 S·cm−1, and close to the crystallized LATP thick layers with a ionic conductivity of 1.12 × 10−6 S·cm−1 obtained via aerosol deposition technique [16, 18]."}, {"line": 49, "text": ""}, {"line": 50, "text": "Figure 4 shows the current–voltage curve. The electronic conductivity of LSTP thin films electrolyte was calculated by the following formula."}, {"line": 51, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ _ 10.1007_s10854-019-02558-5", "line": 48, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> _ 10.1007_s10854-019-02558-5.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1114, "start": 980}], "text": "where d is the thickness of the film, A is the area of the Ag electrode, and R is illustrated in AC impedance spectroscopy. Meanwhile, the area of all the film is about 0.27 cm2. Unlike the crystallized bulk solid electrolytes, whose AC impedance spectroscopy displays two semicircles represented grain resistance and grain boundary resistance, respectively, only one complete semicircle can be found in the spectra, which is mainly due to the amorphous structure of LSTP thin film electrolyte without grain boundary. This result is agreed with the previous studies focused on the thin film electrolyte with amorphous structure [9, 16]. Owing to that of uniform particles compacted in arrangement, proper stoichiometric ratio to the powder target as well as sufficient Li-ion for diffusion, the LSTP thin films prepared with the sputtering power of 160 W hold a considerable ionic conductivity of about 4.12 × 10−6 S·cm−1. This result is better than that of amorphous LLZO with a ionic conductivity of 4 × 10−7 S·cm−1, and close to the crystallized LATP thick layers with a ionic conductivity of 1.12 × 10−6 S·cm−1 obtained via aerosol deposition technique [16, 18]."}, {"context": [{"line": 32, "text": ""}, {"line": 33, "text": "The Li0.33La0.557TiO3 (LLTO) nanowires with high ionic conductivity were prepared through electrospinning and subsequent high temperature calcinations. It was filled in the blend of poly (ethylene oxide) (PEO) and poly (propylene carbonate) (PPC) as a filler and the novel solid PEO/PPC/LLTO nanowires polymer composite electrolytes were prepared via solution casting method with low cost. The addition of LLTO nanowires could effectively improve the ionic conductivity, electrochemical stability window, transference number, and compatibility with lithium metal of polymer composite electrolytes. The LiFePO /Li all solid state lithium ion battery assembled with the novel polymer composite electrolytes exhibited high electrochemical performance."}, {"line": 34, "text": ""}, {"line": 35, "text": "The study of Yu25 showed that the crystallinity of PEO could be effectively reduced by the addition of poly (propylene carbonate) (PPC) to the PEO matrix. So, the mechanical property and some electrochemical performances, such as ionic conductivity and electrochemical stability window, could be improved. However, the sole blending of amorphous PPC with PEO based SPEs still could not meet the requirements to enhance the electrochemical performances, such as ionic conductivity. So, the addition of fast ionic conductor fillers with high ionic conductivity, such as Li1.5Al0.5Ge1.5(PO4)3 (LAGP),26 Li10GeP2S12 (LGPS),27 Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO),28 and Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 (LLZTO),29 or the ceramic fillers, such as Al O 30 SiO ,31 and Li N,32 was also needed. Due to the addition of fillers, the crystallization of polymer was reduced, and the ion transport channel was formed at the interface between fillers and polymers, which further improves the ionic conductivity of SPE. Additionally, to greatly enhance the ionic conductivity of PEO based SPEs, it required to fill as many fillers as possible. However, it would lead to the accumulation of the fillers, which reduced the mechanical properties of SPE.33 So, the filler with a larger specific surface area should be selected as a promising filler."}, {"line": 36, "text": ""}, {"line": 37, "text": "In this study, a novel solid polymer composite electrolyte (SPCE) was prepared by adding appropriate amount of PPC and fast ionic conductor Li0.33La0.557TiO3 nanowires (LLTO NWs) filler into the PEO matrix. The LLTO NWs, which were synthesized by electrospinning and subsequent calcination, had large specific surface area and wide electrochemical window. At the same time, the ionic conductivity, ITN, and electrochemical stability window of PEO PPC lithium bis (fluorosulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI) LLTO SPCE, were also studied. LiFePO , as an active cathode material for lithium battery, which had stable structure, good thermal stability, good contact ability with electrolyte film, and could maintain capacity in a long charge discharge cycle, had already been prepared and applied successfully in laboratory.34,35 So, the button cell was assembled with LiFePO as a cathode active material, lithium metal as anode material, and the prepared SPCE film as electrolyte, and the cycle performance and rate capability were investigated."}, {"line": 38, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ High‐performance solid PEO_PPC_LLTO‐nanowires polymer composite electrolyte for solid‐state lithiu", "line": 35, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> High‐performance solid PEO_PPC_LLTO‐nanowires polymer composite electrolyte for solid‐state lithiu.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1032, "start": 636}], "text": "The study of Yu25 showed that the crystallinity of PEO could be effectively reduced by the addition of poly (propylene carbonate) (PPC) to the PEO matrix. So, the mechanical property and some electrochemical performances, such as ionic conductivity and electrochemical stability window, could be improved. However, the sole blending of amorphous PPC with PEO based SPEs still could not meet the requirements to enhance the electrochemical performances, such as ionic conductivity. So, the addition of fast ionic conductor fillers with high ionic conductivity, such as Li1.5Al0.5Ge1.5(PO4)3 (LAGP),26 Li10GeP2S12 (LGPS),27 Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO),28 and Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 (LLZTO),29 or the ceramic fillers, such as Al O 30 SiO ,31 and Li N,32 was also needed. Due to the addition of fillers, the crystallization of polymer was reduced, and the ion transport channel was formed at the interface between fillers and polymers, which further improves the ionic conductivity of SPE. Additionally, to greatly enhance the ionic conductivity of PEO based SPEs, it required to fill as many fillers as possible. However, it would lead to the accumulation of the fillers, which reduced the mechanical properties of SPE.33 So, the filler with a larger specific surface area should be selected as a promising filler."}, {"context": [{"line": 24, "text": ""}, {"line": 25, "text": "Solid electrolytes for all solid state lithium batteries show unique advantages over organic based liquid electrolytes such as excellent battery lifetime, electrochemical and thermal stability, and enhanced safety.1-3 Accordingly, they received a lot of interest especially for the safety concerning applications such as electrical vehicles. Solid electrolytes can be divided into six different crystallographic orientations. These structures are NASICON (eg, LiTi2(PO4)3), LISICON (eg, Li10GeP2S12), perovskite (eg, Li0.34La0.56TiO3), sulfide (eg, Li7P3S11), antiperovskite (eg, Li3OCl), and garnet (eg, Li7La3Zr2O12) type solid electrolytes.4"}, {"line": 26, "text": ""}, {"line": 27, "text": "Nowadays, garnet type solid electrolytes became popular because of their outstanding ionic conductivities among other types of solid electrolytes. Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) having a cubic crystal structure, as one of the garnet type solid electrolyte, was successfully adapted in the lab scale all solid state lithium battery assembly. However, the observed specific capacity was very limited for practical applications even if the slower charge/discharge rates were used.5 It is worth to emphasize that Al incorporation in LLZO lattice resulted in not only lowering the synthesis temperature but also increasing ionic conductivity by creating Li vacancies.6"}, {"line": 28, "text": ""}, {"line": 29, "text": "There are lots of studies based on the solid state reaction LLZO synthesis. Among them, various types of precursors could be seen. In Table 1, the list of precursors used for LLZO synthesis and their heat treatment conditions were presented."}, {"line": 30, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Achieving high performance for aluminum stabilized Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__su", "line": 27, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> high performance for aluminum stabilized Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__su.md", "submatches": [{"end": 625, "start": 161}], "text": "Nowadays, garnet type solid electrolytes became popular because of their outstanding ionic conductivities among other types of solid electrolytes. Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) having a cubic crystal structure, as one of the garnet type solid electrolyte, was successfully adapted in the lab scale all solid state lithium battery assembly. However, the observed specific capacity was very limited for practical applications even if the slower charge/discharge rates were used.5 It is worth to emphasize that Al incorporation in LLZO lattice resulted in not only lowering the synthesis temperature but also increasing ionic conductivity by creating Li vacancies.6"}], "ok": true, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO).*(ionic conductivity|total conductivity)", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 30, "truncated": true}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:09:49.181135+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
