tivation energy of multicomponent transition metal oxalates to probe synthesis of batte.md", "submatches": [{"end": 656, "start": 642}], "text": "[7] Y.K. Sun, B.R. Lee, H.J. Noh, H. Wu, S.T. Myung, K. Amine, A novel concentrationgradient Li[Ni0.83Co0.07Mn0.10]O2cathode material for high-energy lithium-ion batteries, J. Mater. Chem. 21 (2011) 10108–10112, https://doi.org/10.1039/ c0jm04242k. [8] L. Li, S. Song, X. Zhang, R. Chen, J. Lu, F. Wu, K. Amine, Ultrasonic-assisted coprecipitation to synthesize lithium-rich cathode Li1.3Ni0.21Mn0.64O2+d materials for lithium-ion batteries, J. Power Sources 272 (2014) 922–928, https://doi.org/10. 1016/j.jpowsour.2014.08.063. [9] Q. Ma, D. Mu, Y. Liu, S. Yin, C. Dai, Enhancing coulombic efficiency and rate capability of high capacity lithium excess layered oxide cathode material by electrocatalysis of nanogold, RSC Adv. 6 (2016) 20374–20380, https://doi.org/10.1039/C5RA26667J.   "}, {"context": [{"line": 39, "text": ""}, {"line": 40, "text": "Li4SiO4 was initially obtained via solid-state reaction by mixing lithium oxide (Li2O, Aldrich) and fumed silica (SiO2, Sigma-Aldrich) with a Li/Si molar ratio of 4.1:1. The powders were mechanically mixed during 15 min to obtain a homogenous precursor mixture, which was subsequently heated at 800 C for 6 h. On the other hand, pentalithium ferrite (LisFeO4) was synthesized by solid-state reaction using lithium oxide and iron oxide (Fe2O3, Aldrich) with a 20 mol % of lithium excess, according with the stoichiometric Li/Fe molar ratio of 5:1 due to its high tendency to sublimate. Initially, the powders were mechanically mixed and pressed into pellets (15 MPa). Subsequently, these pellets were heated at 850 C for 20 h.29 Both samples were analyzed by powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) to confirm the presence of each phase. Then, Li4+xSi--Fe,O4 solid solution samples were obtained by dry ball milling process, using a Spex Sample-Prep 8000 M miller, during 15 min. Five different Li4+xSi1--Fe,O4 samples (x = 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4 and 0.5) were prepared by mixing mechanically the corresponding amounts of Li4SiO4 and LisFeO4."}, {"line": 41, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis of Li &lt;sub_&gt;4&#43;x&lt;_sub&gt; Si &lt;sub_&gt;1-x&lt;_sub&gt; Fe &lt;sub_&gt;x&lt", "line": 40, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis of Li &lt;sub_&gt;4&#43;x&lt;_sub&gt; Si &lt;sub_&gt;1-x&lt;_sub&gt; Fe &lt;sub_&gt;x&lt.md", "submatches": [{"end": 485, "start": 471}], "text": "Li4SiO4 was initially obtained via solid-state reaction by mixing lithium oxide (Li2O, Aldrich) and fumed silica (SiO2, Sigma-Aldrich) with a Li/Si molar ratio of 4.1:1. The powders were mechanically mixed during 15 min to obtain a homogenous precursor mixture, which was subsequently heated at 800 C for 6 h. On the other hand, pentalithium ferrite (LisFeO4) was synthesized by solid-state reaction using lithium oxide and iron oxide (Fe2O3, Aldrich) with a 20 mol % of lithium excess, according with the stoichiometric Li/Fe molar ratio of 5:1 due to its high tendency to sublimate. Initially, the powders were mechanically mixed and pressed into pellets (15 MPa). Subsequently, these pellets were heated at 850 C for 20 h.29 Both samples were analyzed by powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) to confirm the presence of each phase. Then, Li4+xSi--Fe,O4 solid solution samples were obtained by dry ball milling process, using a Spex Sample-Prep 8000 M miller, during 15 min. Five different Li4+xSi1--Fe,O4 samples (x = 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4 and 0.5) were prepared by mixing mechanically the corresponding amounts of Li4SiO4 and LisFeO4."}, {"context": [{"line": 59, "text": ""}, {"line": 60, "text": "riguIe 3 snows te expennental AKD pauens o1 ue LI4+xD1-xFexU4 sonu soluuon samples. The X-ray diffraction patterns of these samples were measured with the highest possible accuracy and signal/noise ratio. However, as the diffraction data generally shows a noisy background due to experimental error, i.e. diffractometer geometry, detector resolution, etc, the so-called \"data processing\" had to be carried out, including data smoothing. The smoothed XRD patterns (red lines) were fitted over the experimental profile line. In order to show the experimental diffraction profile, Figure 3-b presents the smoothed and free background XRD patterns. It must be pointed out that diffraction peaks corresponded to LisSiO4 structure, which means that the crystal structure of lithium silicate was preserved, and both, substitutional iron and interstitial lithium atoms must be into this crystal structure. The intensity of (2 0 -6) and (1 2 -3) diffraction peaks decrease as iron content increases, which is in a good agreement with the model presented before and which indicates that iron is located on silicon position. However, it can be noticed that additional peaks decreased their intensities as iron and lithium contents increased, for example, (1 1 4) and (1 1 -3) peaks. This behavior would be an effect of some changes in grain size. The average grain size on (1 1 -3) plane direction decreased as function of the iron content as it is shown in Table 1, from 18.86 to 8.48 nm, when iron content changed from 0 to 0.5, respectively. Between 0 and 0.2, grain size decreased from 18.86 to 9.49 nm. However, after that iron content, changes in the grain size were lower. It has been proved that milling processes induce a decrement in the average grain size.36 In this case all the samples were milled during the same time (15 min). Therefore, the difference between these grain size values should be an effect of the iron content, inhibiting the sintering process. Moreover, lithium excess could cause a delocalization of lithium atoms on those planes (Figure 4). In this figure, (1 2 -3) and (1 1 -3) planes are mostly composed by lithium atoms, thus this decrement on intensity would be explained with a delocalization of lithium atoms. It is worth noting that diffraction peak relative intensities vary considerably, in fact when the iron content increases the (2 0 -6) peak becomes the principal peak of this phase. Finally, the interplanar distances of (2 0 -6), (1 2 -3), (1 1 -3) and (1 1 4) planes were measured and they are presented on Table 2. In all these cases, it can be seen slightly increases of plane spacing as iron and lithium contents increase. This effect should be attributed to iron substitution in silicon positions and lithium incorporations."}, {"line": 61, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis of Li &lt;sub_&gt;4&#43;x&lt;_sub&gt; Si &lt;sub_&gt;1-x&lt;_sub&gt; Fe &lt;sub_&gt;x&lt", "line": 60, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis of Li &lt;sub_&gt;4&#43;x&lt;_sub&gt; Si &lt;sub_&gt;1-x&lt;_sub&gt; Fe &lt;sub_&gt;x&lt.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1988, "start": 1974}], "text": "riguIe 3 snows te expennental AKD pauens o1 ue LI4+xD1-xFexU4 sonu soluuon samples. The X-ray diffraction patterns of these samples were measured with the highest possible accuracy and signal/noise ratio. However, as the diffraction data generally shows a noisy background due to experimental error, i.e. diffractometer geometry, detector resolution, etc, the so-called \"data processing\" had to be carried out, including data smoothing. The smoothed XRD patterns (red lines) were fitted over the experimental profile line. In order to show the experimental diffraction profile, Figure 3-b presents the smoothed and free background XRD patterns. It must be pointed out that diffraction peaks corresponded to LisSiO4 structure, which means that the crystal structure of lithium silicate was preserved, and both, substitutional iron and interstitial lithium atoms must be into this crystal structure. The intensity of (2 0 -6) and (1 2 -3) diffraction peaks decrease as iron content increases, which is in a good agreement with the model presented before and which indicates that iron is located on silicon position. However, it can be noticed that additional peaks decreased their intensities as iron and lithium contents increased, for example, (1 1 4) and (1 1 -3) peaks. This behavior would be an effect of some changes in grain size. The average grain size on (1 1 -3) plane direction decreased as function of the iron content as it is shown in Table 1, from 18.86 to 8.48 nm, when iron content changed from 0 to 0.5, respectively. Between 0 and 0.2, grain size decreased from 18.86 to 9.49 nm. However, after that iron content, changes in the grain size were lower. It has been proved that milling processes induce a decrement in the average grain size.36 In this case all the samples were milled during the same time (15 min). Therefore, the difference between these grain size values should be an effect of the iron content, inhibiting the sintering process. Moreover, lithium excess could cause a delocalization of lithium atoms on those planes (Figure 4). In this figure, (1 2 -3) and (1 1 -3) planes are mostly composed by lithium atoms, thus this decrement on intensity would be explained with a delocalization of lithium atoms. It is worth noting that diffraction peak relative intensities vary considerably, in fact when the iron content increases the (2 0 -6) peak becomes the principal peak of this phase. Finally, the interplanar distances of (2 0 -6), (1 2 -3), (1 1 -3) and (1 1 4) planes were measured and they are presented on Table 2. In all these cases, it can be seen slightly increases of plane spacing as iron and lithium contents increase. This effect should be attributed to iron substitution in silicon positions and lithium incorporations."}, {"context": [{"line": 65, "text": ""}, {"line": 66, "text": "In addition, mechanical attrition of some lithium containing ceramics creates high defect concentrations and partially amorphous materials, which increment the ion conductivity.34 Besides, Izquierdo et al. concluded that small crystal sizes lead to the introduction of vacancies into the crystal lattice of LigSiO4 improving lithium mobility and facilitating ion hopping.2,12 Consequently, lithium excess may be located in interstitial sites and some ion defects created by the milling process. Therefore, ball milling process induces lithium and iron incorporation into the lithium silicate crystal structure, where Li4SiO4 crystal structure is preserved."}, {"line": 67, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis of Li &lt;sub_&gt;4&#43;x&lt;_sub&gt; Si &lt;sub_&gt;1-x&lt;_sub&gt; Fe &lt;sub_&gt;x&lt", "line": 66, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis of Li &lt;sub_&gt;4&#43;x&lt;_sub&gt; Si &lt;sub_&gt;1-x&lt;_sub&gt; Fe &lt;sub_&gt;x&lt.md", "submatches": [{"end": 404, "start": 390}], "text": "In addition, mechanical attrition of some lithium containing ceramics creates high defect concentrations and partially amorphous materials, which increment the ion conductivity.34 Besides, Izquierdo et al. concluded that small crystal sizes lead to the introduction of vacancies into the crystal lattice of LigSiO4 improving lithium mobility and facilitating ion hopping.2,12 Consequently, lithium excess may be located in interstitial sites and some ion defects created by the milling process. Therefore, ball milling process induces lithium and iron incorporation into the lithium silicate crystal structure, where Li4SiO4 crystal structure is preserved."}, {"context": [{"line": 92, "text": ""}, {"line": 93, "text": "Xu et al. [23] used the Z-contrast to visualise the changes in stacking sequence and ordering of transition metal layers upon cycling in layered lithium excess nickel manganese oxides."}, {"line": 94, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Characterisation of battery materials by electron and ion microscopy techniques_ a review _ 10.151", "line": 93, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Characterisation of battery materials by electron and ion microscopy techniques_ a review _ 10.151.md", "submatches": [{"end": 159, "start": 145}], "text": "Xu et al. [23] used the Z-contrast to visualise the changes in stacking sequence and ordering of transition metal layers upon cycling in layered lithium excess nickel manganese oxides."}, {"context": [{"line": 15, "text": ""}, {"line": 16, "text": "It is acknowledged that the working principle of secondary ion batteries is based on the intercalation/deintercalation mechanism. An electronically conducting framework contains transition metal ions in a mixed valent state. Inserting Bguest^ lithium ions into such a Bhost^ framework would reduce the framework, and extracting the guest ions would oxidize the framework. However, when the mixed valent transition metal cannot be oxidized, extracting the guest ions will be related to oxygen release. This situation has been studied in lithium excess compounds such as Li2MnO3 [1, 2]. In this Mn4+- based crystal, Li may be extracted without oxidation of"}, {"line": 17, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis and electrochemical properties of P2-Na0.7Zn0.15Mn0.75O2 _ 10.1007_s11581-018-2681-5", "line": 16, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis and electrochemical properties of P2-Na0.7Zn0.15Mn0.75O2 _ 10.1007_s11581-018-2681-5.md", "submatches": [{"end": 550, "start": 536}], "text": "It is acknowledged that the working principle of secondary ion batteries is based on the intercalation/deintercalation mechanism. An electronically conducting framework contains transition metal ions in a mixed valent state. Inserting Bguest^ lithium ions into such a Bhost^ framework would reduce the framework, and extracting the guest ions would oxidize the framework. However, when the mixed valent transition metal cannot be oxidized, extracting the guest ions will be related to oxygen release. This situation has been studied in lithium excess compounds such as Li2MnO3 [1, 2]. In this Mn4+- based crystal, Li may be extracted without oxidation of"}], "ok": true, "query": "lithium excess", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 8, "truncated": true}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:09:53.290935+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:24:{"duration_ms": 45008.829, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 3, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 30, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO).*(reproducibility|reproducible|batch)", "regex": true, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 66, "text": ""}, {"line": 67, "text": "![](images/edb2fcee6a51e409cc2abb25e26ede7f9bb9b3d6b435ff01a86ac6d603c9e377.jpg)"}, {"line": 68, "text": ""}, {"line": 69, "text": "Along with the different solvent composition, the wt% of PVP in the needleless solution had to be increased to exceed the percentage of LLZO precursors, whereas the loading of precursor reagents was higher in the SNE solution. Using the solution optimized for the needleless reactor (viscosity of 0.17 Pa·s), 0.23 g/h of as-spun nanofibers could be obtained using a precursor feed rate of 10 mL/hr and applied voltage bias of 15 kV between the spinneret and collector. This production rate was increased by increasing the feed rate to 15 mL/hr and voltage to 20 kV, with an average rate of 1.023 ± 0.094 g/h over 14 reactions with good reproducibility (Table S1). Compared to a typical nanofiber production rate using SNE, the needleless electrospinning offers a higher production rate by approximately one order of magnitude. One disadvantage of the needleless reactor, as first observed by Holopainen et al.28, is the gelling or drying of the solution on the twisted wire, which requires cleaning in between use and limits prolonged synthesis. Typically, needleless electrospinning could be performed continuously for 45 min at a time to produce, on average, 767 mg of nanofibers, before needing to be stopped and cleaned. However, this gelling process, which occurs due to solvent evaporation, is analogous to clogging of the needle in conventional electrospinning33,34, which similarly limits SNE of LLZO to \\~2 h of synthesis time before the needle needs to be cleaned."}, {"line": 70, "text": ""}, {"line": 71, "text": "Figure 2A shows a photograph of a nanofiber mat prepared after 45 min of reaction using needless electrospinning (approximate dimensions: 41 inch x 10 inch) . The deposition of nanofibers on the mat was very uniform, confirming the radial formation of liquid jets from the spinneret. The mat could be easily removed from the collector as a single piece (the lower left hand corner of the mat in Figure 2A is partially removed). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) imaging showed that the as-spun nanofibers formed a nanoribbon morphology with more of a rectangular cross-section (Figure 2B), with average diameter/width of 650 nm (Figure 2C). The morphology of the as-spun nanofibers was consistent throughout the mat, except some curled fibers were observed close to the edges of the mat (Figure S2), likely due to non-uniform current distribution at either end of the collector. The nanoribbon morphology in materials produced with needleless electrospinning is different from what was observed in as-spun nanofibers prepared using SNE, which had cylindrical cross-sections. Similar morphologies have been observed previously and have been attributed to the formation of a thin polymer skin on the surface of the liquid jet, which subsequently collapses into a flat nanoribbon due to solvent evaporation35. In our case, this is a feasible explanation as well, since the needless electrospinning precursor required a large vol% of ethanol; the ribbon morphology may also be a feature of this type of reactor, as Holopainen et al. also reported similar morphologies for PVP and hydroxyapatite nanofibers produced in their setup28."}, {"line": 72, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Needleless Electrospinning for High Throughput Production of Li &lt;sub_&gt;7&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;s", "line": 69, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Needleless Electrospinning for High Throughput Production of Li &lt;sub_&gt;7&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;s.md", "submatches": [{"end": 653, "start": 136}], "text": "Along with the different solvent composition, the wt% of PVP in the needleless solution had to be increased to exceed the percentage of LLZO precursors, whereas the loading of precursor reagents was higher in the SNE solution. Using the solution optimized for the needleless reactor (viscosity of 0.17 Pa·s), 0.23 g/h of as-spun nanofibers could be obtained using a precursor feed rate of 10 mL/hr and applied voltage bias of 15 kV between the spinneret and collector. This production rate was increased by increasing the feed rate to 15 mL/hr and voltage to 20 kV, with an average rate of 1.023 ± 0.094 g/h over 14 reactions with good reproducibility (Table S1). Compared to a typical nanofiber production rate using SNE, the needleless electrospinning offers a higher production rate by approximately one order of magnitude. One disadvantage of the needleless reactor, as first observed by Holopainen et al.28, is the gelling or drying of the solution on the twisted wire, which requires cleaning in between use and limits prolonged synthesis. Typically, needleless electrospinning could be performed continuously for 45 min at a time to produce, on average, 767 mg of nanofibers, before needing to be stopped and cleaned. However, this gelling process, which occurs due to solvent evaporation, is analogous to clogging of the needle in conventional electrospinning33,34, which similarly limits SNE of LLZO to \\~2 h of synthesis time before the needle needs to be cleaned."}], "ok": true, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO).*(reproducibility|reproducible|batch)", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": true, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 1, "truncated": false}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:10:34.203374+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:26:{"duration_ms": 3361.003, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 2, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 12, "query": "cover powder", "regex": false, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 29, "text": "To study the composition and functional groups present in the product powder, Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopic measurements were taken on Perkin-Elmer FTIR (model Midac 1000 series) spectrophotometer. Infrared spectra were recorded in the region of 500 cm-1 to 4500 cm-1 with a resolution of 16 cm-1."}, {"line": 30, "text": ""}, {"line": 31, "text": "The X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were taken by using a Bruker D8 Discover powder diffractometer using Cu-K radiation with a wavelength of 1.54 Å. The instrument was operated at 40 kV with a current of 40 mA. The specimens were scanned between 2θ range of 15° to 70° at a scanning speed of 4°/min."}, {"line": 32, "text": ""}, {"line": 33, "text": "The nano powders were characterized including their particle size, shape and morphology by using a Jeol JSM 6340F field-emission scanning electron microscope. Thorough analysis of the crystallite size and shape within individual particles was carried out by using a JEOL 2100 transmission electron microscope also equipped with a field emission gun."}], "document_id": "_ On the Formation of Hydroxyapatite Nano Crystals Prepared Using Cationic Surfactant _ 10.1590_1516", "line": 31, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> On the Formation of Hydroxyapatite Nano Crystals Prepared Using Cationic Surfactant _ 10.1590_1516.md", "submatches": [{"end": 88, "start": 76}], "text": "The X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were taken by using a Bruker D8 Discover powder diffractometer using Cu-K radiation with a wavelength of 1.54 Å. The instrument was operated at 40 kV with a current of 40 mA. The specimens were scanned between 2θ range of 15° to 70° at a scanning speed of 4°/min."}, {"context": [{"line": 122, "text": "# 11 X-ray powder diffraction"}, {"line": 123, "text": ""}, {"line": 124, "text": "Sample material from the solid-state reactions was analysed after each firing process with a Bruker D8-Discover powder-diffractometer. The tablets were finely ground in"}, {"line": 125, "text": ""}, {"line": 126, "text": "# Raman spectroscopic studies"}], "document_id": "Crystal structure, Raman spectroscopy and crystal chemistry of K&#60;sub_7&#60;_sub_Ca&#60;sub_9&#60", "line": 124, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> structure, Raman spectroscopy and crystal chemistry of K&#60;sub_7&#60;_sub_Ca&#60;sub_9&#60.md", "submatches": [{"end": 118, "start": 106}], "text": "Sample material from the solid-state reactions was analysed after each firing process with a Bruker D8-Discover powder-diffractometer. The tablets were finely ground in"}, {"context": [{"line": 6, "text": "2Department of Chemical Engineering, Auburn University, Auburn, Alabama 36849, USA"}, {"line": 7, "text": ""}, {"line": 8, "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}, {"line": 9, "text": ""}, {"line": 10, "text": "© The Author(s) 2019. Published by ECS. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License (CC BY, http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted reuse of the work in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. [DOI: 10.1149/2.1031915jes]"}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 8, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 580, "start": 568}, {"end": 615, "start": 603}, {"end": 811, "start": 799}], "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}, {"context": [{"line": 24, "text": "When the simple method of cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in air is used, additives are typically needed to obtain high density, details of which could be found in Table I. 51–55 Additions of La2Zr2O7 and Li O to Ta-doped LLZO and Li SiO to Al-doped LLZO have led to improvements of 6–17% in density which increased conductivity 3–5 times.51–53 Even larger increases in densities have been reported with additions of LiOH, Li3BO3 and LiBO2 to Al- and Nb-doped LLZO, but the conductivities are all smaller than 1 × 10−4 S/cm.51,54,55 Samples with conductivities larger than 1 10−4 S/cm were sintered at temperature above 1100°C or prepared with cold uniaxial pressing.51–53"}, {"line": 25, "text": ""}, {"line": 26, "text": "In this work, Ta-doped LLZO (LLZTO) was synthesized through a co-precipitation method with a low sintering temperature of 1100°C. The effects of a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which is created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder, on the phase stabilization, densification and Li+ conductivity were examined. LiOH was chosen as the additive since it is one of the starting materials."}, {"line": 27, "text": ""}, {"line": 28, "text": "![](images/dcf5dcced6806b7b3585f290717df8fdbc3703fdde38854c5716d617ce435954.jpg)"}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 26, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 243, "start": 231}], "text": "In this work, Ta-doped LLZO (LLZTO) was synthesized through a co-precipitation method with a low sintering temperature of 1100°C. The effects of a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which is created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder, on the phase stabilization, densification and Li+ conductivity were examined. LiOH was chosen as the additive since it is one of the starting materials."}, {"context": [{"line": 36, "text": "ownloaded on 2019-11-14 to IP 5.189.201.153 address. Redistribution subject to ECS terms of use (see ecsdl.org/site/terms_use) unless CC License in place (see abstract)"}, {"line": 37, "text": ""}, {"line": 38, "text": "After drying at 80°C for 24 hours, the powders were cold pressed into pellets (10 mm in diameter and 1–3 mm in thickness) at a pressure of 200 MPa. The pellets were covered with cover powders, placed in a modified alumina crucible (shown in Figure 1b), with an alumina lid and sintered in air with a heating rate of 1°C/min to 1100°C for 1–32 hours. The detailed procedure for the preparation of LLZTO is shown in Figure 1a."}, {"line": 39, "text": ""}, {"line": 40, "text": "During sintering the pellets were covered with powders to reduce the loss of lithium. Four columns, with four pellets in each column, of samples were sintered at one time. Only the middle two pellets were used as the bottom pellet was in contact with the crucible and the top pellet exhibited different sintering behavior, presumably because the atmosphere was different as it was closer to the air above the cover powder. Two kinds of cover powders were used. One is referred to 15Li, which is the powder after the heat-treatment of 700°C and before the ball milling and with 15 wt% excess LiOH. The other one is referred to 15Li LiOH, which is a mixture of 15Li and 6 wt% LiOH. This mixture was jar milled for 12 hours before the use in sintering. The notches in the crucible were found to be necessary (Figure S1 in Supporting Information) to allow an optimal amount of gas exchange between inside of the crucible and surrounding atmosphere to establish the local gas atmosphere to promote the sintering behavior."}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 38, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 193, "start": 181}], "text": "After drying at 80°C for 24 hours, the powders were cold pressed into pellets (10 mm in diameter and 1–3 mm in thickness) at a pressure of 200 MPa. The pellets were covered with cover powders, placed in a modified alumina crucible (shown in Figure 1b), with an alumina lid and sintered in air with a heating rate of 1°C/min to 1100°C for 1–32 hours. The detailed procedure for the preparation of LLZTO is shown in Figure 1a."}, {"context": [{"line": 38, "text": "After drying at 80°C for 24 hours, the powders were cold pressed into pellets (10 mm in diameter and 1–3 mm in thickness) at a pressure of 200 MPa. The pellets were covered with cover powders, placed in a modified alumina crucible (shown in Figure 1b), with an alumina lid and sintered in air with a heating rate of 1°C/min to 1100°C for 1–32 hours. The detailed procedure for the preparation of LLZTO is shown in Figure 1a."}, {"line": 39, "text": ""}, {"line": 40, "text": "During sintering the pellets were covered with powders to reduce the loss of lithium. Four columns, with four pellets in each column, of samples were sintered at one time. Only the middle two pellets were used as the bottom pellet was in contact with the crucible and the top pellet exhibited different sintering behavior, presumably because the atmosphere was different as it was closer to the air above the cover powder. Two kinds of cover powders were used. One is referred to 15Li, which is the powder after the heat-treatment of 700°C and before the ball milling and with 15 wt% excess LiOH. The other one is referred to 15Li LiOH, which is a mixture of 15Li and 6 wt% LiOH. This mixture was jar milled for 12 hours before the use in sintering. The notches in the crucible were found to be necessary (Figure S1 in Supporting Information) to allow an optimal amount of gas exchange between inside of the crucible and surrounding atmosphere to establish the local gas atmosphere to promote the sintering behavior."}, {"line": 41, "text": ""}, {"line": 42, "text": "Samples with excess Li added in two different steps were fabricated in this work. One group of samples has the excess LiOH added only at the 1st step of the co-precipitation process. The other group has the excess LiOH added in two steps, the first is during the coprecipitation process and the second is the ball milling process. No significant differences in the phase content, morphology and conductivity were observed between adding the Li in one or two steps (Figure S2-S5 and Table S2 in Supporting Information), so results for only one case will be presented. In this work, 25Li and 75Li samples have the excess Li added in one step, while 30Li and 65Li samples all have the excess Li added in two different steps (details of the amount of excess LiOH added during different steps are shown in Table S1 in Supporting Information)."}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 40, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 421, "start": 409}, {"end": 448, "start": 436}], "text": "During sintering the pellets were covered with powders to reduce the loss of lithium. Four columns, with four pellets in each column, of samples were sintered at one time. Only the middle two pellets were used as the bottom pellet was in contact with the crucible and the top pellet exhibited different sintering behavior, presumably because the atmosphere was different as it was closer to the air above the cover powder. Two kinds of cover powders were used. One is referred to 15Li, which is the powder after the heat-treatment of 700°C and before the ball milling and with 15 wt% excess LiOH. The other one is referred to 15Li LiOH, which is a mixture of 15Li and 6 wt% LiOH. This mixture was jar milled for 12 hours before the use in sintering. The notches in the crucible were found to be necessary (Figure S1 in Supporting Information) to allow an optimal amount of gas exchange between inside of the crucible and surrounding atmosphere to establish the local gas atmosphere to promote the sintering behavior."}, {"context": [{"line": 46, "text": "# Results and Discussion"}, {"line": 47, "text": ""}, {"line": 48, "text": "Phase stabilization.—The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with the same cover powder of 15Li are shown in Figure 2a. For 25Li sample, with 25 wt% excess LiOH, La2Zr2O7 (pyrochlore) phase was observed in addition to the cubic garnet phase, presumably because of the loss of lithium during sintering. When the excess LiOH was increased to 30 wt% or larger, a pure cubic garnet phase was obtained. Even when the excess LiOH was increased to 65 and 75 wt%, no peaks related to LiOH were observed for any of the samples, indicating that there was no LiOH left or the remaining amount of LiOH was too small to be detected by XRD."}, {"line": 49, "text": ""}, {"line": 50, "text": "The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with 15Li  LiOH are shown in Figure 2b. All spectra contained peaks for only the cubic garnet phase. The LiOH in the cover powder also helped with the phase stabilization, as 25Li showed the pure cubic garnet phase with the cover of 15Li  LiOH but contained pyrochlore phase with the cover of 15Li."}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 48, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 124, "start": 112}], "text": "Phase stabilization.—The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with the same cover powder of 15Li are shown in Figure 2a. For 25Li sample, with 25 wt% excess LiOH, La2Zr2O7 (pyrochlore) phase was observed in addition to the cubic garnet phase, presumably because of the loss of lithium during sintering. When the excess LiOH was increased to 30 wt% or larger, a pure cubic garnet phase was obtained. Even when the excess LiOH was increased to 65 and 75 wt%, no peaks related to LiOH were observed for any of the samples, indicating that there was no LiOH left or the remaining amount of LiOH was too small to be detected by XRD."}, {"context": [{"line": 48, "text": "Phase stabilization.—The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with the same cover powder of 15Li are shown in Figure 2a. For 25Li sample, with 25 wt% excess LiOH, La2Zr2O7 (pyrochlore) phase was observed in addition to the cubic garnet phase, presumably because of the loss of lithium during sintering. When the excess LiOH was increased to 30 wt% or larger, a pure cubic garnet phase was obtained. Even when the excess LiOH was increased to 65 and 75 wt%, no peaks related to LiOH were observed for any of the samples, indicating that there was no LiOH left or the remaining amount of LiOH was too small to be detected by XRD."}, {"line": 49, "text": ""}, {"line": 50, "text": "The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with 15Li  LiOH are shown in Figure 2b. All spectra contained peaks for only the cubic garnet phase. The LiOH in the cover powder also helped with the phase stabilization, as 25Li showed the pure cubic garnet phase with the cover of 15Li  LiOH but contained pyrochlore phase with the cover of 15Li."}, {"line": 51, "text": ""}, {"line": 52, "text": "Densification of LLZTO.—The densification results of different samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours are summarized in Table II. Shrinkage did not occur with the cover of 15Li, except for 75Li, which showed nonuniform shrinkage. When the cover was changed to 15Li LiOH, shrinkage was observed for all samples, except for 25Li which showed nonuniform shrinkage. 30Li and 65Li samples have relative densities of 58 and 55% with the cover of 15Li, which is different from Rosero-Navarro group’s work that when 12wt% excess LiOH was added into 10Li sample (22Li using the name convention used in this paper), relative density increased from 50% to 77% with the cover of 10Li.54 The relative densities were all increased to 92% and 90% when the cover powder contained LiOH. The densification of pellets sintered with mother powder of high Li amount was also noticed by another research group.48 As noted above, 75Li showed nonuniform shrinkage after the sintering with the 15Li cover, which indicates that sufficient excess LiOH could help with the densification. However, without LiOH in the cover, the shrinkage of 75Li was nonuniform. Subsequent studies focused on samples with 30 or 65 wt% LiOH as these samples all uniformly shrank after the sintering with the cover of 15Li LiOH."}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 50, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 204, "start": 192}], "text": "The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with 15Li  LiOH are shown in Figure 2b. All spectra contained peaks for only the cubic garnet phase. The LiOH in the cover powder also helped with the phase stabilization, as 25Li showed the pure cubic garnet phase with the cover of 15Li  LiOH but contained pyrochlore phase with the cover of 15Li."}, {"context": [{"line": 50, "text": "The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with 15Li  LiOH are shown in Figure 2b. All spectra contained peaks for only the cubic garnet phase. The LiOH in the cover powder also helped with the phase stabilization, as 25Li showed the pure cubic garnet phase with the cover of 15Li  LiOH but contained pyrochlore phase with the cover of 15Li."}, {"line": 51, "text": ""}, {"line": 52, "text": "Densification of LLZTO.—The densification results of different samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours are summarized in Table II. Shrinkage did not occur with the cover of 15Li, except for 75Li, which showed nonuniform shrinkage. When the cover was changed to 15Li LiOH, shrinkage was observed for all samples, except for 25Li which showed nonuniform shrinkage. 30Li and 65Li samples have relative densities of 58 and 55% with the cover of 15Li, which is different from Rosero-Navarro group’s work that when 12wt% excess LiOH was added into 10Li sample (22Li using the name convention used in this paper), relative density increased from 50% to 77% with the cover of 10Li.54 The relative densities were all increased to 92% and 90% when the cover powder contained LiOH. The densification of pellets sintered with mother powder of high Li amount was also noticed by another research group.48 As noted above, 75Li showed nonuniform shrinkage after the sintering with the 15Li cover, which indicates that sufficient excess LiOH could help with the densification. However, without LiOH in the cover, the shrinkage of 75Li was nonuniform. Subsequent studies focused on samples with 30 or 65 wt% LiOH as these samples all uniformly shrank after the sintering with the cover of 15Li LiOH."}, {"line": 53, "text": ""}, {"line": 54, "text": "![](images/3494211a0e6a84e5a8ec60ec19b244d091b1fb542e5d0779b19ca70a00542c3f.jpg)  "}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 52, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 759, "start": 747}], "text": "Densification of LLZTO.—The densification results of different samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours are summarized in Table II. Shrinkage did not occur with the cover of 15Li, except for 75Li, which showed nonuniform shrinkage. When the cover was changed to 15Li LiOH, shrinkage was observed for all samples, except for 25Li which showed nonuniform shrinkage. 30Li and 65Li samples have relative densities of 58 and 55% with the cover of 15Li, which is different from Rosero-Navarro group’s work that when 12wt% excess LiOH was added into 10Li sample (22Li using the name convention used in this paper), relative density increased from 50% to 77% with the cover of 10Li.54 The relative densities were all increased to 92% and 90% when the cover powder contained LiOH. The densification of pellets sintered with mother powder of high Li amount was also noticed by another research group.48 As noted above, 75Li showed nonuniform shrinkage after the sintering with the 15Li cover, which indicates that sufficient excess LiOH could help with the densification. However, without LiOH in the cover, the shrinkage of 75Li was nonuniform. Subsequent studies focused on samples with 30 or 65 wt% LiOH as these samples all uniformly shrank after the sintering with the cover of 15Li LiOH."}, {"context": [{"line": 53, "text": ""}, {"line": 54, "text": "![](images/3494211a0e6a84e5a8ec60ec19b244d091b1fb542e5d0779b19ca70a00542c3f.jpg)  "}, {"line": 55, "text": "Figure 2. XRD patterns of different LLZTO pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours (a) with cover powder of 15Li, (b) with cover powder of 15Li + LiOH.   "}, {"line": 56, "text": "wnloaded on 2019-11-14 to IP 5.189.201.153 address. Redistribution subject to ECS terms of use (see ecsdl.org/site/terms_use) unless CC License in place (see abstract)"}, {"line": 57, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 55, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 104, "start": 92}, {"end": 135, "start": 123}], "text": "Figure 2. XRD patterns of different LLZTO pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours (a) with cover powder of 15Li, (b) with cover powder of 15Li + LiOH.   "}, {"context": [{"line": 58, "text": "![](images/05b107c8f2e267fb4e276a0a6dbc046b24059fc0db5a8e530c5c9f08bdbe8cab.jpg)"}, {"line": 59, "text": ""}, {"line": 60, "text": "A small amount of LiOH in the cover powder (3 wt% LiOH) led to nonuniform shrinkage, while with a cover powders containing 100 wt% LiOH all pellets melted and stuck to the crucible. The LiOH + 15Li mixture (6:94 by weight) cover promoted uniform sintering of pellets and resulted in desirable lithium ion conductivity from LLZTO."}, {"line": 61, "text": ""}, {"line": 62, "text": "SEM images of fracture cross sections of 30Li and 65Li are shown in Figure 3. After sintering with 15Li cover powder, large pores were observed in Figures 3a–3d. When the cover powder contained LiOH, fewer pores were observed as shown in Figures 3e–3h. The fracture surface of the pellets sintered with 15Li cover powder consisted of smooth rounded surfaces (Figures 3b–3d), but in the sample with 15Li LiOH cover powder, flat surface indicative of brittle or intergranular facture surface were observed (Figures 3f–3h) indicating that the grains are more connected when LiOH was added in the cover powder. These results indicate that LiOH in the cover powder helped with the sintering of LLZTO. An interparticle phase between grains was observed in Figures 3f–3h, which is circled in the figures and found from EDS analysis to contain aluminum as shown in Figure 4."}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 60, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 42, "start": 30}, {"end": 110, "start": 98}], "text": "A small amount of LiOH in the cover powder (3 wt% LiOH) led to nonuniform shrinkage, while with a cover powders containing 100 wt% LiOH all pellets melted and stuck to the crucible. The LiOH + 15Li mixture (6:94 by weight) cover promoted uniform sintering of pellets and resulted in desirable lithium ion conductivity from LLZTO."}, {"context": [{"line": 60, "text": "A small amount of LiOH in the cover powder (3 wt% LiOH) led to nonuniform shrinkage, while with a cover powders containing 100 wt% LiOH all pellets melted and stuck to the crucible. The LiOH + 15Li mixture (6:94 by weight) cover promoted uniform sintering of pellets and resulted in desirable lithium ion conductivity from LLZTO."}, {"line": 61, "text": ""}, {"line": 62, "text": "SEM images of fracture cross sections of 30Li and 65Li are shown in Figure 3. After sintering with 15Li cover powder, large pores were observed in Figures 3a–3d. When the cover powder contained LiOH, fewer pores were observed as shown in Figures 3e–3h. The fracture surface of the pellets sintered with 15Li cover powder consisted of smooth rounded surfaces (Figures 3b–3d), but in the sample with 15Li LiOH cover powder, flat surface indicative of brittle or intergranular facture surface were observed (Figures 3f–3h) indicating that the grains are more connected when LiOH was added in the cover powder. These results indicate that LiOH in the cover powder helped with the sintering of LLZTO. An interparticle phase between grains was observed in Figures 3f–3h, which is circled in the figures and found from EDS analysis to contain aluminum as shown in Figure 4."}, {"line": 63, "text": ""}, {"line": 64, "text": "Room temperature impedance spectra for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with cover powders of 15Li or 15Li LiOH are shown in Figure 5. As shown in the typical Nyquist plot for LLZTO pellets in Figure 5, the spectra were fit to an equivalent circuit of L(R CPE )(R CPE ), where L, R and CPE are the inductor, resistor and the constant phase element, and the subscript t and e refer to the total and electrode contribution, respectively. The inductive contribution is attributed to the wires connected to the sample and is not used for the sample properties. The first semicircle is attributed to the total resistance (Rt). The total conductivity is calculated as σtotal t / (A × Rt), where t is the sample thickness and A is the cross-sectional area of the sample. The grain and grain boundary contributions overlapped with each other and cannot be separated, which is consistent with other works.53,56,57 The RT total Li+ conductivity for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with 15Li or 15Li LiOH cover powder are tabulated in Table II. The RT total conductivity of 30Li with 15Li cover powders is 2.0 × 10−5 S/cm. It is significantly lower than that sintered with 15Li + LiOH, which is 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm. Compared to the 30 wt% LiOH samples, samples with higher lithium content (65Li) showed a lower RT σtotal when the cover was 15Li, but similar RT σtotal when the cover contained LiOH. 75Li also showed a similar RT σtotal with 30wt% and 65 wt% LiOH samples when the cover contained LiOH, which indicates that the amount of Li content in the pellets does not affect RT σtotal once it is over 30 wt%. The higher conductivity is consistent with the higher relative density in the samples sintered with 15Li  LiOH cover."}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 62, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 116, "start": 104}, {"end": 185, "start": 173}, {"end": 324, "start": 312}, {"end": 426, "start": 414}, {"end": 613, "start": 601}, {"end": 667, "start": 655}], "text": "SEM images of fracture cross sections of 30Li and 65Li are shown in Figure 3. After sintering with 15Li cover powder, large pores were observed in Figures 3a–3d. When the cover powder contained LiOH, fewer pores were observed as shown in Figures 3e–3h. The fracture surface of the pellets sintered with 15Li cover powder consisted of smooth rounded surfaces (Figures 3b–3d), but in the sample with 15Li LiOH cover powder, flat surface indicative of brittle or intergranular facture surface were observed (Figures 3f–3h) indicating that the grains are more connected when LiOH was added in the cover powder. These results indicate that LiOH in the cover powder helped with the sintering of LLZTO. An interparticle phase between grains was observed in Figures 3f–3h, which is circled in the figures and found from EDS analysis to contain aluminum as shown in Figure 4."}], "ok": true, "query": "cover powder", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 12, "truncated": true}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:11:09.411102+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:27:{"duration_ms": 45031.987, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 3, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 30, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO).*(crucible|Al contamination|aluminum contamination)", "regex": true, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 32, "text": "LLZTO synthesis.—Tantalum-doped Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) was prepared by the co-precipitation method. LiOH (Alfa, anhydrous, 98%), La(OH)3 (Alfa, 99.95% REO), ZrOCl2·8H2O (Alfa, 98%), Ta2O5 (Alfa, 99%, metal basis), nitric acid (Alfa, 67–70%) and NH4OH (Alfa, 5.0N) were used as starting materials. Different amounts of excess LiOH were added during different steps to compensate for the loss of lithium during sintering. All samples in this paper are named as xLi, where x refers to the total amount of excess LiOH. Transparent solutions of LiOH and La(OH)3 in nitric acid, and ZrOCl2·8H2O in deionized water were prepared first. Before mixing these two solutions, Ta2O5 powders were added into the nitric acid solution. After mixing, NH4OH was added to the mixture to obtain precipitates. The precipitates were dried at 80°C for 48 hours and then heated at 700°C under flowing air for 2 hours. The dried powders or the dried powders with additional LiOH were ball milled with zirconia balls in a planetary ball milling machine (Across International, PQN2 gear-drive planetary ball mill) at 500 rpm for 4 hours with 2-propanol as the dispersing reagent."}, {"line": 33, "text": ""}, {"line": 34, "text": "![](images/713c09405f6ccfdb6adb8c54cf7b2fe55e11d21d597f5b7b2c8c28668993926e.jpg)  "}, {"line": 35, "text": "Figure 1. (a) Flow chart for the preparation of LLZTO by a co-precipitation method. (b) Image of the modified alumina crucible (54mm length × 38mm width 20 mm height) with four notches (1 mm width 3 mm depth).   "}, {"line": 36, "text": "wnloaded on 2019-11-14 to IP 5.189.201.153 address. Redistribution subject to ECS terms of use (see ecsdl.org/site/terms_use) unless CC License in place (see abstract"}, {"line": 37, "text": ""}, {"line": 38, "text": "After drying at 80°C for 24 hours, the powders were cold pressed into pellets (10 mm in diameter and 1–3 mm in thickness) at a pressure of 200 MPa. The pellets were covered with cover powders, placed in a modified alumina crucible (shown in Figure 1b), with an alumina lid and sintered in air with a heating rate of 1°C/min to 1100°C for 1–32 hours. The detailed procedure for the preparation of LLZTO is shown in Figure 1a."}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 35, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 126, "start": 48}], "text": "Figure 1. (a) Flow chart for the preparation of LLZTO by a co-precipitation method. (b) Image of the modified alumina crucible (54mm length × 38mm width 20 mm height) with four notches (1 mm width 3 mm depth).   "}, {"context": [{"line": 90, "text": "Figure 5. Electrochemical properties of LLZTO pellet sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours, RT impedance spectra, (a) with 15Li cover powder, (b) with 15Li + LiOH cover powder. Data were fitted using the presented equivalent circuit of L(RtCPEt)(ReCPEe).   "}, {"line": 91, "text": "wnloaded on 2019-11-14 to IP 5.189.201.153 address. Redistribution subject to ECS terms of use (see ecsdl.org/site/terms_use) unless CC License in place (see abstrac"}, {"line": 92, "text": ""}, {"line": 93, "text": "Tantalum appears to be relatively uniformly distributed in the sample. Compositions from ICP analysis of 65Li sintered with different cover powders and different sintering times are listed in Table IV, atomic ratio of Li:Al:La:Zr is normalized by La content as shown in the formula of Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12. The ICP analysis, which presents the overall sample composition, indicates that Al exists in all LLZTO samples, but that the amount of aluminum is higher and increases with increasing time when sintering with a 15Li LiOH cover. In addition, the Li content is lower in the sample sintered with 15Li cover powder than that with 15Li + LiOH cover. These results suggest that the 15Li + LiOH cover powder reduces the loss of Li and promotes the transfer of aluminum from the crucible to the sample."}, {"line": 94, "text": ""}, {"line": 95, "text": "The RT total Li+ conductivities of 65Li pellets sintered for 4 to 32 hours are shown in Figure 9. The conductivity increases significantly after 4 hours sintering, and it does not change significantly between 8 and 32 hours. The relative densities of these pellets shown in Table V follows a similar trend to that of the total Li+ conductivities. The relative density is only 61% for pellets sintered for 4 hours, and it is larger than 80% for 8 to 32 hours. Samples sintered for 16 and 32 hours have similar relative density of 90%. As shown in Table IV, the amount of aluminum increases with increasing sintering time when sintering with a 15Li LiOH cover, and aluminum only exists at the phases between grains (Figure 8), it is concluded that the amount of Al-containing phase in the dense pellets also increases with the sintering time. The increased amount of Al-containing phase may be one reason that the conductivity does not increase as much as might be expected from the relative density change from 83 to 90%."}, {"line": 96, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 93, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 790, "start": 408}], "text": "Tantalum appears to be relatively uniformly distributed in the sample. Compositions from ICP analysis of 65Li sintered with different cover powders and different sintering times are listed in Table IV, atomic ratio of Li:Al:La:Zr is normalized by La content as shown in the formula of Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12. The ICP analysis, which presents the overall sample composition, indicates that Al exists in all LLZTO samples, but that the amount of aluminum is higher and increases with increasing time when sintering with a 15Li LiOH cover. In addition, the Li content is lower in the sample sintered with 15Li cover powder than that with 15Li + LiOH cover. These results suggest that the 15Li + LiOH cover powder reduces the loss of Li and promotes the transfer of aluminum from the crucible to the sample."}, {"context": [{"line": 110, "text": ""}, {"line": 111, "text": "# Conclusions"}, {"line": 112, "text": ""}, {"line": 113, "text": "The addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder used to minimize vaporization from the LLZTO pellet during the sintering has been shown to improve densification and increase Li+ conductivity, such that a relative density of 92% and a total Li+ conductivity of 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm was achieved through a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in air. The 15Li LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary. The increased aluminum content promoted phase stabilization and densification, but the Al-containing phase also limits LLZTO phase continuity. The Li+ conductivity value reached 3.5 10−4 S/cm at a sintering time of 8 hours and did not change significantly between 8 and 32 hours. Further study is needed to determine the role of aluminum and chlorine on the densification and conductivity of LLZTO by using a zirconium salt that does not contain chlorine, using different levels of aluminum by adding Al salt in the co-precipitation process, and sintering with aluminum-free crucibles."}, {"line": 114, "text": ""}, {"line": 115, "text": "# Acknowledgment"}, {"line": 116, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 113, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1198, "start": 28}], "text": "The addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder used to minimize vaporization from the LLZTO pellet during the sintering has been shown to improve densification and increase Li+ conductivity, such that a relative density of 92% and a total Li+ conductivity of 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm was achieved through a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in air. The 15Li LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary. The increased aluminum content promoted phase stabilization and densification, but the Al-containing phase also limits LLZTO phase continuity. The Li+ conductivity value reached 3.5 10−4 S/cm at a sintering time of 8 hours and did not change significantly between 8 and 32 hours. Further study is needed to determine the role of aluminum and chlorine on the densification and conductivity of LLZTO by using a zirconium salt that does not contain chlorine, using different levels of aluminum by adding Al salt in the co-precipitation process, and sintering with aluminum-free crucibles."}, {"context": [{"line": 31, "text": ""}, {"line": 32, "text": "# Abstract:"}, {"line": 33, "text": ""}, {"line": 34, "text": "Ta-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 (Ta-LLZO) is considered as a promising solid electrolyte due to high Li-ion conductivity and good chemical stability against electrode materials. In this work, Ta-LLZO was prepared by a conventional solid-state reaction. Ultrafine powders were obtained by ball-milling to improve the surface activity. Ta-LLZO is sintered in ZrO2 crucibles to avoid introducing Al into the samples. The particle size distribution, phase structure, morphology, ionic conductivity, electronic conductivity, density and electrochemical performance of semi-solid battery were characterized by laser diffraction particle size analyzer, X-ray diffraction, scanning electron microscope, AC-impedance, DC polarization, Archimedes method and a battery testing system, respectively. The results show that the ball milling to reduce the particle size is an effective way to solve the problem of relatively low density and Li-ion conductivity for Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12. For Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12, the increase of x (0.2  x  0.4) promotes the grain growth and sintering densification, but the increase of x (0.4 < x  0.6) has an adverse effect. Li6.7La3Zr1.7Ta0.3O12 sintered at 1180 °C for 12 h shows the relative density of 92 % and the highest Li-ion conductivity of 1.03×10-3 S/cm at 30 °C with the activation energy of about 0.37 eV, while Li6.6La3Zr1.6Ta0.4O12 sintered at 1180 °C for 12 h shows the highest relative density of 96 % and the Li-ion conductivity of 6.68×10-4 S/cm at 30 °C with the activation energy of about 0.46 eV. The electronic conductivity of Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 is 10-9 S/cm orders of magnitude. The semi-solid battery shows the first discharge capacity of 104.6 mAh/g and 92.5% capacity retention after 20 cycles."}, {"line": 35, "text": ""}, {"line": 36, "text": "Keywords: Al-free; Ta-LLZO; relative density; Li-ion conductivity; ultrafine powders semi-solid battery"}, {"line": 37, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ High densification and Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-La3Zr2-Ta O12 garnet solid electrolyte p", "line": 34, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> High densification and Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-La3Zr2-Ta O12 garnet solid electrolyte p.md", "submatches": [{"end": 360, "start": 26}], "text": "Ta-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 (Ta-LLZO) is considered as a promising solid electrolyte due to high Li-ion conductivity and good chemical stability against electrode materials. In this work, Ta-LLZO was prepared by a conventional solid-state reaction. Ultrafine powders were obtained by ball-milling to improve the surface activity. Ta-LLZO is sintered in ZrO2 crucibles to avoid introducing Al into the samples. The particle size distribution, phase structure, morphology, ionic conductivity, electronic conductivity, density and electrochemical performance of semi-solid battery were characterized by laser diffraction particle size analyzer, X-ray diffraction, scanning electron microscope, AC-impedance, DC polarization, Archimedes method and a battery testing system, respectively. The results show that the ball milling to reduce the particle size is an effective way to solve the problem of relatively low density and Li-ion conductivity for Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12. For Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12, the increase of x (0.2  x  0.4) promotes the grain growth and sintering densification, but the increase of x (0.4 < x  0.6) has an adverse effect. Li6.7La3Zr1.7Ta0.3O12 sintered at 1180 °C for 12 h shows the relative density of 92 % and the highest Li-ion conductivity of 1.03×10-3 S/cm at 30 °C with the activation energy of about 0.37 eV, while Li6.6La3Zr1.6Ta0.4O12 sintered at 1180 °C for 12 h shows the highest relative density of 96 % and the Li-ion conductivity of 6.68×10-4 S/cm at 30 °C with the activation energy of about 0.46 eV. The electronic conductivity of Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 is 10-9 S/cm orders of magnitude. The semi-solid battery shows the first discharge capacity of 104.6 mAh/g and 92.5% capacity retention after 20 cycles."}, {"context": [{"line": 43, "text": ""}, {"line": 44, "text": "partly occupies 24 d site, which hinds the Li migration, and decreases the Li-ion conductivity [6]. Ga-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 that possesses the high Li-ion conductivity is not considered as a promising solid electrolyte material due to the low mechanical strength [7, 8]. Although Fe-doped LLZO shows Li-ion conductivity as high as 1.38 × 10-3 S/cm [9], the electronic conductivity increases which is harmful to all-solid-state batteries. Furthermore, Fe3+ is easily reduced to Fe when it contacts with lithium metal. Ta [10, 19-26], Nb [10, 11], Te [12], W [13, 14], Sb [15], and Mo [16-18] partial substituted Zr site was reported. Substitution on the Zr site with Ta has attracted great interest. Relative density, an important indicator of ceramic material, determines the level of conductivity. Many special sintering methods such as hot-pressing [19], spark plasma sintering (SPS) [28], oxygen atmosphere [7], and microwave assist [31] have been developed to enhance the relative density. Allen et al. reported that Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 sintered at 1050 °C for 1 h by hot-pressing showed the Li-ion conductivity of 8.7×10-4 S/cm at 25 °C with the relative density of 96 % [19]. Thompson et al. reported that Li6.5La3Zr1.5Ta0.5O12 sintered at 1050 °C for 1 h by hot-pressing showed the Li-ion conductivity of 8.16×10-4 S/cm at 25 °C with the relative density of 97.8 % [27]. Yamada et al. reported that Li6.5La3Zr1.5Ta0.5O12 sintered at 1000 °C for 10 min by SPS showed the Li-ion conductivity of 6.9×10-4 S/cm at 25 °C with the relative density of 95.5 % [28]. Adding sintering aid is also an effective way to improve the density. Cao et al. reported that Li6.5La3Zr1.5Ta0.5O12 with 1 wt% Li3PO4 sintered at 1175 °C for 6 h showed the Li-ion conductivity of 7.2×10-4 S/cm at 25 °C with the relative density of 92.7 % [21]. Janani et al. reported that Li6.5La3Zr1.5Ta0.5O12 with 1 wt% Li4SiO4 sintered at 1200 °C for 24 h showed the"}, {"line": 45, "text": ""}, {"line": 46, "text": "Li-ion conductivity of 3.7×10-4 S/cm at 25 °C with the relative density of 94 % [26]. Tang et al. reported that Li6.5La3Zr1.5Ta0.5O12 with 1.4 wt% B2O3 sintered at 1100 °C for 6 h showed the Li-ion conductivity of 1.86×10-4 S/cm at 25 °C with the relative density of 92.4 % [29]. Ta-LLZO is usually sintered in Al2O3 crucibles, so Al is easily introduced into the sample, which form amorphous phase LiAlO2 at grain boundaries to promote sintering densification process [37-38]. But the amount of amorphous phase LiAlO2 is hard to control.  Literatures have reported Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 with relatively low density and Li-ion conductivity [35]. The reason is that Al is not introduced into the sample. Using ultrafine powders can promote sintering densification process, which may be an effect way to solve the problem of relatively low density for Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 and now has not been reported."}, {"line": 47, "text": ""}, {"line": 48, "text": "In this work, ultrafine powders of Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 were prepared by ball-milling to improve the surface activity. The particle size distributions of Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 powders were characterized by laser diffraction particle size analyzer. To avoid introducing Al into the samples, we adopted ZrO2 crucibles. The influences of Ta contents (x = 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.5, and 0.6) on the relative density and Li-ion conductivity were investigated. In order to test applicability of Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 as solid electrolyte in Li-ion batteries, we test the electrochemical performance of semi-solid battery."}, {"line": 49, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ High densification and Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-La3Zr2-Ta O12 garnet solid electrolyte p", "line": 46, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> High densification and Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-La3Zr2-Ta O12 garnet solid electrolyte p.md", "submatches": [{"end": 330, "start": 288}], "text": "Li-ion conductivity of 3.7×10-4 S/cm at 25 °C with the relative density of 94 % [26]. Tang et al. reported that Li6.5La3Zr1.5Ta0.5O12 with 1.4 wt% B2O3 sintered at 1100 °C for 6 h showed the Li-ion conductivity of 1.86×10-4 S/cm at 25 °C with the relative density of 92.4 % [29]. Ta-LLZO is usually sintered in Al2O3 crucibles, so Al is easily introduced into the sample, which form amorphous phase LiAlO2 at grain boundaries to promote sintering densification process [37-38]. But the amount of amorphous phase LiAlO2 is hard to control.  Literatures have reported Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 with relatively low density and Li-ion conductivity [35]. The reason is that Al is not introduced into the sample. Using ultrafine powders can promote sintering densification process, which may be an effect way to solve the problem of relatively low density for Al-free Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 and now has not been reported."}, {"context": [{"line": 22, "text": ""}, {"line": 23, "text": "For the solid-state processing, the starting source materials used for the powder mixture were Lithium carbonate (Li CO , 99.0%, Sigma Aldrich), Lanthanum(III) hydroxide (La(OH) , 99.9%, Sigma Aldrich), Zirconium(IV) oxide (ZrO , 99%, Sigma Aldrich), and Aluminum oxide (Al O , 20 wt% in isopropanol, Sigma Aldrich). The comDelivered by Ingenta to:position of the mixture powder followed the mole ratio IP: 95.85.80.74 On: Mformula of Li Al La Zr O for which the excess Li addition x was varied from 0 to 1.24 mol. (20 mol% excess) and the Al doping y was varied from 0.3 to 0.5 mol. The Al element was added to stabilize the cubic phase at room temperature. Before ball-milling, the powder material was dried at 100 -C to minimize the effect of moisture. The powder weighed according to the stoichiometric ratio was ball-milled at a rotation speed of 250 rpm for 24 h. Pellet-disk type specimens with a diameter of 20 mm were prepared with oil-hydraulic pressing under 10 MPa. After the pressing process, the specimens were calcined at various temperatures ranging from 700 to 1000 -C in a box furnace for 4 h. After the calcination, they were finally crushed into the powder state with a mortar followed by a ball miller."}, {"line": 24, "text": ""}, {"line": 25, "text": "To prepare the sol–gel synthesized LLZO powder, Lithium nitrate hydrate (LiNO xH O, 99.999%, Alfa Aesar), Lanthanum nitrate hydrate (La(NO \u0003 6H O, 99.9%, Sigma Aldrich), and Zirconium(IV) Propoxide (Zr(OC H \u0003 , 70 wt% in 1-Propanol, Sigma Aldrich) were used as gel precursors. First, 1-propanol (anhydrous 99.7%, Sigma Aldrich) used as a diluting solvent was mixed and stirred with LiNO xH O and La(NO \u0003 6H O. Zr(OC H \u0003 was separately mixed and stirred with acetic acid (1.0 M CH COOH, Fluka) used as a chelating agent and 2-methoxyethanol (2-MOE, CH OCH CH OH, anhydrous 99.8%, Sigma Aldrich) solvent to prevent reactions with moisture. An aluminum element was added as cientific Publishersaluminum oxide (Al O , 20 wt% in isopropanol, Sigma Aldrich) particles with a diameter of 50 nm to the precursor solution before gelation occurs. The precursor solutions were then mixed along with the addition of the aluminum oxide powders. Gelation occurred in approximately 45 min., which was followed by 12 h of ageing to ensure that the reaction was completed. The gel was subsequently dried for 9 h followed by pre-calcination heat treatment at 450 -C in air to remove organics.21 Similar to the solid-state processing, the dried powder was cold pressed into pellets with a diameter of 20 mm using an oil-hydraulic pressing at 10 MPa, and they were sintered at various temperatures ranging from 700 to 1000 -C in a box furnace for 4 h. The detailed experimental procedures for the sol–gel synthesis are summarized in Figure 1. Alumina crucibles were used for the calcination. After the calcination, they were finally crushed into a powder state with a mortar followed by ball milling."}, {"line": 26, "text": ""}, {"line": 27, "text": "![](images/aa1f3570bcd7aab01b672129bf24a9d566a4d428a97d983fa4909e674d83ab7f.jpg)  "}, {"line": 28, "text": "Figure 1. Process flow diagram for the sol–gel synthesis of the LLZO powder.   "}], "document_id": "_ _ 10.1166_jnn.2016.13570", "line": 25, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> _ 10.1166_jnn.2016.13570.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1543, "start": 37}], "text": "To prepare the sol–gel synthesized LLZO powder, Lithium nitrate hydrate (LiNO xH O, 99.999%, Alfa Aesar), Lanthanum nitrate hydrate (La(NO \u0003 6H O, 99.9%, Sigma Aldrich), and Zirconium(IV) Propoxide (Zr(OC H \u0003 , 70 wt% in 1-Propanol, Sigma Aldrich) were used as gel precursors. First, 1-propanol (anhydrous 99.7%, Sigma Aldrich) used as a diluting solvent was mixed and stirred with LiNO xH O and La(NO \u0003 6H O. Zr(OC H \u0003 was separately mixed and stirred with acetic acid (1.0 M CH COOH, Fluka) used as a chelating agent and 2-methoxyethanol (2-MOE, CH OCH CH OH, anhydrous 99.8%, Sigma Aldrich) solvent to prevent reactions with moisture. An aluminum element was added as cientific Publishersaluminum oxide (Al O , 20 wt% in isopropanol, Sigma Aldrich) particles with a diameter of 50 nm to the precursor solution before gelation occurs. The precursor solutions were then mixed along with the addition of the aluminum oxide powders. Gelation occurred in approximately 45 min., which was followed by 12 h of ageing to ensure that the reaction was completed. The gel was subsequently dried for 9 h followed by pre-calcination heat treatment at 450 -C in air to remove organics.21 Similar to the solid-state processing, the dried powder was cold pressed into pellets with a diameter of 20 mm using an oil-hydraulic pressing at 10 MPa, and they were sintered at various temperatures ranging from 700 to 1000 -C in a box furnace for 4 h. The detailed experimental procedures for the sol–gel synthesis are summarized in Figure 1. Alumina crucibles were used for the calcination. After the calcination, they were finally crushed into a powder state with a mortar followed by ball milling."}, {"context": [{"line": 43, "text": ""}, {"line": 44, "text": "# 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION"}, {"line": 45, "text": ""}, {"line": 46, "text": "The XRD patterns of Li6.4La3Zr1.4M0.6O12 (M=Sb, Ta, Nb) are exhibited in Fig. 2(a) to identify the phase composition. As presented, the positions and relative intensities of diffraction peaks of all samples are similar and consistent with the Li5La3Nb2O12 (PDF45-0109) 34 standard pattern which possesses garnet phase. Furthermore, no other diffraction peaks appear. This suggests that pure c-LLZO doped with Sb or Ta or Nb have obtained. The peaks of samples are narrow and sharp, indicating well sintering. Fig. 2(b) shows the high-angle magnified XRD patterns. It is obvious that the peak at approximate 52.9o gradually shifts to high-angle with the rise of the radius of doping ions. According to the formula of Bragg: 2dsinθ = λ, where d, 2θ and λ represent interplanar spacing, diffraction angle, and diffraction wave respectively, the larger diffraction angle suggest smaller cell parameter. On the other hand, the Al3+ of these samples cannot be detected by ICP-OES, suggesting these samples are not affected by Al3+ from Al2O3 crucible."}, {"line": 47, "text": ""}, {"line": 48, "text": "This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved"}, {"line": 49, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Dual regulation of Li_br __ _sup_+__sup__br __ migration of Li_br __ _sub__6.4__sub__br __ La_br __ ", "line": 46, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> regulation of Li_br __ _sup_+__sup__br __ migration of Li_br __ _sub__6.4__sub__br __ La_br __ .md", "submatches": [{"end": 1048, "start": 393}], "text": "The XRD patterns of Li6.4La3Zr1.4M0.6O12 (M=Sb, Ta, Nb) are exhibited in Fig. 2(a) to identify the phase composition. As presented, the positions and relative intensities of diffraction peaks of all samples are similar and consistent with the Li5La3Nb2O12 (PDF45-0109) 34 standard pattern which possesses garnet phase. Furthermore, no other diffraction peaks appear. This suggests that pure c-LLZO doped with Sb or Ta or Nb have obtained. The peaks of samples are narrow and sharp, indicating well sintering. Fig. 2(b) shows the high-angle magnified XRD patterns. It is obvious that the peak at approximate 52.9o gradually shifts to high-angle with the rise of the radius of doping ions. According to the formula of Bragg: 2dsinθ = λ, where d, 2θ and λ represent interplanar spacing, diffraction angle, and diffraction wave respectively, the larger diffraction angle suggest smaller cell parameter. On the other hand, the Al3+ of these samples cannot be detected by ICP-OES, suggesting these samples are not affected by Al3+ from Al2O3 crucible."}, {"context": [{"line": 8, "text": ""}, {"line": 9, "text": "The use of solid electrolytes, rather than currently used organic carbonate liquid solutions, can improve battery safety, so solid electrolytes are considered as replacements for these liquid electrolytes. In last few years, garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows the most promising properties, such as high chemical stability and high ionic conductivity of 3×10-4 S∙cm-1 at 25℃ (1)."}, {"line": 10, "text": ""}, {"line": 11, "text": "Garnet-type LLZO can form tetragonal and cubic phases, and the tetragonal phase has a much lower ionic conductivity (around 1.63×10-6 S∙cm-1) (1, 2). Elemental doping, such as Al, W and Ta (3-5) is an effective method to stabilize the cubic phase. Aluminum in LLZO can originate as contamination from alumina crucible used during the high temperature sintering period (7). Different Al molar concentrations will lead to different garnet-type structures. It is reported that the tetragonal LLZO formed at Al molar concentration between 0.074 and 0.1 (8), while cubic LLZO formed at an amount of 0.24-0.39 mole (9-11)."}, {"line": 12, "text": ""}, {"line": 13, "text": "Garnet-type LLZO can be obtained by different synthesis methods, such as solid-state reaction (1, 12-14), and sol-gel techniques (10, 11, 15, 16). The former involves timeconsuming ball milling and sintering steps, while the latter contains only has one sintering step, which could greatly reduce the loss of lithium ions (1). Therefore this paper focuses on sol-gel method for fabrication of Al doped garnet-type LLZO."}, {"line": 14, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ The Fabrication of Garnet-Type Li7La3Zr2O12 Solid Electrolyte Materials _ 10.1149_07207.0133ecst", "line": 11, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> The Fabrication of Garnet-Type Li7La3Zr2O12 Solid Electrolyte Materials _ 10.1149_07207.0133ecst.md", "submatches": [{"end": 320, "start": 12}], "text": "Garnet-type LLZO can form tetragonal and cubic phases, and the tetragonal phase has a much lower ionic conductivity (around 1.63×10-6 S∙cm-1) (1, 2). Elemental doping, such as Al, W and Ta (3-5) is an effective method to stabilize the cubic phase. Aluminum in LLZO can originate as contamination from alumina crucible used during the high temperature sintering period (7). Different Al molar concentrations will lead to different garnet-type structures. It is reported that the tetragonal LLZO formed at Al molar concentration between 0.074 and 0.1 (8), while cubic LLZO formed at an amount of 0.24-0.39 mole (9-11)."}, {"context": [{"line": 16, "text": ""}, {"line": 17, "text": "# Experimental"}, {"line": 18, "text": ""}, {"line": 19, "text": "Aluminum-doped and tantalum-doped LLZO was prepared by co-precipitation method. Powers of LiOH (Alfa, anhydrous, 98%), La(OH)3 (Alfa, 99.95% REO), ZrOCl2∙8H2O (Alfa, 98%), Ta2O5 (Alfa, 99%, metal basis), and Al2O3 (Johnson Matthey, 99.9+%, metals basis), nitric acid (Alfa, 67-70%), ammonium hydroxide (Alfa, 5.0N) were used as starting materials. Due to evaporation losses of lithium during heat treatment, 15 wt.% excess of LiOH was added. The process flow is shown in Figure 1. LiOH and La(OH)3 were dissolved in HNO3 and ZrOCl2∙8H2O in deionized water, respectively. Both solutions were mixed and subsequently NH4OH was added. The resultant precipitates were filtered and then ambient dried for 48 hours followed by heat treatment at 700°C in air for 2 hours to remove the organic compounds. The dried powders were cold pressed into 12.5 mm diameter pellets. These pellets with additional unpressed powders under and above were sintered in alumina crucibles at temperatures in the range of 1000-1100°C for 4-16 hours in air, using a heating rate of 120°C/h."}, {"line": 20, "text": ""}, {"line": 21, "text": "The samples were analyzed by X-ray diffraction (XRD) using a Bruker D8 diffractometer with Cu X-ray source. The working current and voltage were 40 mA and 40 kV, respectively. The densities of the sintered pellets were measured at room temperature with the Archimedes method using alcohol as the immersion medium. Morphology characterization was carried out using a JEOL JSM-7000F scanning electron microscope (SEM). Conductivity was analyzed by AC impedance spectroscopy using a frequency response analyzer (Solartron 1260, USA) in the frequency range 0.1 Hz-15 MHz with amplitude of 50 mV. The temperature was ramped from 20°C to 290°C in increments of 30°C and 30 minutes was allowed for temperature equilibration after each temperature change. Silver paste was painted on both sides of the pellets and they were cured at room temperature for 30 minutes to remove the organic binders. Impedance plots were fitted using ZView software."}, {"line": 22, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Phase Content and Conductivity of Aluminum- and Tantalum-Doped Garnet-Type Lithium Lanthanum Zirco", "line": 19, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Phase Content and Conductivity of Aluminum- and Tantalum-Doped Garnet-Type Lithium Lanthanum Zirco.md", "submatches": [{"end": 965, "start": 34}], "text": "Aluminum-doped and tantalum-doped LLZO was prepared by co-precipitation method. Powers of LiOH (Alfa, anhydrous, 98%), La(OH)3 (Alfa, 99.95% REO), ZrOCl2∙8H2O (Alfa, 98%), Ta2O5 (Alfa, 99%, metal basis), and Al2O3 (Johnson Matthey, 99.9+%, metals basis), nitric acid (Alfa, 67-70%), ammonium hydroxide (Alfa, 5.0N) were used as starting materials. Due to evaporation losses of lithium during heat treatment, 15 wt.% excess of LiOH was added. The process flow is shown in Figure 1. LiOH and La(OH)3 were dissolved in HNO3 and ZrOCl2∙8H2O in deionized water, respectively. Both solutions were mixed and subsequently NH4OH was added. The resultant precipitates were filtered and then ambient dried for 48 hours followed by heat treatment at 700°C in air for 2 hours to remove the organic compounds. The dried powders were cold pressed into 12.5 mm diameter pellets. These pellets with additional unpressed powders under and above were sintered in alumina crucibles at temperatures in the range of 1000-1100°C for 4-16 hours in air, using a heating rate of 120°C/h."}, {"context": [{"line": 62, "text": ""}, {"line": 63, "text": "In theory, different Li-diffusion pathways are possible,30 but calculations and Li nuclear magnetic resonance suggest a mechanism wherein the 24d Li ions are immobile, and Li migrates by hopping between the octahedral sites in garnets with lithium contents 5 and below.23,44–46 Based on ab initio calculations, Xu et al.47 have proposed two potential pathways of Li-ion migration in the cubic garnets (route A and route B) with lithium contents 45 per formula unit. In route A, the Li-ion migrates via the interstice between neighboring octahedral sites, bypassing their common tetrahedral neighbor as shown in Fig. 2b. In route B, the Li-ion moves through the shared triangular faces (‘‘neck’’) of the octahedral and tetrahedral sites. The Li-ion migration in route A is associated with an activation energy of 0.8 eV and is preferred when Li+ content is lower, such as Li5La3Nb2O12, while route B is associated with an activation energy of 0.26 eV and is preferred in highly Li-stuffed garnets such as Li7LaZr2O12. 19 Such a low-energy barrier explains why LLZO exhibits rather high ionic conductivity."}, {"line": 64, "text": ""}, {"line": 65, "text": "In 2009, it was reported by Awaka et al.29 that LLZO also adopts a tetragonal structure with space group I41/acd (no. 142) after sintering at a lower temperature of 980 1C. Percival et al.48 also reported a tetragonal structure for a garnet-type Li7La3Sn2O12 if sintered below 750 1C. Later on, it was shown that the tetragonal LLZO phase is the stable structure at room temperature.49 In general, it was suggested that partial substitution of LLZO by dopants such as Al3+ from the crucible or intentional doping stabilizes the cubic phase. Tetragonal t-LLZO is a slightly distorted cubic garnet-type structure.29 The lattice parameters reported in the literature are a = 13.07–13.12 and c = 12.67–12.72 Å.29,50–55 In the transition from the cubic to the tetragonal phase (tetragonal distortion), the 24d site in the cubic phase is transformed into fully occupied 8a sites and unoccupied 16e sites while the 96h site in the cubic phase is transformed into two 16f and 32g sites.29,37,56,57"}, {"line": 66, "text": ""}, {"line": 67, "text": "The notable differences of t-LLZO from c-LLZO is the ordering of the Li atoms and occupation of lithium sites.58 The tetragonal distortion dramatically affects lithium distribution and lowers the ionic conductivity by two orders of magnitude at room temperature as shown in Fig. 3.28,29,35,39,40,50–55,57,59,60 The lower lithium-ion conductivity in t-LLZO (bulk conductivity B 1 \u0003 10-6 S cm-1 )29 vs. c-LLZO (bulk conductivity B 1 \u0003 10-4 S cm-1 ) 28 has been explained by Awaka et al.29 as suppressed hopping between Li sites by the complete tetrahedral 8a–vacancy ordering, and the decrease in equivalent Li positions in the tetragonal phase compared to the cubic garnet-related phase. Recently, Meier et al.61 have explained the difference in the mechanism of Li-ion conduction in tetragonal and cubic LLZO by first-principles calculations."}, {"line": 68, "text": ""}], "document_id": "A bird's-eye view of Li-stuffed garnet-type Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br _", "line": 65, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> bird's-eye view of Li-stuffed garnet-type Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br _.md", "submatches": [{"end": 490, "start": 48}], "text": "In 2009, it was reported by Awaka et al.29 that LLZO also adopts a tetragonal structure with space group I41/acd (no. 142) after sintering at a lower temperature of 980 1C. Percival et al.48 also reported a tetragonal structure for a garnet-type Li7La3Sn2O12 if sintered below 750 1C. Later on, it was shown that the tetragonal LLZO phase is the stable structure at room temperature.49 In general, it was suggested that partial substitution of LLZO by dopants such as Al3+ from the crucible or intentional doping stabilizes the cubic phase. Tetragonal t-LLZO is a slightly distorted cubic garnet-type structure.29 The lattice parameters reported in the literature are a = 13.07–13.12 and c = 12.67–12.72 Å.29,50–55 In the transition from the cubic to the tetragonal phase (tetragonal distortion), the 24d site in the cubic phase is transformed into fully occupied 8a sites and unoccupied 16e sites while the 96h site in the cubic phase is transformed into two 16f and 32g sites.29,37,56,57"}, {"context": [{"line": 69, "text": "The study found that the motion of Li ions in t-LLZO is entirely collective or synchronous which can be interpreted as a consequence of the ordering within the Li-ion sublattice and the lack of available vacancies. In c-LLZO, the mechanism is an asynchronous type dominated by single-ion jumps and induced collective motion (Fig. 2b).61 The chance of single-ion jumps that can occur at much lower energetic cost than a fully synchronous collective motion of several ions at the same time (in t-LLZO) is increased due to a large number of vacancies and disorder in the Li-ion sublattice of c-LLZO. t-LLZO has a lower ionic conductivity than c-LLZO because the mechanism of synchronous collective motion requires a higher activation energy.61"}, {"line": 70, "text": ""}, {"line": 71, "text": "![](images/b3ae4c49bc1e24c68b297f46b033dff80170c1a06e3b0ef72984169d407b9e8d.jpg)  "}, {"line": 72, "text": "Fig. 3 Arrhenius plot of the total lithium-ion conductivity of cubic ‘‘Li La Zr O ’’ (c-‘‘LLZO’’, dashed lines) and tetragonal LLZO (t-LLZO, solid lines). Cubic ‘‘LLZO’’ from the work of (a) Murugan et al., 28 (b) Ohta et al., 39 (c) Huang et al.,40 (d) Kaeriyama et al. (1230 1C),35 and (e) Kaeriyama et al. (1200 1C).35 Tetragonal LLZO from the work of (f) Zhao et al.,50 (g) Wolfenstine et al., 51 (h) Inada et al., 52 (i) Il’ina et al. (sol–gel),53 (j) Il’ina et al. (ceramic),53 (k) Rangasamy et al., 54 (l) Awaka et al., 29 and (m) Kokal et al. (sol–gel).55 The compositions may or may not contain traces of aluminum, a common unintentional dopant that originates from alumina crucibles during the high temperature sintering step.59,60"}, {"line": 73, "text": ""}, {"line": 74, "text": "The low Li-ion conducting tetragonal phase can undergo a phase transition to the high ionic conducting cubic phase at higher temperature. However, the specific temperature for the experimentally observed phase transition varies. Adams and Rao reported a tetragonal-to-cubic phase transition at 177 1C for LLZO based on in situ X-ray diffraction (XRD) results32 while Geiger et al., using XRD, reported the transition to occur between 100 and 150 1C.59 Kokal et al.55 showed, using differential scanning calorimetry, that the phase transition temperature is much higher at approximately 700 1C. It is deemed necessary to stabilize the cubic phase at room temperature to take advantage of its high ionic conductivity."}, {"line": 75, "text": ""}], "document_id": "A bird's-eye view of Li-stuffed garnet-type Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br _", "line": 72, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> bird's-eye view of Li-stuffed garnet-type Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br _.md", "submatches": [{"end": 723, "start": 102}], "text": "Fig. 3 Arrhenius plot of the total lithium-ion conductivity of cubic ‘‘Li La Zr O ’’ (c-‘‘LLZO’’, dashed lines) and tetragonal LLZO (t-LLZO, solid lines). Cubic ‘‘LLZO’’ from the work of (a) Murugan et al., 28 (b) Ohta et al., 39 (c) Huang et al.,40 (d) Kaeriyama et al. (1230 1C),35 and (e) Kaeriyama et al. (1200 1C).35 Tetragonal LLZO from the work of (f) Zhao et al.,50 (g) Wolfenstine et al., 51 (h) Inada et al., 52 (i) Il’ina et al. (sol–gel),53 (j) Il’ina et al. (ceramic),53 (k) Rangasamy et al., 54 (l) Awaka et al., 29 and (m) Kokal et al. (sol–gel).55 The compositions may or may not contain traces of aluminum, a common unintentional dopant that originates from alumina crucibles during the high temperature sintering step.59,60"}, {"context": [{"line": 73, "text": ""}, {"line": 74, "text": "The low Li-ion conducting tetragonal phase can undergo a phase transition to the high ionic conducting cubic phase at higher temperature. However, the specific temperature for the experimentally observed phase transition varies. Adams and Rao reported a tetragonal-to-cubic phase transition at 177 1C for LLZO based on in situ X-ray diffraction (XRD) results32 while Geiger et al., using XRD, reported the transition to occur between 100 and 150 1C.59 Kokal et al.55 showed, using differential scanning calorimetry, that the phase transition temperature is much higher at approximately 700 1C. It is deemed necessary to stabilize the cubic phase at room temperature to take advantage of its high ionic conductivity."}, {"line": 75, "text": ""}, {"line": 76, "text": "A significant amount of work has focused on the inclusion of dopants to stabilize the cubic phase and using various synthesis methods that usually require several calcination steps in the range 750–1230 1C and total synthesis times of over one day.28,36,40,41,59,62–64 A study on the effect of sintering temperature on the structure and ionic conductivity of LLZO found that the cubic structure forms in a temperature range of 1150–1230 1C,40 and that below the sintering temperature of 1150 1C, a tetragonal structure is formed. However, it was not mentioned whether care was taken to avoid aluminum contamination during the preparation. Some were able to retain the cubic phase LLZO at room temperature without the addition of dopants such as Al, but also found that cubic dopant-free LLZO does not exhibit a significant enhancement of ionic conductivity.46,65 Nowadays, doping with ions with ionic radii66 that is close to the host ions is the strategy of choice to promote the stabilization of the cubic LLZO phase at room temperature and to increase ionic conductivity.31,39,58,64,67–78"}, {"line": 77, "text": ""}, {"line": 78, "text": "![](images/83f743285a0c13c2e7c3b7f4d16d90a2ce9ab132161ca807a879fcc49b25c9b7.jpg)"}, {"line": 79, "text": ""}], "document_id": "A bird's-eye view of Li-stuffed garnet-type Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br _", "line": 76, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> bird's-eye view of Li-stuffed garnet-type Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br _.md", "submatches": [{"end": 620, "start": 363}], "text": "A significant amount of work has focused on the inclusion of dopants to stabilize the cubic phase and using various synthesis methods that usually require several calcination steps in the range 750–1230 1C and total synthesis times of over one day.28,36,40,41,59,62–64 A study on the effect of sintering temperature on the structure and ionic conductivity of LLZO found that the cubic structure forms in a temperature range of 1150–1230 1C,40 and that below the sintering temperature of 1150 1C, a tetragonal structure is formed. However, it was not mentioned whether care was taken to avoid aluminum contamination during the preparation. Some were able to retain the cubic phase LLZO at room temperature without the addition of dopants such as Al, but also found that cubic dopant-free LLZO does not exhibit a significant enhancement of ionic conductivity.46,65 Nowadays, doping with ions with ionic radii66 that is close to the host ions is the strategy of choice to promote the stabilization of the cubic LLZO phase at room temperature and to increase ionic conductivity.31,39,58,64,67–78"}, {"context": [{"line": 7, "text": ""}, {"line": 8, "text": "# Abstract"}, {"line": 9, "text": ""}, {"line": 10, "text": "Ga-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 (Ga-LLZO) is a promising solid electrolyte because it shows higher Li-ion conductivity than LLZO doped with other cations. In this work, Ga-LLZO was prepared by a conventional solid-state reaction and sintered in ZrO2 crucibles to avoid introducing Al into the samples. The particle size distribution, phase structure, morphology, ionic conductivity, and bulk density of the samples were characterized by laser diffraction particle size analyzer, X-ray diffraction (XRD), scanning electron microscope (SEM), AC impedance, and Archimedes method, respectively. The effects of sintering temperature and dopant content on Li-ion conductivity and density were investigated. As for Al-free Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12, the Li-ion conductivity and bulk density are improved with the sintering temperature rise. But when the sintering temperature is 1250 °C, the Li-ion conductivity and bulk density decline. As for Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12, the tetragonal phase disappears completely when x ≥ 0.15. The Li-ion conductivity and bulk density of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 are improved with an increase of x (x ≤ 0.2). The ionic conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 sintered in the air by liquid-phase sintering is close to Ga-LLZO sintered in dry O2 atmosphere. Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 sintered at 1200 °C shows the highest Li-ion conductivity of 1.5 × 10−3 S/cm at 30 °C, and the activation energy is about 0.28 eV."}, {"line": 11, "text": ""}, {"line": 12, "text": "Keywords Al-free . Liquid-phase sintering . Solid electrolyte . Li-ion conductivity . Bulk density"}, {"line": 13, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase ", "line": 10, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase .md", "submatches": [{"end": 248, "start": 26}], "text": "Ga-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 (Ga-LLZO) is a promising solid electrolyte because it shows higher Li-ion conductivity than LLZO doped with other cations. In this work, Ga-LLZO was prepared by a conventional solid-state reaction and sintered in ZrO2 crucibles to avoid introducing Al into the samples. The particle size distribution, phase structure, morphology, ionic conductivity, and bulk density of the samples were characterized by laser diffraction particle size analyzer, X-ray diffraction (XRD), scanning electron microscope (SEM), AC impedance, and Archimedes method, respectively. The effects of sintering temperature and dopant content on Li-ion conductivity and density were investigated. As for Al-free Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12, the Li-ion conductivity and bulk density are improved with the sintering temperature rise. But when the sintering temperature is 1250 °C, the Li-ion conductivity and bulk density decline. As for Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12, the tetragonal phase disappears completely when x ≥ 0.15. The Li-ion conductivity and bulk density of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 are improved with an increase of x (x ≤ 0.2). The ionic conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 sintered in the air by liquid-phase sintering is close to Ga-LLZO sintered in dry O2 atmosphere. Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 sintered at 1200 °C shows the highest Li-ion conductivity of 1.5 × 10−3 S/cm at 30 °C, and the activation energy is about 0.28 eV."}, {"context": [{"line": 17, "text": ""}, {"line": 18, "text": "Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has two polymorphs: a tetragonal phase in the space of I41/acd (142) and a cubic phase in the space of Ia-3d (230) [5–7]. The framework structure of tetragonal phase and cubic phase is composed of LaO8 dodecahedron and ZrO6 octahedral. Li atoms are located in three types of crystallographic positions in tetragonal phase: the tetrahedral site (8 a) and distorted octahedral sites (16 f and 32 g) [8, 9]. The tetragonal phase exhibits an extremely poor Li-ion conductivity because the crystalline symmetry is low and the distribution of Li atoms is in order [4]. Li atoms are located in two types of crystallographic positions in cubic phase: the tetrahedral site (24 d) and distorted octahedral site (96 h) [10]. The Li-ion conductivity of tetragonal phase is lower than that of the cubic phase, but the cubic phase is not stabilized, so it needs to be stabilized by partial cationic substitution [11]."}, {"line": 19, "text": ""}, {"line": 20, "text": "Much work has been done on Al-doped LLZO [12–20]. Appropriate Al3+ substitution increases Li+ vacancies, leading to the improvement of Li-ion conductivity and stability of the cubic phase [12–20]. The crystal-chemical behavior is similar between gallium and aluminum on the basis of the periodic table. Ga-doped LLZO has attracted considerable attention [21–24]. The structure of LLZO doped with Ga3+ or Fe3+ in the space group of I-43d was reported [7, 24]. Li atoms occupy three positions: two tetrahedrally coordinated sites (12 a and 12 b), and an octahedrally coordinated site (48 e) [7, 23, 24]. Wagner et al. reported that the bulk Li-ion conductivity of Fe-LLZO sintered at 1230 °C is 1.38 × 10−3 S/cm at room temperature [7]. Shinawi et al. reported that the Li-ion conductivity of Ga-doped LLZO is 5.4 × 10−4 S/cm [21]. Rettenwander et al. reported that the Li-ion conductivity of LLZO doped with Al3+ and Ga3+ is 1.2 × 10−3 S/cm at room temperature [23]. Ernuy-Lopez et al. reported that the Li-ion conductivity of Ga-LLZO sintered in dry O2 atmosphere is 1.3 × 10−3 S/cm at 24 °C [25]. Janani et al. reported that AlLLZO added with 1 wt% Li4SiO4 formed a Li-Al-Si-O liquid phase during the sintering process, and its Li-ion conductivity is 6.1 × 10−4 S/cm at room temperature [15]. Li Yiqiu et al. reported that Li-Al-O amorphous phase is formed at grain boundaries due to the introduction of Al from Al2O3 crucibles, which promotes the densification due to the formation of liquid phase [26–28]. As for Ga-LLZO, the liquid phase of the Li-Ga-O compounds is also formed during the sintering process [28]. Ga-LLZO sintered in Al2O3 crucibles leads to the introduction of Al into the sample, resulting in the formation of Li-Al-O liquid phase. So, the effects of Ga and Al on Li-ion conductivity and density cannot be clearly distinguished. Literature has reported that Ga-LLZO was sintered in Pt crucibles to avoid the introduction of Al into the samples to research the crystal chemical, but the Li-ion conductivity was not reported [22]. And the formation of Li-Ga-O liquid phase for Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 is closely related to the sintering temperature."}, {"line": 21, "text": ""}, {"line": 22, "text": "In this work, Ga-LLZO was prepared by a traditional solid-state method. Ultrafine powders were obtained by ball-milling. Ga-LLZO was sintered in ZrO2 crucibles to avoid the introduction of Al into the samples. The effects of sintering temperature and Ga-doping amount on Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 were investigated."}, {"line": 23, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase ", "line": 20, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase .md", "submatches": [{"end": 1940, "start": 36}], "text": "Much work has been done on Al-doped LLZO [12–20]. Appropriate Al3+ substitution increases Li+ vacancies, leading to the improvement of Li-ion conductivity and stability of the cubic phase [12–20]. The crystal-chemical behavior is similar between gallium and aluminum on the basis of the periodic table. Ga-doped LLZO has attracted considerable attention [21–24]. The structure of LLZO dop