ity and stability of the cubic phase [12–20]. The crystal-chemical behavior is similar between gallium and aluminum on the basis of the periodic table. Ga-doped LLZO has attracted considerable attention [21–24]. The structure of LLZO doped with Ga3+ or Fe3+ in the space group of I-43d was reported [7, 24]. Li atoms occupy three positions: two tetrahedrally coordinated sites (12 a and 12 b), and an octahedrally coordinated site (48 e) [7, 23, 24]. Wagner et al. reported that the bulk Li-ion conductivity of Fe-LLZO sintered at 1230 °C is 1.38 × 10−3 S/cm at room temperature [7]. Shinawi et al. reported that the Li-ion conductivity of Ga-doped LLZO is 5.4 × 10−4 S/cm [21]. Rettenwander et al. reported that the Li-ion conductivity of LLZO doped with Al3+ and Ga3+ is 1.2 × 10−3 S/cm at room temperature [23]. Ernuy-Lopez et al. reported that the Li-ion conductivity of Ga-LLZO sintered in dry O2 atmosphere is 1.3 × 10−3 S/cm at 24 °C [25]. Janani et al. reported that AlLLZO added with 1 wt% Li4SiO4 formed a Li-Al-Si-O liquid phase during the sintering process, and its Li-ion conductivity is 6.1 × 10−4 S/cm at room temperature [15]. Li Yiqiu et al. reported that Li-Al-O amorphous phase is formed at grain boundaries due to the introduction of Al from Al2O3 crucibles, which promotes the densification due to the formation of liquid phase [26–28]. As for Ga-LLZO, the liquid phase of the Li-Ga-O compounds is also formed during the sintering process [28]. Ga-LLZO sintered in Al2O3 crucibles leads to the introduction of Al into the sample, resulting in the formation of Li-Al-O liquid phase. So, the effects of Ga and Al on Li-ion conductivity and density cannot be clearly distinguished. Literature has reported that Ga-LLZO was sintered in Pt crucibles to avoid the introduction of Al into the samples to research the crystal chemical, but the Li-ion conductivity was not reported [22]. And the formation of Li-Ga-O liquid phase for Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 is closely related to the sintering temperature."}, {"line": 21, "text": ""}, {"line": 22, "text": "In this work, Ga-LLZO was prepared by a traditional solid-state method. Ultrafine powders were obtained by ball-milling. Ga-LLZO was sintered in ZrO2 crucibles to avoid the introduction of Al into the samples. The effects of sintering temperature and Ga-doping amount on Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 were investigated."}, {"line": 23, "text": ""}, {"line": 24, "text": "# Experimental"}, {"line": 25, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase ", "line": 22, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase .md", "submatches": [{"end": 158, "start": 17}], "text": "In this work, Ga-LLZO was prepared by a traditional solid-state method. Ultrafine powders were obtained by ball-milling. Ga-LLZO was sintered in ZrO2 crucibles to avoid the introduction of Al into the samples. The effects of sintering temperature and Ga-doping amount on Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 were investigated."}, {"context": [{"line": 68, "text": ""}, {"line": 69, "text": "# Conclusions"}, {"line": 70, "text": ""}, {"line": 71, "text": "Ga-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 is a promising solid electrolyte material due to the high conductivity. Ga-LLZO was prepared by a traditional solid-state method. Ultrafine powders were obtained by ball-milling. Ga-LLZO was sintered in ZrO2 crucibles to avoid the introduction of Al into the samples. The formation of Li-Ga-O liquid phase is beneficial for the improvement of Li-ion conductivity and bulk density. As for Al-free Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12, the Li-ion conductivity and bulk density are improved with the sintering temperature rise. But when the sintering temperature reaches 1250 °C, the Li-ion conductivity and bulk density decline. As for Al-free Li7- 3xGaxLa3Zr2O12, the tetragonal phase disappears completely when x ≥ 0.15. The Li-ion conductivity and bulk density of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 are improved with an increase of x (x ≤ 0.2). The ionic conductivity of Al-free Li7- 3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 sintered in the air by liquid-phase sintering is close to Ga-LLZO sintered in dry O2 atmosphere. Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 sintered at 1200 °C exhibits the highest Li-ion conductivity of 1.5 × 10−3 S/cm at 30 °C, and the activation energy is about 0.28 eV."}, {"line": 72, "text": ""}, {"line": 73, "text": "Funding information The authors acknowledge the financial support from the National Natural Science Foundation of China (51374055)."}, {"line": 74, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase ", "line": 71, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase .md", "submatches": [{"end": 238, "start": 97}], "text": "Ga-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 is a promising solid electrolyte material due to the high conductivity. Ga-LLZO was prepared by a traditional solid-state method. Ultrafine powders were obtained by ball-milling. Ga-LLZO was sintered in ZrO2 crucibles to avoid the introduction of Al into the samples. The formation of Li-Ga-O liquid phase is beneficial for the improvement of Li-ion conductivity and bulk density. As for Al-free Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12, the Li-ion conductivity and bulk density are improved with the sintering temperature rise. But when the sintering temperature reaches 1250 °C, the Li-ion conductivity and bulk density decline. As for Al-free Li7- 3xGaxLa3Zr2O12, the tetragonal phase disappears completely when x ≥ 0.15. The Li-ion conductivity and bulk density of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 are improved with an increase of x (x ≤ 0.2). The ionic conductivity of Al-free Li7- 3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 sintered in the air by liquid-phase sintering is close to Ga-LLZO sintered in dry O2 atmosphere. Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 sintered at 1200 °C exhibits the highest Li-ion conductivity of 1.5 × 10−3 S/cm at 30 °C, and the activation energy is about 0.28 eV."}, {"context": [{"line": 51, "text": ""}, {"line": 52, "text": "# 2.1 Preparation of Ga-LLZO powders, green pellets and ceramics"}, {"line": 53, "text": ""}, {"line": 54, "text": "Ga-LLZO with nominal composition of Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 was prepared through conventional solid-state reaction [15]. Starting materials (total 0.1mol) of LiOH·H2O (AR, 99%), Ga2O3 (4N), La2O3 (AR, 99%) and ZrO2 (AR, 99%) were weighted at the stoichiometric ratio. The La2O3 powders were heated at 900 °C for 6 h before use to remove volatiles. The mixture is then planetary ball milled with isopropanol at 250 rpm for 12 h, followed by drying at 75 °C and calcined in an Al2O3 crucible at 850 °C for 6 h. The powders were then wet ball milled again at 250 rpm for 12 h, dried and sieved over 200 mesh to obtain a finer powder. Afterward this powder was dry pressed at 90 MPa into green pellets with a diameter of 18 mm. Finally, the pellets were sintered using either single-step or two-step heating schedules in Pt crucibles at a heating and cooling rate of 5 °C/min. The Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 mother powder with 5% excess lithium was lined at the bottom of the Pt crucible to compensate the lithium loss during high temperature sintering."}, {"line": 55, "text": ""}, {"line": 56, "text": "# 2.2 Preparation of Li/Ga-LLZO/Li and Li/Ga-LLZO/LiFePO4 batteries"}, {"line": 57, "text": ""}], "document_id": "Overcoming the abnormal grain growth in Ga-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 to enhance the electrochemical stabili", "line": 54, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> the abnormal grain growth in Ga-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 to enhance the electrochemical stabili.md", "submatches": [{"end": 977, "start": 3}], "text": "Ga-LLZO with nominal composition of Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 was prepared through conventional solid-state reaction [15]. Starting materials (total 0.1mol) of LiOH·H2O (AR, 99%), Ga2O3 (4N), La2O3 (AR, 99%) and ZrO2 (AR, 99%) were weighted at the stoichiometric ratio. The La2O3 powders were heated at 900 °C for 6 h before use to remove volatiles. The mixture is then planetary ball milled with isopropanol at 250 rpm for 12 h, followed by drying at 75 °C and calcined in an Al2O3 crucible at 850 °C for 6 h. The powders were then wet ball milled again at 250 rpm for 12 h, dried and sieved over 200 mesh to obtain a finer powder. Afterward this powder was dry pressed at 90 MPa into green pellets with a diameter of 18 mm. Finally, the pellets were sintered using either single-step or two-step heating schedules in Pt crucibles at a heating and cooling rate of 5 °C/min. The Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 mother powder with 5% excess lithium was lined at the bottom of the Pt crucible to compensate the lithium loss during high temperature sintering."}, {"context": [{"line": 22, "text": ""}, {"line": 23, "text": "# Experimental Procedure"}, {"line": 24, "text": ""}, {"line": 25, "text": "The garnet-type solid electrolyte with nominal chemical formula Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 (LLZTO) was prepared by conventional solid state reaction using Li2CO3 (99.9%), La2O3 (99.99%), ZrO2 (99.99%) and Ta2O5 (99.99%) as starting materials. The La2O3 powder was dried at 900ºC to remove the absorbed moisture and carbon dioxide. The Li2CO3 was 20 wt% excessive and other components were weighted according to chemical stoichiometry. The powders were thoroughly mixed and ground with an agate mortar and pestle, and then heated at 900 °C for 6 h. The pre-heated powders were mixed with 1.2 wt% Al2O3 as sintering additive and then ground with an agate mortar and pestle. The mixed powders were pressed into tablets with a diameter of 10mm. The tablets were placed in a corundum crucible where the tablets were supported by a corundum holder (the apparatus and synthesized samples"}, {"line": 26, "text": ""}, {"line": 27, "text": "This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 28, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &", "line": 25, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &.md", "submatches": [{"end": 784, "start": 87}], "text": "The garnet-type solid electrolyte with nominal chemical formula Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 (LLZTO) was prepared by conventional solid state reaction using Li2CO3 (99.9%), La2O3 (99.99%), ZrO2 (99.99%) and Ta2O5 (99.99%) as starting materials. The La2O3 powder was dried at 900ºC to remove the absorbed moisture and carbon dioxide. The Li2CO3 was 20 wt% excessive and other components were weighted according to chemical stoichiometry. The powders were thoroughly mixed and ground with an agate mortar and pestle, and then heated at 900 °C for 6 h. The pre-heated powders were mixed with 1.2 wt% Al2O3 as sintering additive and then ground with an agate mortar and pestle. The mixed powders were pressed into tablets with a diameter of 10mm. The tablets were placed in a corundum crucible where the tablets were supported by a corundum holder (the apparatus and synthesized samples"}, {"context": [{"line": 36, "text": ""}, {"line": 37, "text": "# Results and discussion"}, {"line": 38, "text": ""}, {"line": 39, "text": "Various LLZTO tablets were synthesized with 0, 0.5g, 1g and 1.5g Li2CO3 providing the protective atmosphere at 1060°C, 1100°C, and 1140°C in a corundum crucible, respectively. For the sake of convenience, we note the samples synthesized with 0, 0.5g, 1g and 1.5g Li2CO3 as L0, L0.5, L1 and L1.5. TG-DTA was taken to characterize the sintering process and heat treatment process of LLZTO and the result is shown in Fig.2. An endothermic peak at 732°C in the DTA curve of sintering indicates the Li2CO3 melting and decomposing process then a relative mass loss of about 10% happens in the process. As a comparison, the heat treatment of LLZTO has no endothermic peak at 732°C but has one at 1032°C, which is the melting of the eutectic of LiAlO2 and Li5AlO4 19. The TG curve of heat treatment of LLZTO shows that there is only 1% mass loss in the heat treatment of LLZTO pure phase even when it is heated to 1400°C. The result indicates that the garnet phase of LLZTO is stable at high temperature. Therefore, the Li2CO3 just need to   "}, {"line": 40, "text": "compensate the loss of lithium before the garnet phase is formed.   "}, {"line": 41, "text": "This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 42, "text": ""}], "document_id": "_ Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &", "line": 39, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &.md", "submatches": [{"end": 163, "start": 8}], "text": "Various LLZTO tablets were synthesized with 0, 0.5g, 1g and 1.5g Li2CO3 providing the protective atmosphere at 1060°C, 1100°C, and 1140°C in a corundum crucible, respectively. For the sake of convenience, we note the samples synthesized with 0, 0.5g, 1g and 1.5g Li2CO3 as L0, L0.5, L1 and L1.5. TG-DTA was taken to characterize the sintering process and heat treatment process of LLZTO and the result is shown in Fig.2. An endothermic peak at 732°C in the DTA curve of sintering indicates the Li2CO3 melting and decomposing process then a relative mass loss of about 10% happens in the process. As a comparison, the heat treatment of LLZTO has no endothermic peak at 732°C but has one at 1032°C, which is the melting of the eutectic of LiAlO2 and Li5AlO4 19. The TG curve of heat treatment of LLZTO shows that there is only 1% mass loss in the heat treatment of LLZTO pure phase even when it is heated to 1400°C. The result indicates that the garnet phase of LLZTO is stable at high temperature. Therefore, the Li2CO3 just need to   "}, {"context": [{"line": 44, "text": ""}, {"line": 45, "text": "At 1100°C, the LLZTO tablet samples were prepared with different amount of Li2CO3 added to provide protective atmosphere. Fig.3(b) shows the XRD patterns of L0, L0.5, L1 and L1.5 samples, and the diffraction peaks of Li5LaNb2O12 (ICSD PDF #45-0109) which has This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 46, "text": ""}, {"line": 47, "text": "the same crystal structure of Li7La3Zr2O12 are shown below the patterns. From the Fig.3 (b), it can be seen that all the samples except the L0 have the main phase of LLZTO. However, the L0 sample, which has no Li2CO3 to compensate the Li loss, has the mixed phases of LLZTO and La2Zr2O7. The high intensity of the La2Zr2O7 phase indicates that massive La2Zr2O7 can form easily at a Li-deficient condition during the high temperature sintering process. The TG-DTA curve in Fig.2 shows that there is an exothermic peak at about 732°C, where Li2CO3 melts at this temperature and the process of Li2CO3 discomposing into Li2O will speed up. If Li2CO3 within the LLZTO sample decomposes into Li2O and CO2, and Li2O is easy to volatilize at high temperature. The protective Li2CO3 outside the samples will supply additive lithium source to compensate the loss of Li. In the experiment, the Li2CO3 on the bottle of crucible kept a high Li2O concentration in the atmosphere and thus suppressed the volatilization of Li2O. It is obvious that with the effect of Li2CO3 compensation, samples of L0.5, L1 and L1.5 eliminated the La2Zr2O7 phase and remained LLZTO as the dominant phase. For samples with a larger quantity of Li2CO3 compensation, such as L1 and L1.5 samples, also shows a certain amount of LiAlO2 phase in the patterns. The more the Li2CO3 is, the more LiAlO2 the samples have. It is reasonable to conclude that the compensative outside Li2CO3 can also promote the formation of eutectic liquid and thus enhance the densification of the LLZTO samples. Fig.5 shows the micro-morphology of LLZTO sample prepared at 1100°C with different amount 