workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:28:{"duration_ms": 30705.779, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 2, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 12, "query": "mother powder", "regex": false, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 50, "text": "The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with 15Li LiOH are shown in Figure 2b. All spectra contained peaks for only the cubic garnet phase. The LiOH in the cover powder also helped with the phase stabilization, as 25Li showed the pure cubic garnet phase with the cover of 15Li LiOH but contained pyrochlore phase with the cover of 15Li."}, {"line": 51, "text": ""}, {"line": 52, "text": "Densification of LLZTO.—The densification results of different samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours are summarized in Table II. Shrinkage did not occur with the cover of 15Li, except for 75Li, which showed nonuniform shrinkage. When the cover was changed to 15Li LiOH, shrinkage was observed for all samples, except for 25Li which showed nonuniform shrinkage. 30Li and 65Li samples have relative densities of 58 and 55% with the cover of 15Li, which is different from Rosero-Navarro group’s work that when 12wt% excess LiOH was added into 10Li sample (22Li using the name convention used in this paper), relative density increased from 50% to 77% with the cover of 10Li.54 The relative densities were all increased to 92% and 90% when the cover powder contained LiOH. The densification of pellets sintered with mother powder of high Li amount was also noticed by another research group.48 As noted above, 75Li showed nonuniform shrinkage after the sintering with the 15Li cover, which indicates that sufficient excess LiOH could help with the densification. However, without LiOH in the cover, the shrinkage of 75Li was nonuniform. Subsequent studies focused on samples with 30 or 65 wt% LiOH as these samples all uniformly shrank after the sintering with the cover of 15Li LiOH."}, {"line": 53, "text": ""}, {"line": 54, "text": "![](images/245ce9cf80270471df7e08cdc66a6aa667309c107851e997162abe9202542aeb.jpg)  "}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 52, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 832, "start": 819}], "text": "Densification of LLZTO.—The densification results of different samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours are summarized in Table II. Shrinkage did not occur with the cover of 15Li, except for 75Li, which showed nonuniform shrinkage. When the cover was changed to 15Li LiOH, shrinkage was observed for all samples, except for 25Li which showed nonuniform shrinkage. 30Li and 65Li samples have relative densities of 58 and 55% with the cover of 15Li, which is different from Rosero-Navarro group’s work that when 12wt% excess LiOH was added into 10Li sample (22Li using the name convention used in this paper), relative density increased from 50% to 77% with the cover of 10Li.54 The relative densities were all increased to 92% and 90% when the cover powder contained LiOH. The densification of pellets sintered with mother powder of high Li amount was also noticed by another research group.48 As noted above, 75Li showed nonuniform shrinkage after the sintering with the 15Li cover, which indicates that sufficient excess LiOH could help with the densification. However, without LiOH in the cover, the shrinkage of 75Li was nonuniform. Subsequent studies focused on samples with 30 or 65 wt% LiOH as these samples all uniformly shrank after the sintering with the cover of 15Li LiOH."}, {"context": [{"line": 41, "text": "# Methods"}, {"line": 42, "text": ""}, {"line": 43, "text": "Synthesis.—NZTO was synthesized via conventional solid-state methods from Na2CO3 (Sigma Aldrich, 99.5%), ZnO (Sigma Aldrich, 99.99%) and TeO2 (Sigma Aldrich, 99%). An excess of 10 wt% Na2CO3 was added to compensate for evaporation of Na at elevated temperature. All precursors were ball milled for 30 min and heated to 900°C at a heating rate of 5°C/min for 6 h in an alumina crucible. After calcination, the powder was ball milled for 1 h and cold pressed into 10 mm diameter pellets. The pellets were covered with the mother powder in a capped alumina crucible and re-heated to 900°C for 15 h (heating/cooling rate of 5°C/min). Additionally, quenched and longterm-annealed samples were synthesized for comparison: NZTO (ZQ) was quenched from 900°C to room temperature (RT) in air; long-term annealing of NZTO (ZA7d) was done at 600°C for 7 additional days and cooled to RT at a cooling rate of 5°C/min."}, {"line": 44, "text": ""}, {"line": 45, "text": "Data collection and analysis.—Initial sample characterization was done by powder X-ray diffraction measurements on a Bruker D8 diffractometer using Cu Kα1 radiation."}], "document_id": "_ P2 Type Layered Solid-State Electrolyte Na &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_sub&gt; Zn &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_sub&gt; ", "line": 43, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> P2 Type Layered Solid-State Electrolyte Na &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_sub&gt; Zn &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_sub&gt; .md", "submatches": [{"end": 537, "start": 524}], "text": "Synthesis.—NZTO was synthesized via conventional solid-state methods from Na2CO3 (Sigma Aldrich, 99.5%), ZnO (Sigma Aldrich, 99.99%) and TeO2 (Sigma Aldrich, 99%). An excess of 10 wt% Na2CO3 was added to compensate for evaporation of Na at elevated temperature. All precursors were ball milled for 30 min and heated to 900°C at a heating rate of 5°C/min for 6 h in an alumina crucible. After calcination, the powder was ball milled for 1 h and cold pressed into 10 mm diameter pellets. The pellets were covered with the mother powder in a capped alumina crucible and re-heated to 900°C for 15 h (heating/cooling rate of 5°C/min). Additionally, quenched and longterm-annealed samples were synthesized for comparison: NZTO (ZQ) was quenched from 900°C to room temperature (RT) in air; long-term annealing of NZTO (ZA7d) was done at 600°C for 7 additional days and cooled to RT at a cooling rate of 5°C/min."}, {"context": [{"line": 15, "text": "The recently discovered solid electrolyte with a garnet structure and the overall formula of Li7La3Zr2O12 attracted much attention since the first publication [3]. High lithium-ion conductivity combined with the stability in contact with metallic lithium, high decomposition voltage (6 V vs. Li+/Li), easy availability and low const of the initial materials make lithium–lanthanum zirconate Li7La3Zr2O12 promising as a solid electrolyte for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries [3‒7]. In this connection, synthesis and study of ion conductivity Li7La3Zr2O12 are of current concern."}, {"line": 16, "text": ""}, {"line": 17, "text": "At present, different methods of Li7La3Zr2O12 synthesis are published: solid-phase, sol–gel, solid-phase with mechanical preactivation, combined solidphase-liquid, codeposition, sintering, synthesis from molten salts in the LiCl–KCl eutectics) [8–13]. The most widespread methods are high-temperature solidphase sintering of a multicomponent mix of the initial reagents in the form of lanthanum and zirconium oxides and lithium salts and also the sol–gel technique. A fault of the conventional method of solid-phase synthesis of lithium–lanthanum zirconate Li7La3Zr2O12 on the basis of refractory oxides of La2O3 (tmelt \\~ 2320°C) and ZrO2 (tmelt \\~ 2700°C) and lithium carbonate Li2CO3 is the high sintering temperature (1230°C) for a prolonged period of time and necessity of repeated burden changes to obtain a homogeneous final product. Thus, in [3] the initial powders of La2O3, ZrO2, and LiOH were ground using a ball mill for 12 h to obtain Li7La3Zr2O12; this process was repeated twice after thermal treatment at 900°C and after thermal treatment at 1125°C. The reaction product was compacted into tablets and sintered at 1230°C for 36 h under the mother powder. Ion conductivity (3 × 10–4 S/cm at 25°C) was measured at the example of tablets with the density of 92%. This value of specific ion conductivity of the new lithium–ion conductor of Li7La3Zr2O12 with a garnet structure is higher than the ion conductivity of all the earlier described lithium garnets [3]. However, synthesis of such a structure is related to prolonged conditioning of the lithium–containing material at the high temperature (36 h at 1230°C). The overall synthesis time was more than 90 h. One should point out that the authors obtained the cubic modification by sintering at the high temperature with a considerable conditioning time. Formation of the cubic modification of Li La Zr O was also favored by synthesis of the solid electrolyte in an alumina crucible, as the crucible material interacted with the reaction mixture, which resulted in the uncontrolled doping of Li7La3Zr2O12 by A13+ ions. In the further works, the doping of the solid electrolyte based on lithium and lanthanum zirconate by aluminum oxide was used for stabilization of the high-conductivity cubic modification."}, {"line": 18, "text": ""}, {"line": 19, "text": "The initial materials of Li2CO3, La2O3 and aqueous solution of ZrO(NO ) were used for synthesis of Li7La3Zr2O12 using the citrate–nitrate technique [9]. These components were dissolved in a mixture of dilute nitric and citric acids. The overall solution was evaporated at 80°C up to transparent gel. At the next stage, the gel was dried and subjected to pyrolysis at \\~200°C. Then the powder was sintered at 900°C for 3 h, but the nonconducting impurity phase of La2Zr2O7 was present in the final product. The citrate–nitrate technique allows obtaining a product with a uniform grain size distribution. The faults of this method include a prolonged stage of gel formation and low ion conductivity value. Conductivity of Li7La3Zr2O12 synthesized using the citrate–nitrate technique was 5.2 × 10−6 S/cm at 20°C. Conductivity of Li7La3Zr2O12 was measured for tablets with the density of \\~70% of the theoretical value after their sintering at 1100°C for 1 h."}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132", "line": 17, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1185, "start": 1172}], "text": "At present, different methods of Li7La3Zr2O12 synthesis are published: solid-phase, sol–gel, solid-phase with mechanical preactivation, combined solidphase-liquid, codeposition, sintering, synthesis from molten salts in the LiCl–KCl eutectics) [8–13]. The most widespread methods are high-temperature solidphase sintering of a multicomponent mix of the initial reagents in the form of lanthanum and zirconium oxides and lithium salts and also the sol–gel technique. A fault of the conventional method of solid-phase synthesis of lithium–lanthanum zirconate Li7La3Zr2O12 on the basis of refractory oxides of La2O3 (tmelt \\~ 2320°C) and ZrO2 (tmelt \\~ 2700°C) and lithium carbonate Li2CO3 is the high sintering temperature (1230°C) for a prolonged period of time and necessity of repeated burden changes to obtain a homogeneous final product. Thus, in [3] the initial powders of La2O3, ZrO2, and LiOH were ground using a ball mill for 12 h to obtain Li7La3Zr2O12; this process was repeated twice after thermal treatment at 900°C and after thermal treatment at 1125°C. The reaction product was compacted into tablets and sintered at 1230°C for 36 h under the mother powder. Ion conductivity (3 × 10–4 S/cm at 25°C) was measured at the example of tablets with the density of 92%. This value of specific ion conductivity of the new lithium–ion conductor of Li7La3Zr2O12 with a garnet structure is higher than the ion conductivity of all the earlier described lithium garnets [3]. However, synthesis of such a structure is related to prolonged conditioning of the lithium–containing material at the high temperature (36 h at 1230°C). The overall synthesis time was more than 90 h. One should point out that the authors obtained the cubic modification by sintering at the high temperature with a considerable conditioning time. Formation of the cubic modification of Li La Zr O was also favored by synthesis of the solid electrolyte in an alumina crucible, as the crucible material interacted with the reaction mixture, which resulted in the uncontrolled doping of Li7La3Zr2O12 by A13+ ions. In the further works, the doping of the solid electrolyte based on lithium and lanthanum zirconate by aluminum oxide was used for stabilization of the high-conductivity cubic modification."}, {"context": [{"line": 66, "text": "Fig. 5. Complex plane plot of LLZ samples synthesized with different excess amounts of Li2CO3 in the mix: (1) 20% excess of Li2CO3, (2) 30% excess of Li2CO3, (3) 40% excess of Li2CO3."}, {"line": 67, "text": ""}, {"line": 68, "text": "For measurement of ion conductivity of the synthesized solid electrolyte from a single-phase LLZ powder, cylindrical tables with the diameter of 12 mm and height of \\~2 mm were compacted. Tablets were sintered under the mother powder in the following mode: heating to 1050°C at the rate of 10 deg/min, then heating from 1050 to 1150°C at the rate of 2 deg/min, and isothermal conditioning at 1150°C for 5–6 h. After the sintering, the tablet density was measured and graphite electrodes for impedance measurements, alongside with the Au or Ag paste, were applied on their faces. The tablet density was calculated as the result of dividing the sintered sample mass by its volume calculated on the basis of the geometric dimensions of the cylindrical tablet. The size of the prepolished sample was measured using a micrometer to the accuracy of ±0.01 mm and weighed at LV-210A electronic balance to the accuracy of ±0.0005 g. Due to the high porosity of samples, it was not practical to determine the density using the hydrostatic weighing technique. Conductivity measurements were carried out in a screened clamping cell."}, {"line": 69, "text": ""}, {"line": 70, "text": "To obtain a single-phase sample, the excess of the lithium-containing component (Li2CO3) in the initial mix were varied in order to compensate lithium losses in the course of the high-temperature treatment, as the data presented in the literature are contradictory [7]. It was found on the basis of the XRD data and conductivity measurements for the LLZ synthesized samples that the optimum excess of Li CO is 20%. The Li2CO3 phase in X-ray patterns and therefore high resistance in impedance complex plane plots were registered when Li CO excess above 20% was used (Fig. 5). Using the Li CO excess below <20% did not lead to synthesis of a single-phase product; the La2Zr2O7 impurity phase was present in the X-ray pattern."}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132", "line": 68, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132.md", "submatches": [{"end": 233, "start": 220}], "text": "For measurement of ion conductivity of the synthesized solid electrolyte from a single-phase LLZ powder, cylindrical tables with the diameter of 12 mm and height of \\~2 mm were compacted. Tablets were sintered under the mother powder in the following mode: heating to 1050°C at the rate of 10 deg/min, then heating from 1050 to 1150°C at the rate of 2 deg/min, and isothermal conditioning at 1150°C for 5–6 h. After the sintering, the tablet density was measured and graphite electrodes for impedance measurements, alongside with the Au or Ag paste, were applied on their faces. The tablet density was calculated as the result of dividing the sintered sample mass by its volume calculated on the basis of the geometric dimensions of the cylindrical tablet. The size of the prepolished sample was measured using a micrometer to the accuracy of ±0.01 mm and weighed at LV-210A electronic balance to the accuracy of ±0.0005 g. Due to the high porosity of samples, it was not practical to determine the density using the hydrostatic weighing technique. Conductivity measurements were carried out in a screened clamping cell."}, {"context": [{"line": 50, "text": "The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with 15Li  LiOH are shown in Figure 2b. All spectra contained peaks for only the cubic garnet phase. The LiOH in the cover powder also helped with the phase stabilization, as 25Li showed the pure cubic garnet phase with the cover of 15Li  LiOH but contained pyrochlore phase with the cover of 15Li."}, {"line": 51, "text": ""}, {"line": 52, "text": "Densification of LLZTO.—The densification results of different samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours are summarized in Table II. Shrinkage did not occur with the cover of 15Li, except for 75Li, which showed nonuniform shrinkage. When the cover was changed to 15Li LiOH, shrinkage was observed for all samples, except for 25Li which showed nonuniform shrinkage. 30Li and 65Li samples have relative densities of 58 and 55% with the cover of 15Li, which is different from Rosero-Navarro group’s work that when 12wt% excess LiOH was added into 10Li sample (22Li using the name convention used in this paper), relative density increased from 50% to 77% with the cover of 10Li.54 The relative densities were all increased to 92% and 90% when the cover powder contained LiOH. The densification of pellets sintered with mother powder of high Li amount was also noticed by another research group.48 As noted above, 75Li showed nonuniform shrinkage after the sintering with the 15Li cover, which indicates that sufficient excess LiOH could help with the densification. However, without LiOH in the cover, the shrinkage of 75Li was nonuniform. Subsequent studies focused on samples with 30 or 65 wt% LiOH as these samples all uniformly shrank after the sintering with the cover of 15Li LiOH."}, {"line": 53, "text": ""}, {"line": 54, "text": "![](images/3494211a0e6a84e5a8ec60ec19b244d091b1fb542e5d0779b19ca70a00542c3f.jpg)  "}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 52, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 832, "start": 819}], "text": "Densification of LLZTO.—The densification results of different samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours are summarized in Table II. Shrinkage did not occur with the cover of 15Li, except for 75Li, which showed nonuniform shrinkage. When the cover was changed to 15Li LiOH, shrinkage was observed for all samples, except for 25Li which showed nonuniform shrinkage. 30Li and 65Li samples have relative densities of 58 and 55% with the cover of 15Li, which is different from Rosero-Navarro group’s work that when 12wt% excess LiOH was added into 10Li sample (22Li using the name convention used in this paper), relative density increased from 50% to 77% with the cover of 10Li.54 The relative densities were all increased to 92% and 90% when the cover powder contained LiOH. The densification of pellets sintered with mother powder of high Li amount was also noticed by another research group.48 As noted above, 75Li showed nonuniform shrinkage after the sintering with the 15Li cover, which indicates that sufficient excess LiOH could help with the densification. However, without LiOH in the cover, the shrinkage of 75Li was nonuniform. Subsequent studies focused on samples with 30 or 65 wt% LiOH as these samples all uniformly shrank after the sintering with the cover of 15Li LiOH."}, {"context": [{"line": 24, "text": "# Experimental"}, {"line": 25, "text": ""}, {"line": 26, "text": "The Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 samples (x = 0.1, 0.15, 0.2, 0.25, and 0.3) were prepared by a conventional solid-state method. Stoichiometric amounts of Li2CO3, ZrO2, Ga2O3, and La2O3 were weighted with an excess of 10 wt% Li2CO3 to compensate for the loss of Li2O. The regents were ball-milled under absolute ethyl alcohol for 10 h at a milling speed of 500 rpm/ min, dried at 100 °C for 12 h, cold-pressed into pellets with a diameter of 20 mm at a pressure of 200 MPa for 5 min, and calcined at 850 °C for 6 h in ZrO2 crucibles at a heating rate of 5 °C/min. The sintered pellets were ground, ball-milled under absolute ethyl alcohol for 10 h at a milling speed of 500 rpm/ min, dried at 100 °C for 12 h, pressed at 200 MPa into pellets with diameter of 10 mm for 5 min, and sintered at temperatures for 6 h covered with mother powder in ZrO2 crucibles."}, {"line": 27, "text": ""}, {"line": 28, "text": "![](images/6584d34469515d0c9dfcafbcc1fe352782674ada550912398b4b4814a7c4da2e.jpg)  "}], "document_id": "_ High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase ", "line": 26, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> High Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by liquid-phase .md", "submatches": [{"end": 833, "start": 820}], "text": "The Li7-3xGaxLa3Zr2O12 samples (x = 0.1, 0.15, 0.2, 0.25, and 0.3) were prepared by a conventional solid-state method. Stoichiometric amounts of Li2CO3, ZrO2, Ga2O3, and La2O3 were weighted with an excess of 10 wt% Li2CO3 to compensate for the loss of Li2O. The regents were ball-milled under absolute ethyl alcohol for 10 h at a milling speed of 500 rpm/ min, dried at 100 °C for 12 h, cold-pressed into pellets with a diameter of 20 mm at a pressure of 200 MPa for 5 min, and calcined at 850 °C for 6 h in ZrO2 crucibles at a heating rate of 5 °C/min. The sintered pellets were ground, ball-milled under absolute ethyl alcohol for 10 h at a milling speed of 500 rpm/ min, dried at 100 °C for 12 h, pressed at 200 MPa into pellets with diameter of 10 mm for 5 min, and sintered at temperatures for 6 h covered with mother powder in ZrO2 crucibles."}, {"context": [{"line": 50, "text": "# 2. Experimental"}, {"line": 51, "text": ""}, {"line": 52, "text": "The Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 (x =0, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.5, and 0.6) samples were prepared by a conventional solid-state method. Stoichiometric amounts of Li2CO3, ZrO2, Ta2O5 and La2O3 were weighted with an excess of 10 wt% Li2CO3 to compensate for the loss of Li, ball-milled under absolute ethyl alcohol at a milling speed of 500 rpm, dried at 100 °C for 12 h, cold-pressed into pellets with diameter of 20 mm at a pressure of 200 MPa for 5 min, and calcined at 900 °C for 12 h in Al2O3 crucibles at a heating rate of 3 °C/min. The sintered pellets were ground, ball-milled under absolute ethyl alcohol at a milling speed of 500 rpm, and dried at 100 °C for 12 h. The particle size distributions of the resulting powders were characterized by a laser diffraction particle size analyzer. The resulting powders were pressed at 200 MPa into pellets with diameter of 10 mm for 5 min, and sintered at 1200 °C for 12 h covered with mother powder in ZrO2 crucibles."}, {"line": 53, "text": ""}, {"line": 54, "text": "The phase structures and the morphologies of the pellets were examined by a X-ray diffractometer (XRD) using Cu Ka (= 0.154056 nm) radiation in the range of 2 θ angles from 10-70 (°) and a scanning electron microscope (SEM), respectively. The density of the pellet was obtained by an Archimedes method using kerosene for measurement. Silver paste was screen-printed on both sides of the pellet, and calcinated at 850 °C for 10 min. The ionic conductivity of the pellets was measured by AC impedance spectroscopy (AUTOLAB PGSTAT 204) in the frequency range of 100 Hz – 0.2 MHz with an amplitude of 10 mV and in the temperature range from 30 to 70 °C. The electronic conductivity of Ta-LLZO was measured by DC polarization with a DC voltage of 0.1 V, and the current was recorded for 480 s. AC impedance spectroscopy of the semi-solid batteries was measured in a frequency range of 1 MHz to 0.1 Hz with an amplitude of 10 mV. And the performance of charge–discharge for semi-solid batteries was tested on a battery testing system between 2 and 4.2 V at 0.1 C."}], "document_id": "_ High densification and Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-La3Zr2-Ta O12 garnet solid electrolyte p", "line": 52, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> High densification and Li-ion conductivity of Al-free Li7-La3Zr2-Ta O12 garnet solid electrolyte p.md", "submatches": [{"end": 937, "start": 924}], "text": "The Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12 (x =0, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.5, and 0.6) samples were prepared by a conventional solid-state method. Stoichiometric amounts of Li2CO3, ZrO2, Ta2O5 and La2O3 were weighted with an excess of 10 wt% Li2CO3 to compensate for the loss of Li, ball-milled under absolute ethyl alcohol at a milling speed of 500 rpm, dried at 100 °C for 12 h, cold-pressed into pellets with diameter of 20 mm at a pressure of 200 MPa for 5 min, and calcined at 900 °C for 12 h in Al2O3 crucibles at a heating rate of 3 °C/min. The sintered pellets were ground, ball-milled under absolute ethyl alcohol at a milling speed of 500 rpm, and dried at 100 °C for 12 h. The particle size distributions of the resulting powders were characterized by a laser diffraction particle size analyzer. The resulting powders were pressed at 200 MPa into pellets with diameter of 10 mm for 5 min, and sintered at 1200 °C for 12 h covered with mother powder in ZrO2 crucibles."}, {"context": [{"line": 52, "text": "# 2.1 Preparation of Ga-LLZO powders, green pellets and ceramics"}, {"line": 53, "text": ""}, {"line": 54, "text": "Ga-LLZO with nominal composition of Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 was prepared through conventional solid-state reaction [15]. Starting materials (total 0.1mol) of LiOH·H2O (AR, 99%), Ga2O3 (4N), La2O3 (AR, 99%) and ZrO2 (AR, 99%) were weighted at the stoichiometric ratio. The La2O3 powders were heated at 900 °C for 6 h before use to remove volatiles. The mixture is then planetary ball milled with isopropanol at 250 rpm for 12 h, followed by drying at 75 °C and calcined in an Al2O3 crucible at 850 °C for 6 h. The powders were then wet ball milled again at 250 rpm for 12 h, dried and sieved over 200 mesh to obtain a finer powder. Afterward this powder was dry pressed at 90 MPa into green pellets with a diameter of 18 mm. Finally, the pellets were sintered using either single-step or two-step heating schedules in Pt crucibles at a heating and cooling rate of 5 °C/min. The Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 mother powder with 5% excess lithium was lined at the bottom of the Pt crucible to compensate the lithium loss during high temperature sintering."}, {"line": 55, "text": ""}, {"line": 56, "text": "# 2.2 Preparation of Li/Ga-LLZO/Li and Li/Ga-LLZO/LiFePO4 batteries"}], "document_id": "Overcoming the abnormal grain growth in Ga-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 to enhance the electrochemical stabili", "line": 54, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> the abnormal grain growth in Ga-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 to enhance the electrochemical stabili.md", "submatches": [{"end": 911, "start": 898}], "text": "Ga-LLZO with nominal composition of Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 was prepared through conventional solid-state reaction [15]. Starting materials (total 0.1mol) of LiOH·H2O (AR, 99%), Ga2O3 (4N), La2O3 (AR, 99%) and ZrO2 (AR, 99%) were weighted at the stoichiometric ratio. The La2O3 powders were heated at 900 °C for 6 h before use to remove volatiles. The mixture is then planetary ball milled with isopropanol at 250 rpm for 12 h, followed by drying at 75 °C and calcined in an Al2O3 crucible at 850 °C for 6 h. The powders were then wet ball milled again at 250 rpm for 12 h, dried and sieved over 200 mesh to obtain a finer powder. Afterward this powder was dry pressed at 90 MPa into green pellets with a diameter of 18 mm. Finally, the pellets were sintered using either single-step or two-step heating schedules in Pt crucibles at a heating and cooling rate of 5 °C/min. The Li6.4Ga0.2La3Zr2O12 mother powder with 5% excess lithium was lined at the bottom of the Pt crucible to compensate the lithium loss during high temperature sintering."}, {"context": [{"line": 9, "text": "# Abstract"}, {"line": 10, "text": ""}, {"line": 11, "text": "The garnet-type solid Li-ion conductor, Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12, is viewed as a promising solid state electrolyte for the next generation high performance Li-ion battery because of its high ionic conductivity and stability. The previous researches, in order to avoid Li volatilization loss during solid state reaction of sintering, mother powder with the same composition as green bodies was usually used to cover the samples, it would waste the expensive raw materials and increase the fabrication cost. In this paper, a new method was proposed to prepare the Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 ceramic solid state electrolyte where the Li loss is compensated by the outside Li2CO3. Samples prepared by this method have pure garnet phase and compact micromorphology with few pores. These samples have a high Li-ion conductivity of 2.9×10-4 S/cm and a low activation energy of 0.37eV at room temperature. The electronic conductivity is 1.08×10-8 S/cm, which can be neglected for solid state electrolyte."}, {"line": 12, "text": ""}, {"line": 13, "text": "# Introduction"}], "document_id": "_ Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &", "line": 11, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &.md", "submatches": [{"end": 343, "start": 330}], "text": "The garnet-type solid Li-ion conductor, Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12, is viewed as a promising solid state electrolyte for the next generation high performance Li-ion battery because of its high ionic conductivity and stability. The previous researches, in order to avoid Li volatilization loss during solid state reaction of sintering, mother powder with the same composition as green bodies was usually used to cover the samples, it would waste the expensive raw materials and increase the fabrication cost. In this paper, a new method was proposed to prepare the Li6.4La3Zr1.4Ta0.6O12 ceramic solid state electrolyte where the Li loss is compensated by the outside Li2CO3. Samples prepared by this method have pure garnet phase and compact micromorphology with few pores. These samples have a high Li-ion conductivity of 2.9×10-4 S/cm and a low activation energy of 0.37eV at room temperature. The electronic conductivity is 1.08×10-8 S/cm, which can be neglected for solid state electrolyte."}, {"context": [{"line": 17, "text": "This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 18, "text": ""}, {"line": 19, "text": "Garnet-type metal oxide such as Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is considered as one of the most promising solid electrolytes among the inorganic solid electrolytes4, for it shows high Li+ ionic conductivity, high stability with lithium metal and wide electrochemical window5. The sintering process6, doping strategy7-9, microstructure10 and composite design11 have been widely investigated to enhance its performance. The LLZO solid electrolyte can be synthesized by solid state reaction12, sol-gel method13, spark plasma sintering14 and hot-press sintering method15. Solid state reaction at high temperature is preferred because the cost is relatively low and the procedure is simple, which is suitable for large-scale industrial producing. The LLZO electrolyte sample prepared by solid state reaction has a moderate ionic conductivity in the order of 10-4 S/cm. Doping various elements into LLZO can increase the Li-ion conductivity and the Ta doped sample, Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12(x=0.6), has an ionic conductivity of about 1×10-3 S/cm 16, 17. However, because of the high volatility nature of Li element, Li is easy to lose during the high temperature sintering which leads to form the undesirable La2Zr2O7 as an impure phase. In order to prepare the stoichiometric LLZO, the lost Li should be compensated and the most common means is to cover the tablet samples up with the mother powder 18. The mother powder with the same chemistry composition as the samples acts as the sacrificial component to ensure the sample to form the right garnet phase. The amount of the sacrificed mother powder usually reaches the ratio 1:1 in weight to the samples and after sintering, the mother powder cannot be reused. That is the problem, as we know that the mother powder contains the rare earth element La and the element Ta that are expensive. Therefore, the conventional solid state reaction with mother powder protecting method is not economical. We noticed that the effect of the This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 20, "text": ""}, {"line": 21, "text": "mother powder is merely providing another Li source to compensate the lost Li. We proposed an alternative method to compensate the lost Li where the mother powder is replaced by Li2CO3. In this method, Li2CO3 is separated from the sample and it will melt and decompound above 730ºC. The decomposed Li2CO3 provides a Li2O atmosphere and ensures the sample to form the pure garnet phase. The method is also suitable for other oxide solid electrolytes. Li2CO3 is much cheaper than the mother powder and this method will lower the synthesis cost of LLZO as well as other oxide solid electrolytes, which is important for the large-scale application of the solid state batteries in the future."}], "document_id": "_ Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &", "line": 19, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1378, "start": 1365}, {"end": 1400, "start": 1387}, {"end": 1581, "start": 15