cting method is not economical. We noticed that the effect of the This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"context": [{"line": 19, "text": "Garnet-type metal oxide such as Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is considered as one of the most promising solid electrolytes among the inorganic solid electrolytes4, for it shows high Li+ ionic conductivity, high stability with lithium metal and wide electrochemical window5. The sintering process6, doping strategy7-9, microstructure10 and composite design11 have been widely investigated to enhance its performance. The LLZO solid electrolyte can be synthesized by solid state reaction12, sol-gel method13, spark plasma sintering14 and hot-press sintering method15. Solid state reaction at high temperature is preferred because the cost is relatively low and the procedure is simple, which is suitable for large-scale industrial producing. The LLZO electrolyte sample prepared by solid state reaction has a moderate ionic conductivity in the order of 10-4 S/cm. Doping various elements into LLZO can increase the Li-ion conductivity and the Ta doped sample, Li7-xLa3Zr2-xTaxO12(x=0.6), has an ionic conductivity of about 1×10-3 S/cm 16, 17. However, because of the high volatility nature of Li element, Li is easy to lose during the high temperature sintering which leads to form the undesirable La2Zr2O7 as an impure phase. In order to prepare the stoichiometric LLZO, the lost Li should be compensated and the most common means is to cover the tablet samples up with the mother powder 18. The mother powder with the same chemistry composition as the samples acts as the sacrificial component to ensure the sample to form the right garnet phase. The amount of the sacrificed mother powder usually reaches the ratio 1:1 in weight to the samples and after sintering, the mother powder cannot be reused. That is the problem, as we know that the mother powder contains the rare earth element La and the element Ta that are expensive. Therefore, the conventional solid state reaction with mother powder protecting method is not economical. We noticed that the effect of the This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 20, "text": ""}, {"line": 21, "text": "mother powder is merely providing another Li source to compensate the lost Li. We proposed an alternative method to compensate the lost Li where the mother powder is replaced by Li2CO3. In this method, Li2CO3 is separated from the sample and it will melt and decompound above 730ºC. The decomposed Li2CO3 provides a Li2O atmosphere and ensures the sample to form the pure garnet phase. The method is also suitable for other oxide solid electrolytes. Li2CO3 is much cheaper than the mother powder and this method will lower the synthesis cost of LLZO as well as other oxide solid electrolytes, which is important for the large-scale application of the solid state batteries in the future."}, {"line": 22, "text": ""}, {"line": 23, "text": "# Experimental Procedure"}], "document_id": "_ Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &", "line": 21, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &.md", "submatches": [{"end": 13, "start": 0}, {"end": 162, "start": 149}, {"end": 496, "start": 483}], "text": "mother powder is merely providing another Li source to compensate the lost Li. We proposed an alternative method to compensate the lost Li where the mother powder is replaced by Li2CO3. In this method, Li2CO3 is separated from the sample and it will melt and decompound above 730ºC. The decomposed Li2CO3 provides a Li2O atmosphere and ensures the sample to form the pure garnet phase. The method is also suitable for other oxide solid electrolytes. Li2CO3 is much cheaper than the mother powder and this method will lower the synthesis cost of LLZO as well as other oxide solid electrolytes, which is important for the large-scale application of the solid state batteries in the future."}, {"context": [{"line": 27, "text": "This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved."}, {"line": 28, "text": ""}, {"line": 29, "text": "are shown in Fig.1, and the volume of the crucible is 12mL). A certain amount of Li2CO3 was put on the bottom of the crucible. The green bodies were heated to sintering temperature (1060 ºC, 1100ºC and 1140ºC respectively) with a heating rate of 3ºC/min and soaked for 16h. As comparison, LLZTO samples wrapped by 1g mother powder were sintered at 1100°C for 16h and the weight ratio of sample to the protective mother powder was 1:1."}, {"line": 30, "text": ""}, {"line": 31, "text": "The phases of the sintered samples were identified by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 ADVANCE, Germany) with Cu Kα (λ = 0.154178 nm) radiation. The micromorphology of bulk materials was analyzed with a field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, ZEISS MERLIN Compact, Germany)."}], "document_id": "_ Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &", "line": 29, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Sintering behavior of garnet-type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt; Zr &.md", "submatches": [{"end": 334, "start": 321}, {"end": 430, "start": 417}], "text": "are shown in Fig.1, and the volume of the crucible is 12mL). A certain amount of Li2CO3 was put on the bottom of the crucible. The green bodies were heated to sintering temperature (1060 ºC, 1100ºC and 1140ºC respectively) with a heating rate of 3ºC/min and soaked for 16h. As comparison, LLZTO samples wrapped by 1g mother powder were sintered at 1100°C for 16h and the weight ratio of sample to the protective mother powder was 1:1."}], "ok": true, "query": "mother powder", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 12, "truncated