workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:29:{"duration_ms": 2156.831, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 0, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 8, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO).*(Al|Ga|Ta|Nb|W).*(dop|cubic|stabil)", "regex": true, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 8, "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 8, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 311, "start": 26}], "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}, {"context": [{"line": 14, "text": "Using a solid-state electrolyte instead of the commonly used organic liquid electrolyte to produce an all-solid-state lithium-ion battery is regarded as one promising way to overcome safety issues, such as flammability and leakage of electrolyte.1–4 A large number of solid-state electrolytes have been reported, such as solid polymer electrolytes using different Li salts of LiClO4, LiBF4, or LiN(CF3SO2)2,5,6 and ceramic electrolytes including perovskite-type titanates (LLTO),7,8 NASICON-type (sodium super ionic conductor) oxide electrolytes,9,10 sulfide-type and garnet-type materials.11 The cubic garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention due to its high chemical stability and high ionic conductivity (8.7 10−4 S/cm at 25°C).12–15"}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 14, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 697, "start": 631}], "text": "Using a solid-state electrolyte instead of the commonly used organic liquid electrolyte to produce an all-solid-state lithium-ion battery is regarded as one promising way to overcome safety issues, such as flammability and leakage of electrolyte.1–4 A large number of solid-state electrolytes have been reported, such as solid polymer electrolytes using different Li salts of LiClO4, LiBF4, or LiN(CF3SO2)2,5,6 and ceramic electrolytes including perovskite-type titanates (LLTO),7,8 NASICON-type (sodium super ionic conductor) oxide electrolytes,9,10 sulfide-type and garnet-type materials.11 The cubic garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention due to its high chemical stability and high ionic conductivity (8.7 10−4 S/cm at 25°C).12–15"}, {"context": [{"line": 16, "text": "Besides the cubic structure, garnet-type LLZO can also exist with a tetragonal crystal structure with a Li+ conductivity of around 2 × 10−6 S/cm,16,17 which is two orders of magnitude lower than the conductivity of the cubic phase structure. In the cubic crystal structure, Li ions occupy two types of crystallographic sites. A tetrahedral site, Li(1), and a distorted octahedral site, Li(2).16 In the tetragonal phase structure, Li atoms occupy three types of crystallographic sites: a tetrahedral site, Li(1), and two distorted octahedral sites, Li(2) and Li(3).17 The lower conductivity of the tetragonal crystal structure has been attributed to the ordering of Li atoms and Li vacancies in the tetrahedral and octahedral sites, as the tetrahedral or distorted octahedral sites are either full or empty.18 In the cubic phase, however, the Li sub-lattice is disordered, so both types of Li sites are partially occupied and the Li ion vacancies are also not ordered.18–20 In the tetragonal phase, all Li sites are fully occupied (g 1), while a reduced site occupancy of Li(1) site (g 0.94) and Li(2) site (g 0.35) were found in the cubic phase.17,21 Thus, compared to the tetragonal phase, in the cubic phase the larger number of vacancies and the disorder in the Li sub-lattice enable a lower activation energy for Li motion, which enhance the Li-ion conductivity in the cubic phase.22"}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 16, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1383, "start": 41}], "text": "Besides the cubic structure, garnet-type LLZO can also exist with a tetragonal crystal structure with a Li+ conductivity of around 2 × 10−6 S/cm,16,17 which is two orders of magnitude lower than the conductivity of the cubic phase structure. In the cubic crystal structure, Li ions occupy two types of crystallographic sites. A tetrahedral site, Li(1), and a distorted octahedral site, Li(2).16 In the tetragonal phase structure, Li atoms occupy three types of crystallographic sites: a tetrahedral site, Li(1), and two distorted octahedral sites, Li(2) and Li(3).17 The lower conductivity of the tetragonal crystal structure has been attributed to the ordering of Li atoms and Li vacancies in the tetrahedral and octahedral sites, as the tetrahedral or distorted octahedral sites are either full or empty.18 In the cubic phase, however, the Li sub-lattice is disordered, so both types of Li sites are partially occupied and the Li ion vacancies are also not ordered.18–20 In the tetragonal phase, all Li sites are fully occupied (g 1), while a reduced site occupancy of Li(1) site (g 0.94) and Li(2) site (g 0.35) were found in the cubic phase.17,21 Thus, compared to the tetragonal phase, in the cubic phase the larger number of vacancies and the disorder in the Li sub-lattice enable a lower activation energy for Li motion, which enhance the Li-ion conductivity in the cubic phase.22"}, {"context": [{"line": 20, "text": "The phase stability and the conductivity are affected by the Al incorporation, as the substitution mechanism of one Al atom for three Li atoms leads to a reduction of the Li content and an increase in the Li vacancies.25 Although the stabilization of cubic phase by Al doping could help to obtain high conductivity,12,16,17 an undesired Li5AlO4 phase forming at higher doping levels, has lower Li+ conductivity than LLZO.38,39 In addition, it has been reported that the substitutional Al ions sit in the conductive pathway of Li, lead to lower conductivity.25,40 The substitutional Ta ions occupy Li sites, which will not hinder the Li-ion motion.12 The addition of Al into Ta-doped LLZO has been shown to lead to a decrease in the ionic conductivity from 8.7 × 10−4 S/cm to 3.7 × 10−4 S/cm.12 The conductivity of Al-doped LLZO has been reported as 4 10−4 S/cm, while Ta-doped LLZO could reach 8.7 10−4 S/cm.12,41 In addition to the higher ionic conductivity, Ta-doped LLZO was also found to be stable with lithium metal electrode.34,40 While in Al-doped LLZO, intergranular Li metal was observed to propagate through the grain boundaries.42 Therefore, Ta was chosen as the doping element in this work."}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 20, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1187, "start": 416}], "text": "The phase stability and the conductivity are affected by the Al incorporation, as the substitution mechanism of one Al atom for three Li atoms leads to a reduction of the Li content and an increase in the Li vacancies.25 Although the stabilization of cubic phase by Al doping could help to obtain high conductivity,12,16,17 an undesired Li5AlO4 phase forming at higher doping levels, has lower Li+ conductivity than LLZO.38,39 In addition, it has been reported that the substitutional Al ions sit in the conductive pathway of Li, lead to lower conductivity.25,40 The substitutional Ta ions occupy Li sites, which will not hinder the Li-ion motion.12 The addition of Al into Ta-doped LLZO has been shown to lead to a decrease in the ionic conductivity from 8.7 × 10−4 S/cm to 3.7 × 10−4 S/cm.12 The conductivity of Al-doped LLZO has been reported as 4 10−4 S/cm, while Ta-doped LLZO could reach 8.7 10−4 S/cm.12,41 In addition to the higher ionic conductivity, Ta-doped LLZO was also found to be stable with lithium metal electrode.34,40 While in Al-doped LLZO, intergranular Li metal was observed to propagate through the grain boundaries.42 Therefore, Ta was chosen as the doping element in this work."}, {"context": [{"line": 24, "text": "When the simple method of cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in air is used, additives are typically needed to obtain high density, details of which could be found in Table I.51–55 Additions of La2Zr2O7 and Li2O to Ta-doped LLZO and Li4SiO4 to Al-doped LLZO have led to improvements of 6–17% in density which increased conductivity 3–5 times.51–53 Even larger increases in densities have been reported with additions of LiOH, Li3BO3 and LiBO2 to Al- and Nb-doped LLZO, but the conductivities are all smaller than 1 × 10−4 S/cm.51,54,55 Samples with conductivities larger than 1 10−4 S/cm were sintered at temperature above 1100°C or prepared with cold uniaxial pressing.51–53"}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 24, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 470, "start": 228}], "text": "When the simple method of cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in air is used, additives are typically needed to obtain high density, details of which could be found in Table I.51–55 Additions of La2Zr2O7 and Li2O to Ta-doped LLZO and Li4SiO4 to Al-doped LLZO have led to improvements of 6–17% in density which increased conductivity 3–5 times.51–53 Even larger increases in densities have been reported with additions of LiOH, Li3BO3 and LiBO2 to Al- and Nb-doped LLZO, but the conductivities are all smaller than 1 × 10−4 S/cm.51,54,55 Samples with conductivities larger than 1 10−4 S/cm were sintered at temperature above 1100°C or prepared with cold uniaxial pressing.51–53"}, {"context": [{"line": 26, "text": "In this work, Ta-doped LLZO (LLZTO) was synthesized through a co-precipitation method with a low sintering temperature of 1100°C. The effects of a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which is created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder, on the phase stabilization, densification and Li+ conductivity were examined. LiOH was chosen as the additive since it is one of the starting materials."}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 26, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 264, "start": 23}], "text": "In this work, Ta-doped LLZO (LLZTO) was synthesized through a co-precipitation method with a low sintering temperature of 1100°C. The effects of a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which is created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder, on the phase stabilization, densification and Li+ conductivity were examined. LiOH was chosen as the additive since it is one of the starting materials."}, {"context": [{"line": 32, "text": "LLZTO synthesis.—Tantalum-doped Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) was prepared by the co-precipitation method. LiOH (Alfa, anhydrous, 98%), La(OH)3 (Alfa, 99.95% REO), ZrOCl2·8H2O (Alfa, 98%), Ta2O5 (Alfa, 99%, metal basis), nitric acid (Alfa, 67–70%) and NH4OH (Alfa, 5.0N) were used as starting materials. Different amounts of excess LiOH were added during different steps to compensate for the loss of lithium during sintering. All samples in this paper are named as xLi, where x refers to the total amount of excess LiOH. Transparent solutions of LiOH and La(OH)3 in nitric acid, and ZrOCl2·8H2O in deionized water were prepared first. Before mixing these two solutions, Ta2O5 powders were added into the nitric acid solution. After mixing, NH4OH was added to the mixture to obtain precipitates. The precipitates were dried at 80°C for 48 hours and then heated at 700°C under flowing air for 2 hours. The dried powders or the dried powders with additional LiOH were ball milled with zirconia balls in a planetary ball milling machine (Across International, PQN2 gear-drive planetary ball mill) at 500 rpm for 4 hours with 2-propanol as the dispersing reagent."}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 32, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 31, "start": 0}], "text": "LLZTO synthesis.—Tantalum-doped Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) was prepared by the co-precipitation method. LiOH (Alfa, anhydrous, 98%), La(OH)3 (Alfa, 99.95% REO), ZrOCl2·8H2O (Alfa, 98%), Ta2O5 (Alfa, 99%, metal basis), nitric acid (Alfa, 67–70%) and NH4OH (Alfa, 5.0N) were used as starting materials. Different amounts of excess LiOH were added during different steps to compensate for the loss of lithium during sintering. All samples in this paper are named as xLi, where x refers to the total amount of excess LiOH. Transparent solutions of LiOH and La(OH)3 in nitric acid, and ZrOCl2·8H2O in deionized water were prepared first. Before mixing these two solutions, Ta2O5 powders were added into the nitric acid solution. After mixing, NH4OH was added to the mixture to obtain precipitates. The precipitates were dried at 80°C for 48 hours and then heated at 700°C under flowing air for 2 hours. The dried powders or the dried powders with additional LiOH were ball milled with zirconia balls in a planetary ball milling machine (Across International, PQN2 gear-drive planetary ball mill) at 500 rpm for 4 hours with 2-propanol as the dispersing reagent."}, {"context": [{"line": 48, "text": "Phase stabilization.—The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with the same cover powder of 15Li are shown in Figure 2a. For 25Li sample, with 25 wt% excess LiOH, La2Zr2O7 (pyrochlore) phase was observed in addition to the cubic garnet phase, presumably because of the loss of lithium during sintering. When the excess LiOH was increased to 30 wt% or larger, a pure cubic garnet phase was obtained. Even when the excess LiOH was increased to 65 and 75 wt%, no peaks related to LiOH were observed for any of the samples, indicating that there was no LiOH left or the remaining amount of LiOH was too small to be detected by XRD."}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 48, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 407, "start": 43}], "text": "Phase stabilization.—The XRD patterns of LLZTO samples sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours covered with the same cover powder of 15Li are shown in Figure 2a. For 25Li sample, with 25 wt% excess LiOH, La2Zr2O7 (pyrochlore) phase was observed in addition to the cubic garnet phase, presumably because of the loss of lithium during sintering. When the excess LiOH was increased to 30 wt% or larger, a pure cubic garnet phase was obtained. Even when the excess LiOH was increased to 65 and 75 wt%, no peaks related to LiOH were observed for any of the samples, indicating that there was no LiOH left or the remaining amount of LiOH was too small to be detected by XRD."}], "ok": true, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO).*(Al|Ga|Ta|Nb|W).*(dop|cubic|stabil)", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 8, "truncated": true}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:11:48.577699+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:30:{"duration_ms": 12807.737, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 0, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 8, "query": "(Al|Ga|Ta|Nb|W).*(dop|substitut).*(LLZO|LLZTO|Li7La3Zr2O12)", "regex": true, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 47, "text": "An LLZO electrolyte was synthesized by using a solution-based method. The sample formed a Li+ conducting cubic phase without any extrinsic dopants at 900 ◦C. Remarkably, cubic materials were synthesized at 900 ◦C, which is lower than the conventional synthesis temperatures of 1000 - 1200 ◦C range, by using a solution-based method. The synthesized LLZO pellet revealed relatively non-uniform particles as a result of impurities and reduced density. As a result, the total ionic conductivity of LLZO was found to be 1.09 10−6 S/cm."}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 47, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 505, "start": 20}], "text": "An LLZO electrolyte was synthesized by using a solution-based method. The sample formed a Li+ conducting cubic phase without any extrinsic dopants at 900 ◦C. Remarkably, cubic materials were synthesized at 900 ◦C, which is lower than the conventional synthesis temperatures of 1000 - 1200 ◦C range, by using a solution-based method. The synthesized LLZO pellet revealed relatively non-uniform particles as a result of impurities and reduced density. As a result, the total ionic conductivity of LLZO was found to be 1.09 10−6 S/cm."}, {"context": [{"line": 106, "text": "10. Jin Y, McGinn PJ (2011) Al-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 synthesized by a polymerized complex method. Journal of Power Sources 196: 8683-8687."}], "document_id": "_ Crystallization of Starting Powders of Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; Ga &lt;sub_&gt;0.2&lt;_sub&g", "line": 106, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Crystallization of Starting Powders of Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; Ga &lt;sub_&gt;0.2&lt;_sub&g.md", "submatches": [{"end": 49, "start": 28}], "text": "10. Jin Y, McGinn PJ (2011) Al-doped Li7La3Zr2O12 synthesized by a polymerized complex method. Journal of Power Sources 196: 8683-8687."}, {"context": [{"line": 108, "text": "11. Chen RJ, Huang M, Huang WZ, Shen Y, Lin YH, Nan CW (2014) Effect of calcining and Al doping on structure and conductivity of Li7La3Zr2O12. Solid State Ionics 265: 7-12."}], "document_id": "_ Crystallization of Starting Powders of Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; Ga &lt;sub_&gt;0.2&lt;_sub&g", "line": 108, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Crystallization of Starting Powders of Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; Ga &lt;sub_&gt;0.2&lt;_sub&g.md", "submatches": [{"end": 141, "start": 28}], "text": "11. Chen RJ, Huang M, Huang WZ, Shen Y, Lin YH, Nan CW (2014) Effect of calcining and Al doping on structure and conductivity of Li7La3Zr2O12. Solid State Ionics 265: 7-12."}, {"context": [{"line": 110, "text": "12. Li C, Liu Y, He J, Brinkman KS (2017) Ga-substituted Li7La3Zr2O12: An investigation based on grain coarsening in garnet-type lithium ion conductors. Journal of Alloys and Compounds 695: 3744-3752."}], "document_id": "_ Crystallization of Starting Powders of Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; Ga &lt;sub_&gt;0.2&lt;_sub&g", "line": 110, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Crystallization of Starting Powders of Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; Ga &lt;sub_&gt;0.2&lt;_sub&g.md", "submatches": [{"end": 69, "start": 42}], "text": "12. Li C, Liu Y, He J, Brinkman KS (2017) Ga-substituted Li7La3Zr2O12: An investigation based on grain coarsening in garnet-type lithium ion conductors. Journal of Alloys and Compounds 695: 3744-3752."}, {"context": [{"line": 86, "text": "The Li-ion compounds Li5La3M2O12 (M = Ta, Nb), with garnet structure, were reported in 1988, and the structure was examined by x-ray diffraction, without knowing the Li distribution, due to limited experimental resolution of Li atoms.[157] However, this system did not attract much attention as a solid electrolyte until 2003, when Thangadurai and Weppner found that it is a good Li-ion conductor with high conductivity and a wide electrochemical window.[158] In contrast to the common perovskite or NASICON solid state electrolyte, the garnet family excludes Li-reducible Ti4+, so it can directly contact Li metal without damage, which has been viewed as its distinguishing advantage.[159] In 2004, Thangadurai and Weppner, together with Adams, used the bond valence sum method to verify the structure, and they minimized the mismatch of the Li-bond valence state as the isosurface to map the conduction pathways visually. It was concluded that Li ions occupy the octahedral sites other than tetrahedral vacancies.[160] The Li-ion occupation pattern is of great importance for understanding the diffusion mechanism but was not located precisely until 2006, by Cussen. It was revealed from neutron powder diffraction data that Li ions occupy both tetrahedral (80%) and octahedral sites (43%), and the latter are responsible for the mobile carriers via a clustering mechanism.[161] NMR results indicate that the Liion distribution depends on the annealing temperature; that is, the high annealing temperature implies that more Li ions can be quenched at the octahedral sites as mobile carriers, which leads to higher ionic conductivity.[162] In 2012, Han and Yusheng Zhao utilized variable-temperature neutron diffraction (HTND), combined with the maximum-entropy method (MEM) for data refining, to estimate the Li nuclear-density distribution and visualize the conduction pathway experimentally for the first time. These results confirm that displacement is directly driven by temperature.[163] More efforts in calculation have gradually elucidated the Li-ion diffusion mechanism; for example, conductivity and distinct mechanisms for different M elements and various Li concentrations,[164] structural phase transition,[165] concerted[166,167] or asynchronous phenomena,[168] point defects,[169] local structure and dynamics performance,[170] often in combination with experimental data. Other efforts focused on enhancing conductivity. Substituting and doping with lower-valence elements, e.g., replacing La by Sr[171] or Ba,[172] replacing M (= Nb, Ta) partly[173] or totally[174] by Zr, can increase Li content and thus improve conductivity. The highest conductivity found was 4 × 10−4 S/cm in Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO),[174] the most attractive composition so far. LLZO has cubic and tetragonal phases. Cubic phase LLZO has higher conductivity and can be synthesized by doping Al or Ga, or by pulsed laser annealing.[175–179] Cubic phase thin film can be obtained from epitaxial growth[180] or aerosol deposition (AD).[181] The latter method prevents the electrolyte from reacting with the active electrode by avoiding the annealing process, although the obtained electrolyte exhibits extremely low conductivity (1.0 × 10−8 S/cm@140 ◦C) and worse cyclic performance if applied in all-solid-state batteries. Nebulized spray pyrolysis results in a thin-film hybrid of both phases.[182] The disadvantages of LLZO used in sandwich-structure all-solidstate batteries include high interface resistance and poor cyclic stability,[183] both of which can be ameliorated by introducing a thin Nb interlayer between the electrolyte and cathode (LiCoO2).[184]"}], "document_id": "_ Lithium-ion transport in inorganic solid state electrolyte _ 10.1088_1674-1056_25_1_018211", "line": 86, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Lithium-ion transport in inorganic solid state electrolyte _ 10.1088_1674-1056_25_1_018211.md", "submatches": [{"end": 3418, "start": 38}], "text": "The Li-ion compounds Li5La3M2O12 (M = Ta, Nb), with garnet structure, were reported in 1988, and the structure was examined by x-ray diffraction, without knowing the Li distribution, due to limited experimental resolution of Li atoms.[157] However, this system did not attract much attention as a solid electrolyte until 2003, when Thangadurai and Weppner found that it is a good Li-ion conductor with high conductivity and a wide electrochemical window.[158] In contrast to the common perovskite or NASICON solid state electrolyte, the garnet family excludes Li-reducible Ti4+, so it can directly contact Li metal without damage, which has been viewed as its distinguishing advantage.[159] In 2004, Thangadurai and Weppner, together with Adams, used the bond valence sum method to verify the structure, and they minimized the mismatch of the Li-bond valence state as the isosurface to map the conduction pathways visually. It was concluded that Li ions occupy the octahedral sites other than tetrahedral vacancies.[160] The Li-ion occupation pattern is of great importance for understanding the diffusion mechanism but was not located precisely until 2006, by Cussen. It was revealed from neutron powder diffraction data that Li ions occupy both tetrahedral (80%) and octahedral sites (43%), and the latter are responsible for the mobile carriers via a clustering mechanism.[161] NMR results indicate that the Liion distribution depends on the annealing temperature; that is, the high annealing temperature implies that more Li ions can be quenched at the octahedral sites as mobile carriers, which leads to higher ionic conductivity.[162] In 2012, Han and Yusheng Zhao utilized variable-temperature neutron diffraction (HTND), combined with the maximum-entropy method (MEM) for data refining, to estimate the Li nuclear-density distribution and visualize the conduction pathway experimentally for the first time. These results confirm that displacement is directly driven by temperature.[163] More efforts in calculation have gradually elucidated the Li-ion diffusion mechanism; for example, conductivity and distinct mechanisms for different M elements and various Li concentrations,[164] structural phase transition,[165] concerted[166,167] or asynchronous phenomena,[168] point defects,[169] local structure and dynamics performance,[170] often in combination with experimental data. Other efforts focused on enhancing conductivity. Substituting and doping with lower-valence elements, e.g., replacing La by Sr[171] or Ba,[172] replacing M (= Nb, Ta) partly[173] or totally[174] by Zr, can increase Li content and thus improve conductivity. The highest conductivity found was 4 × 10−4 S/cm in Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO),[174] the most attractive composition so far. LLZO has cubic and tetragonal phases. Cubic phase LLZO has higher conductivity and can be synthesized by doping Al or Ga, or by pulsed laser annealing.[175–179] Cubic phase thin film can be obtained from epitaxial growth[180] or aerosol deposition (AD).[181] The latter method prevents the electrolyte from reacting with the active electrode by avoiding the annealing process, although the obtained electrolyte exhibits extremely low conductivity (1.0 × 10−8 S/cm@140 ◦C) and worse cyclic performance if applied in all-solid-state batteries. Nebulized spray pyrolysis results in a thin-film hybrid of both phases.[182] The disadvantages of LLZO used in sandwich-structure all-solidstate batteries include high interface resistance and poor cyclic stability,[183] both of which can be ameliorated by introducing a thin Nb interlayer between the electrolyte and cathode (LiCoO2).[184]"}, {"context": [{"line": 197, "text": "7. Murugan R, Ramakumar S, Janani N. High conductive yttrium doped Li7La3Zr2O12 cubic lithium garnet. Electrochem Commun. 2011;13(12):1373 1375.   "}], "document_id": "Achieving high performance for aluminum stabilized Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__su", "line": 197, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> high performance for aluminum stabilized Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__su.md", "submatches": [{"end": 79, "start": 7}], "text": "7. Murugan R, Ramakumar S, Janani N. High conductive yttrium doped Li7La3Zr2O12 cubic lithium garnet. Electrochem Commun. 2011;13(12):1373 1375.   "}, {"context": [{"line": 200, "text": "10. Chen RJ, Huang M, Huang WZ, Shen Y, Lin YH, Nan CW. Effect of calcining and Al doping on structure and conductivity of Li7La3Zr2O12. Solid State Ion. 2014;265:7 12.   "}], "document_id": "Achieving high performance for aluminum stabilized Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__su", "line": 200, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> high performance for aluminum stabilized Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__su.md", "submatches": [{"end": 135, "start": 28}], "text": "10. Chen RJ, Huang M, Huang WZ, Shen Y, Lin YH, Nan CW. Effect of calcining and Al doping on structure and conductivity of Li7La3Zr2O12. Solid State Ion. 2014;265:7 12.   "}, {"context": [{"line": 202, "text": "12. Düvel A, Kuhn A, Robben L, Wilkening M, Heitjans P. Mechanosynthesis of solid electrolytes: preparation, characterization, and Li ion transport properties of garnet type Al doped Li7La3Zr2O12crystallizing with cubic symmetry. J Phys Chem C. 2012;116(29):15192 15202.   "}], "document_id": "Achieving high performance for aluminum stabilized Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__su", "line": 202, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> high performance for aluminum stabilized Li_br __ _sub__7__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__su.md", "submatches": [{"end": 196, "start": 32}], "text": "12. Düvel A, Kuhn A, Robben L, Wilkening M, Heitjans P. Mechanosynthesis of solid electrolytes: preparation, characterization, and Li ion transport properties of garnet type Al doped Li7La3Zr2O12crystallizing with cubic symmetry. J Phys Chem C. 2012;116(29):15192 15202.   "}], "ok": true, "query": "(Al|Ga|Ta|Nb|W).*(dop|substitut).*(LLZO|LLZTO|Li7La3Zr2O12)", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 8, "truncated": true}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:12:01.393333+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:31:{"duration_ms": 4281.23, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 0, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 8, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO|Li7La3Zr2O12).*(calcination|calcined|sintering|sintered|densification)", "regex": true, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 46, "text": "Li6.4Gao.2La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) samples were prepared in this study via solid state reaction. The starting materials used were LiCO3 (>99.9%, Aldrich), Ga2O3 (>99.9%, Aldrich), La2O3 (>99.9%, Aldrich) and ZrO2 (>99.9%, Aldrich). The appropriate amount of starting materials were mixed thoroughly in a pestle mortar and then pre-dried at 150 C for 30 mins in an electric vacuum drying oven. The dried mixture was annealed in a tube furnace (GSL-1700X) at 850C for 4h under the flow of dry air to remove any volatile components. The resultant powders were ground thoroughly again in the pestle mortar. Then the annealed powders were loaded into an attrition mill (JM-1L) to achieve a thorough mix and reduce the particle size. The duration for the attrition milling was varied in the range of 8 h - 96 h. Ethanol was used as the solvent and the ratio of materials and balls was 1:3 in this study. And the rotation speed was maintained 600 rpm throughout the milling process. The attrition-milled powders were dried in an electric vacuum drying oven and then thermally treated again in the tube furnace at 850C for 4h under the flow of dry air. At this stage, the obtained powders were ready for the preparation of LLZO pellets. These obtained powders were pressed into a small coin in a 15mm diameter by applying a pressure of 10MPa for 10mins. Finally, the green pellets were sintered in the tube furnace at 1230C for 6h under the flow of dry air. All the sintered pellets were polished and painted with silver paste on both sides for electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) analysis."}], "document_id": "_ Crystallization of Starting Powders of Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; Ga &lt;sub_&gt;0.2&lt;_sub&g", "line": 46, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Crystallization of Starting Powders of Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.4&lt;_sub&gt; Ga &lt;sub_&gt;0.2&lt;_sub&g.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1458, "start": 21}], "text": "Li6.4Gao.2La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) samples were prepared in this study via solid state reaction. The starting materials used were LiCO3 (>99.9%, Aldrich), Ga2O3 (>99.9%, Aldrich), La2O3 (>99.9%, Aldrich) and ZrO2 (>99.9%, Aldrich). The appropriate amount of starting materials were mixed thoroughly in a pestle mortar and then pre-dried at 150 C for 30 mins in an electric vacuum drying oven. The dried mixture was annealed in a tube furnace (GSL-1700X) at 850C for 4h under the flow of dry air to remove any volatile components. The resultant powders were ground thoroughly again in the pestle mortar. Then the annealed powders were loaded into an attrition mill (JM-1L) to achieve a thorough mix and reduce the particle size. The duration for the attrition milling was varied in the range of 8 h - 96 h. Ethanol was used as the solvent and the ratio of materials and balls was 1:3 in this study. And the rotation speed was maintained 600 rpm throughout the milling process. The attrition-milled powders were dried in an electric vacuum drying oven and then thermally treated again in the tube furnace at 850C for 4h under the flow of dry air. At this stage, the obtained powders were ready for the preparation of LLZO pellets. These obtained powders were pressed into a small coin in a 15mm diameter by applying a pressure of 10MPa for 10mins. Finally, the green pellets were sintered in the tube furnace at 1230C for 6h under the flow of dry air. All the sintered pellets were polished and painted with silver paste on both sides for electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) analysis."}, {"context": [{"line": 10, "text": "Keywords: Li7La3Zr2O12, Reducing calcination temperature, Solution-based synthesis   "}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 10, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 44, "start": 10}], "text": "Keywords: Li7La3Zr2O12, Reducing calcination temperature, Solution-based synthesis   "}, {"context": [{"line": 17, "text": "In general, the solid electrolytes that have been studied extensively are mainly composed of sulfides and oxides; Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is probably one of the most promising candidates to date for use in all-solid-state Liion batteries on account of its excellent chemical and electrochemical stabilities while exhibiting high Li-ion conductivity compared to those of other garnet structure conductors such as Li5La3M2O12 (M = Nb, Ta) [5,11– 13]. LLZO is a garnet-type material that has two stable phases: tetragonal and cubic. Although the tetragonal phase can be obtained at lower synthesis temperatures than the cubic phase, the cubic phase has a bulk conductivity of ∼ 10−3 S/cm compared to ∼ 10−6 S/cm for tetragonal LLZO [14]. For high ionic conductivity, the LLZO solid electrolytes should maintain a stable cubic structure, which usually require several calcination steps and long sintering times at temperatures greater than 1000 - 1200 ◦C [15–17]. However, during this hightemperature thermal process, significant Li loss unavoidably occurs, which often results in a structural collapse of LLZO accompanied by the formation of second phases, thereby reducing the ionic conductivity [18]. Doping with external elements is a well-known method to stabilize the cubic phase at lower temperatures compared to the conventional 1000 - 1200 ◦C range and increase Li+ conductivity by introducing additional Li-ion vacancies, which balance the ionic charge [19–24]. However, doping has the disadvantage of being difficult to control; therefore, finding other ways to stabilize the cubic phase at room temperature is of at most importance."}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 17, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 906, "start": 114}], "text": "In general, the solid electrolytes that have been studied extensively are mainly composed of sulfides and oxides; Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) is probably one of the most promising candidates to date for use in all-solid-state Liion batteries on account of its excellent chemical and electrochemical stabilities while exhibiting high Li-ion conductivity compared to those of other garnet structure conductors such as Li5La3M2O12 (M = Nb, Ta) [5,11– 13]. LLZO is a garnet-type material that has two stable phases: tetragonal and cubic. Although the tetragonal phase can be obtained at lower synthesis temperatures than the cubic phase, the cubic phase has a bulk conductivity of ∼ 10−3 S/cm compared to ∼ 10−6 S/cm for tetragonal LLZO [14]. For high ionic conductivity, the LLZO solid electrolytes should maintain a stable cubic structure, which usually require several calcination steps and long sintering times at temperatures greater than 1000 - 1200 ◦C [15–17]. However, during this hightemperature thermal process, significant Li loss unavoidably occurs, which often results in a structural collapse of LLZO accompanied by the formation of second phases, thereby reducing the ionic conductivity [18]. Doping with external elements is a well-known method to stabilize the cubic phase at lower temperatures compared to the conventional 1000 - 1200 ◦C range and increase Li+ conductivity by introducing additional Li-ion vacancies, which balance the ionic charge [19–24]. However, doping has the disadvantage of being difficult to control; therefore, finding other ways to stabilize the cubic phase at room temperature is of at most importance."}, {"context": [{"line": 23, "text": "In order to obtain LLZO, we prepared the precursor by mixing a nitrate-based sol into a PVP solution. Lithium nitrate (LiNO3, Sigma-Aldrich, 98%), lanthanum(III) nitrate hexahydrate (La(NO3)3 6H2O, Sigma-Aldrich, 99.99%), and zirconium nitrate oxide dihydrate (ZrO(NO3)2·2H2O, KANTO, 99%) were prepared so that the Li:La:Zr ratio was 7:3:2, with 10% excess Li used to compensate for the loss occurring during calcination. The solution was pre-calcined at 350 ◦C for 10 h in air, followed calcination at 900 ◦C for 5 h in air. After calcination, the powders were pressed under 40MPa pressure and sintered at 1180 ◦C for 20 h in air. Thermogravimetry (TG) and differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) of the as-prepared precursor were performed in the temperature range from 25 to 1000 ◦C in flowing air. The crystallinity of the sample was examined using X-ray diffraction (XRD; Siemens D-5000) in the 2θ range from 10 - 70◦ with Cu-Kα radiation (λ = 1.5406 ˚A). The morphology of the obtained samples was observed through scanning electron microscopy (SEM; Quanta 300) and atomic force microscopy (AFM; Multimode N3-AM). Impedance spectroscopy was carried out at room temperature. Frequencies were varied from 0.1 Hz to 7 MHz, and the alternating current signal had an amplitude of 10 mV. Nyquist plots (Z′ vs Z′′) were drawn and analyzed using the Z-plot and the Zview packages."}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 23, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 608, "start": 19}], "text": "In order to obtain LLZO, we prepared the precursor by mixing a nitrate-based sol into a PVP solution. Lithium nitrate (LiNO3, Sigma-Aldrich, 98%), lanthanum(III) nitrate hexahydrate (La(NO3)3 6H2O, Sigma-Aldrich, 99.99%), and zirconium nitrate oxide dihydrate (ZrO(NO3)2·2H2O, KANTO, 99%) were prepared so that the Li:La:Zr ratio was 7:3:2, with 10% excess Li used to compensate for the loss occurring during calcination. The solution was pre-calcined at 350 ◦C for 10 h in air, followed calcination at 900 ◦C for 5 h in air. After calcination, the powders were pressed under 40MPa pressure and sintered at 1180 ◦C for 20 h in air. Thermogravimetry (TG) and differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) of the as-prepared precursor were performed in the temperature range from 25 to 1000 ◦C in flowing air. The crystallinity of the sample was examined using X-ray diffraction (XRD; Siemens D-5000) in the 2θ range from 10 - 70◦ with Cu-Kα radiation (λ = 1.5406 ˚A). The morphology of the obtained samples was observed through scanning electron microscopy (SEM; Quanta 300) and atomic force microscopy (AFM; Multimode N3-AM). Impedance spectroscopy was carried out at room temperature. Frequencies were varied from 0.1 Hz to 7 MHz, and the alternating current signal had an amplitude of 10 mV. Nyquist plots (Z′ vs Z′′) were drawn and analyzed using the Z-plot and the Zview packages."}, {"context": [{"line": 8, "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 8, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 977, "start": 12}], "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}, {"context": [{"line": 24, "text": "When the simple method of cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in air is used, additives are typically needed to obtain high density, details of which could be found in Table I.51–55 Additions of La2Zr2O7 and Li2O to Ta-doped LLZO and Li4SiO4 to Al-doped LLZO have led to improvements of 6–17% in density which increased conductivity 3–5 times.51–53 Even larger increases in densities have been reported with additions of LiOH, Li3BO3 and LiBO2 to Al- and Nb-doped LLZO, but the conductivities are all smaller than 1 × 10−4 S/cm.51,54,55 Samples with conductivities larger than 1 10−4 S/cm were sintered at temperature above 1100°C or prepared with cold uniaxial pressing.51–53"}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 24, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 616, "start": 228}], "text": "When the simple method of cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in air is used, additives are typically needed to obtain high density, details of which could be found in Table I.51–55 Additions of La2Zr2O7 and Li2O to Ta-doped LLZO and Li4SiO4 to Al-doped LLZO have led to improvements of 6–17% in density which increased conductivity 3–5 times.51–53 Even larger increases in densities have been reported with additions of LiOH, Li3BO3 and LiBO2 to Al- and Nb-doped LLZO, but the conductivities are all smaller than 1 × 10−4 S/cm.51,54,55 Samples with conductivities larger than 1 10−4 S/cm were sintered at temperature above 1100°C or prepared with cold uniaxial pressing.51–53"}, {"context": [{"line": 26, "text": "In this work, Ta-doped LLZO (LLZTO) was synthesized through a co-precipitation method with a low sintering temperature of 1100°C. The effects of a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which is created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder, on the phase stabilization, densification and Li+ conductivity were examined. LiOH was chosen as the additive since it is one of the starting materials."}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 26, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 286, "start": 23}], "text": "In this work, Ta-doped LLZO (LLZTO) was synthesized through a co-precipitation method with a low sintering temperature of 1100°C. The effects of a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which is created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder, on the phase stabilization, densification and Li+ conductivity were examined. LiOH was chosen as the additive since it is one of the starting materials."}, {"context": [{"line": 32, "text": "LLZTO synthesis.—Tantalum-doped Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) was prepared by the co-precipitation method. LiOH (Alfa, anhydrous, 98%), La(OH)3 (Alfa, 99.95% REO), ZrOCl2·8H2O (Alfa, 98%), Ta2O5 (Alfa, 99%, metal basis), nitric acid (Alfa, 67–70%) and NH4OH (Alfa, 5.0N) were used as starting materials. Different amounts of excess LiOH were added during different steps to compensate for the loss of lithium during sintering. All samples in this paper are named as xLi, where x refers to the total amount of excess LiOH. Transparent solutions of LiOH and La(OH)3 in nitric acid, and ZrOCl2·8H2O in deionized water were prepared first. Before mixing these two solutions, Ta2O5 powders were added into the nitric acid solution. After mixing, NH4OH was added to the mixture to obtain precipitates. The precipitates were dried at 80°C for 48 hours and then heated at 700°C under flowing air for 2 hours. The dried powders or the dried powders with additional LiOH were ball milled with zirconia balls in a planetary ball milling machine (Across International, PQN2 gear-drive planetary ball mill) at 500 rpm for 4 hours with 2-propanol as the dispersing reagent."}], "document_id": "Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z", "line": 32, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li_br __ _sub__6.75__sub__br __ La_br __ _sub__3__sub__br __ Z.md", "submatches": [{"end": 433, "start": 0}], "text": "LLZTO synthesis.—Tantalum-doped Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) was prepared by the co-precipitation method. LiOH (Alfa, anhydrous, 98%), La(OH)3 (Alfa, 99.95% REO), ZrOCl2·8H2O (Alfa, 98%), Ta2O5 (Alfa, 99%, metal basis), nitric acid (Alfa, 67–70%) and NH4OH (Alfa, 5.0N) were used as starting materials. Different amounts of excess LiOH were added during different steps to compensate for the loss of lithium during sintering. All samples in this paper are named as xLi, where x refers to the total amount of excess LiOH. Transparent solutions of LiOH and La(OH)3 in nitric acid, and ZrOCl2·8H2O in deionized water were prepared first. Before mixing these two solutions, Ta2O5 powders were added into the nitric acid solution. After mixing, NH4OH was added to the mixture to obtain precipitates. The precipitates were dried at 80°C for 48 hours and then heated at 700°C under flowing air for 2 hours. The dried powders or the dried powders with additional LiOH were ball milled with zirconia balls in a planetary ball milling machine (Across International, PQN2 gear-drive planetary ball mill) at 500 rpm for 4 hours with 2-propanol as the dispersing reagent."}], "ok": true, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO|Li7La3Zr2O12).*(calcination|calcined|sintering|sintered|densification)", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 8, "truncated": true}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:12:05.683551+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:32:{"duration_ms": 13936.03, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 0, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 8, "query": "(LLZO|LLZTO|Li7La3Zr2O12).*(SPS|spark plasma|hot press|grain boundary|grain growth|porosity)", "regex": true, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 37, "text": "Figure 4(a) shows the Nyquist plots obtained from AC impedance spectroscopy analyses of the LLZO pellets. The ratios of the bulk and the grain boundary resistances to the total resistance were determined by fitting the experimental data to the equivalent circuit shown in Fig. 4(b). The ionic conductivity of the sample was calculated as follows: σ = (ALR), where L is the length of the pellet, A is the surface area of the pellet, and R is the Ohmic resistance determined from the AC impedance. The bulk, grain boundary and total ionic conductivities determined in this fashion are 2.91 10−5 S/cm, 1.13 10−6 S/cm and 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, respectively. The equivalent circuit was of the form RB/CB+RGB/CGB+Wo, where RB is the bulk resistance, RGB is the grain boundary resistance, and Wo is the Warburg element [5,25]. The calculated total Li+ conductivity of LLZO is 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, which is higher than that reported by Kokal et al. [26] (3.12 10−7 S/cm), but is lower than the value for other cubic materials [15, 17]. This is a result of the impurity phase and the many voids observed from the XRD results and the SEM images."}], "document_id": "_ Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr", "line": 37, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Use of Polyvinylpyrrolidone as a Chelating Agent to Reduce the Calcination Temperature of Li7La3Zr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 773, "start": 92}], "text": "Figure 4(a) shows the Nyquist plots obtained from AC impedance spectroscopy analyses of the LLZO pellets. The ratios of the bulk and the grain boundary resistances to the total resistance were determined by fitting the experimental data to the equivalent circuit shown in Fig. 4(b). The ionic conductivity of the sample was calculated as follows: σ = (ALR), where L is the length of the pellet, A is the surface area of the pellet, and R is the Ohmic resistance determined from the AC impedance. The bulk, grain boundary and total ionic conductivities determined in this fashion are 2.91 10−5 S/cm, 1.13 10−6 S/cm and 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, respectively. The equivalent circuit was of the form RB/CB+RGB/CGB+Wo, where RB is the bulk resistance, RGB is the grain boundary resistance, and Wo is the Warburg element [5,25]. The calculated total Li+ conductivity of LLZO is 1.09 × 10−6 S/cm, which is higher than that reported by Kokal et al. [26] (3.12 10−7 S/cm), but is lower than the value for other cubic materials [15, 17]. This is a result of the impurity phase and the many voids observed from the XRD results and the SEM images."}, {"context": [{"line": 8, "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 8, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1014, "start": 12}], "text": "Garnet-type Li7La3Zr2O12 (LLZO) has attracted much attention because it shows promising properties for all-solid-state lithium-ion batteries. A dense microstructure is needed to obtain a high Li+ conductivity, as porous microstructures result in much lower Li+ conductivities. In this work, tantalum-doped cubic garnet-type Li6.75La3Zr1.75Ta0.25O12 (LLZTO) with a relative density of 92% was prepared by a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in a lithium-rich air atmosphere, which was created by the addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder. This LiOH-containing cover powder (15Li + LiOH) has been shown to improve densification and increase the room temperature total conductivity to 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm with an activation energy of 0.2 eV. This 15Li + LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary."}, {"context": [{"line": 64, "text": "Room temperature impedance spectra for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with cover powders of 15Li or 15Li LiOH are shown in Figure 5. As shown in the typical Nyquist plot for LLZTO pellets in Figure 5, the spectra were fit to an equivalent circuit of L(R CPE )(R CPE ), where L, R and CPE are the inductor, resistor and the constant phase element, and the subscript t and e refer to the total and electrode contribution, respectively. The inductive contribution is attributed to the wires connected to the sample and is not used for the sample properties. The first semicircle is attributed to the total resistance (Rt). The total conductivity is calculated as σtotal t / (A × Rt), where t is the sample thickness and A is the cross-sectional area of the sample. The grain and grain boundary contributions overlapped with each other and cannot be separated, which is consistent with other works.53,56,57 The RT total Li+ conductivity for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with 15Li or 15Li LiOH cover powder are tabulated in Table II. The RT total conductivity of 30Li with 15Li cover powders is 2.0 × 10−5 S/cm. It is significantly lower than that sintered with 15Li + LiOH, which is 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm. Compared to the 30 wt% LiOH samples, samples with higher lithium content (65Li) showed a lower RT σtotal when the cover was 15Li, but similar RT σtotal when the cover contained LiOH. 75Li also showed a similar RT σtotal with 30wt% and 65 wt% LiOH samples when the cover contained LiOH, which indicates that the amount of Li content in the pellets does not affect RT σtotal once it is over 30 wt%. The higher conductivity is consistent with the higher relative density in the samples sintered with 15Li  LiOH cover."}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 64, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 816, "start": 198}], "text": "Room temperature impedance spectra for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with cover powders of 15Li or 15Li LiOH are shown in Figure 5. As shown in the typical Nyquist plot for LLZTO pellets in Figure 5, the spectra were fit to an equivalent circuit of L(R CPE )(R CPE ), where L, R and CPE are the inductor, resistor and the constant phase element, and the subscript t and e refer to the total and electrode contribution, respectively. The inductive contribution is attributed to the wires connected to the sample and is not used for the sample properties. The first semicircle is attributed to the total resistance (Rt). The total conductivity is calculated as σtotal t / (A × Rt), where t is the sample thickness and A is the cross-sectional area of the sample. The grain and grain boundary contributions overlapped with each other and cannot be separated, which is consistent with other works.53,56,57 The RT total Li+ conductivity for 30Li and 65Li pellets sintered at 1100°C for 16 hours with 15Li or 15Li LiOH cover powder are tabulated in Table II. The RT total conductivity of 30Li with 15Li cover powders is 2.0 × 10−5 S/cm. It is significantly lower than that sintered with 15Li + LiOH, which is 6.9 × 10−4 S/cm. Compared to the 30 wt% LiOH samples, samples with higher lithium content (65Li) showed a lower RT σtotal when the cover was 15Li, but similar RT σtotal when the cover contained LiOH. 75Li also showed a similar RT σtotal with 30wt% and 65 wt% LiOH samples when the cover contained LiOH, which indicates that the amount of Li content in the pellets does not affect RT σtotal once it is over 30 wt%. The higher conductivity is consistent with the higher relative density in the samples sintered with 15Li  LiOH cover."}, {"context": [{"line": 112, "text": "The addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder used to minimize vaporization from the LLZTO pellet during the sintering has been shown to improve densification and increase Li+ conductivity, such that a relative density of 92% and a total Li+ conductivity of 6.9 10−4 S/cm was achieved through a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in air. The 15Li LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary. The increased aluminum content promoted phase stabilization and densification, but the Al-containing phase also limits LLZTO phase continuity. The Li+ conductivity value reached 3.5 10−4 S/cm at a sintering time of 8 hours and did not change significantly between 8 and 32 hours. Further study is needed to determine the role of aluminum and chlorine on the densification and conductivity of LLZTO by using a zirconium salt that does not contain chlorine, using different levels of aluminum by adding Al salt in the co-precipitation process, and sintering with aluminum-free crucibles."}], "document_id": "_ Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;", "line": 112, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Densification of Ta-Doped Garnet-Type Li &lt;sub_&gt;6.75&lt;_sub&gt; La &lt;sub_&gt;3&lt;_sub&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 608, "start": 28}], "text": "The addition of LiOH to the LLZTO cover powder used to minimize vaporization from the LLZTO pellet during the sintering has been shown to improve densification and increase Li+ conductivity, such that a relative density of 92% and a total Li+ conductivity of 6.9 10−4 S/cm was achieved through a simple co-precipitation method with cold uniaxial pressing and sintering in air. The 15Li LiOH cover powder results in the formation of an Al-containing interparticle phase, which facilitates the densification of LLZTO. The aluminum concentration increases during sintering and is located in the grain boundary. The increased aluminum content promoted phase stabilization and densification, but the Al-containing phase also limits LLZTO phase continuity. The Li+ conductivity value reached 3.5 10−4 S/cm at a sintering time of 8 hours and did not change significantly between 8 and 32 hours. Further study is needed to determine the role of aluminum and chlorine on the densification and conductivity of LLZTO by using a zirconium salt that does not contain chlorine, using different levels of aluminum by adding Al salt in the co-precipitation process, and sint