workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:33:{"duration_ms": 45015.144, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 2, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 12, "query": "Li volatilization", "regex": false, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 21, "text": "![](images/0cc06a28ed13752cca40fed7da036036eee82675cc6f57dc48ae1dc0805fd3e2.jpg)"}, {"line": 22, "text": ""}, {"line": 23, "text": "The conventional solid-state method was used to prepare the Li-based garnet-type ceramic materials. As-received, high purity precursor powders including LiNO, (99%, Alfa Aesar), La2O3 (99%, Alfa Aesar), Gd2O3 (99%, Alfa Aesar) and Nb2O5 (99%, Alfa Aesar) were used. LaO3 and GdO3 were dried in a furnace at 900 C for 8h prior to the mixing of the materials. Stoichiometric amounts of the required precursors were used to prepare Gd-doped Lis+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). A 10 wt. % excess of Li salt was added to compensate for Li volatilization during the high temperature sintering stages. All syntheses were carried out in alumina crucibles, in air. The powders were mixed with isopropanol in zirconia bowls with zirconia balls, using a ball mill (Pullverisette, Frtisch, Germany) for 6h at 200 min'. The direction of rotation was changed every hour to ensure even mixing. Powders were dried in air, placed in crucibles and heated at 700 C for 6 h. Sintered powders were ball-milled once more under the previously mentioned conditions. After ball-milling, dried powders were pressed into pellets (\\~1cm diameter, \\~2 cm length) using an iso-static pressure of 200 kN for 5 minutes (isostatic press, P.O Weber). The pellets were covered with some of the powder to help reduce LiO volatilization, then sintered at 900 C for 24 h. Powder was also placed on the bottom of the alumina crucible to minimize Al contamination. A second sintering step (1100 C for 6 h) was used to improve the density of the samples."}, {"line": 24, "text": ""}, {"line": 25, "text": "# Characterization of Li5+2xLa3Nb2-xGd,O12 (0  x  0.45)"}], "document_id": "Evaluation on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like", "line": 23, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like.md", "submatches": [{"end": 546, "start": 529}], "text": "The conventional solid-state method was used to prepare the Li-based garnet-type ceramic materials. As-received, high purity precursor powders including LiNO, (99%, Alfa Aesar), La2O3 (99%, Alfa Aesar), Gd2O3 (99%, Alfa Aesar) and Nb2O5 (99%, Alfa Aesar) were used. LaO3 and GdO3 were dried in a furnace at 900 C for 8h prior to the mixing of the materials. Stoichiometric amounts of the required precursors were used to prepare Gd-doped Lis+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). A 10 wt. % excess of Li salt was added to compensate for Li volatilization during the high temperature sintering stages. All syntheses were carried out in alumina crucibles, in air. The powders were mixed with isopropanol in zirconia bowls with zirconia balls, using a ball mill (Pullverisette, Frtisch, Germany) for 6h at 200 min'. The direction of rotation was changed every hour to ensure even mixing. Powders were dried in air, placed in crucibles and heated at 700 C for 6 h. Sintered powders were ball-milled once more under the previously mentioned conditions. After ball-milling, dried powders were pressed into pellets (\\~1cm diameter, \\~2 cm length) using an iso-static pressure of 200 kN for 5 minutes (isostatic press, P.O Weber). The pellets were covered with some of the powder to help reduce LiO volatilization, then sintered at 900 C for 24 h. Powder was also placed on the bottom of the alumina crucible to minimize Al contamination. A second sintering step (1100 C for 6 h) was used to improve the density of the samples."}, {"context": [{"line": 47, "text": "Li2CO3 (3.088 g), TiO2 (8.104 g) and graphene solution (9.18 mL) dispersed in ethanol were mixed by sand milling at 500 rpm for 0.5 h and 2000 rpm for 2.5 h using zirconia (0.2 mm) as the milling media. The mixture was dried at 105 °C for 3 h. The precursor powders were calcined at 800 °C for 5 h in Ar to obtain the LTO/graphene composite (denoted GLTO-1). The molar ratio of Li:Ti was 4:5 and excess Li (5%)"}, {"line": 48, "text": ""}, {"line": 49, "text": "was added to compensate for Li volatilization during calcination. To explore the effect of graphene on the structure and electrochemical properties of the Li4Ti5O12/graphene composites, LTO/graphene composites with the other two increasing mass percentages of graphene (the content of graphene solution is 16.36 mL and 36.72 mL respectively) were prepared, and the obtained materials were denoted GLTO-2 and GLTO-3, respectively. For comparison, pure LTO was prepared under the same conditions without graphene addition."}, {"line": 50, "text": ""}, {"line": 51, "text": "Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD, Ultima IV, Rigaku, Japan) with Cu K radiation was used to identify the phase composition of all samples. The diffraction patterns were collected at room temperature by scanning stepwise between 2θ values of 10 to 90° at 0.02° per 10 s. The mass percentage of graphene in the GLTO composites was determined by thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA, STA 449 F3 Jupiter, NETZSCH, Germany) with a heating rate of 10 °C min−1 in air from 30 to 750 °C. The material morphology was studied by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, SU 8020, Hitachi, Japan) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2100 plus, JEOL, Japan). The surface composition of the GLTO-2 composite was examined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, PHI5600, Physical Electronics, USA). Raman measurements were conducted on a Raman microscope (RM2000, Renishaw, England) with a 633 nm Ar-ion laser and charge-coupled device detector."}], "document_id": "A facile one-step solid-state synthesis of a Li4Ti5O12_graphene composite as an anode material for h", "line": 49, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> facile one-step solid-state synthesis of a Li4Ti5O12_graphene composite as an anode material for h.md", "submatches": [{"end": 45, "start": 28}], "text": "was added to compensate for Li volatilization during calcination. To explore the effect of graphene on the structure and electrochemical properties of the Li4Ti5O12/graphene composites, LTO/graphene composites with the other two increasing mass percentages of graphene (the content of graphene solution is 16.36 mL and 36.72 mL respectively) were prepared, and the obtained materials were denoted GLTO-2 and GLTO-3, respectively. For comparison, pure LTO was prepared under the same conditions without graphene addition."}, {"context": [{"line": 41, "text": "The 700 °C-fired powder was dominantly composed of fine primary particles smaller than 5 μm and their secondary particles (agglomerates) before ball-milling (Fig. S1(a)). After ball-milling (Fig. S1(b) and (c)), the size of primary particles decreased to about 0.1–1 μm. On the other hand, the 900 °C-fired powder contained coarse particles of about 10 μm in addition to several aggregates of primary particles with a size of few micrometer before ball-milling (Fig. S1(d)). After ball-milling (Fig. S1(e) and (f)), the coarse particles and aggregates were pulverized to form agglomerates of primary particles with a size of 0.2–5 μm. Hereafter, ceramics prepared from the powders with the primary particle size of 0.1–1 μm and 0.2–5 μm are called LLZ_LG and LLG_SG, respectively."}, {"line": 42, "text": ""}, {"line": 43, "text": "The powders were pressed into pellets with a diameter of 10.0 mm, and then isostatically cold pressed under a pressure of 120 MPa. They were sintered in Al2O3 crucibles for 20 h at 1230 °C in air. The heating rate was 10 °C min−1 from room temperature to 600 °C and 1 °C min−1 from 600 °C to 1230 °C. The cooling rate was 2 °C min−1. In order to avoid Li volatilization during sintering, the pellets were covered with mother powders with the same composition as the pellets. The mother powders also played a role in preventing mutual diffusion of elements between Al O crucibles and the LLZ pellets. The amount of the mother powders was 6 times larger in weight than that of LLZ pellets. After the sintering, LLZ surfaces were conditioned by an automated polisher (ML182, MKL-250, Maruto Instrument Co., Ltd.) using 9 μm, 3 μm, and 1 μm diamond paste in air. Immediately after polishing, the samples were transferred to an Ar glovebox. Apparent densities and bulk densities of LLZ sintered bodies were respectively determined by pycnometry (AccuPycII1340, SHIMADZU Corp.) and shape-measurement. Relative densities were calculated by dividing apparent densities or bulk densities by the reported theoretical density (5.1 g cm−3) [10]. The microstructures of the LLZ sintered bodies were observed by SEMEDX. The acceleration voltage ranged from 1 to 10 kV and XRD measurements (SmartLab, Rigaku Corp.) were performed with CuKα radiation. The conductivities of the LLZ ceramics were characterized by the AC impedance method using an impedance analyzer (SI-1260, Solartron) at 25–80 °C. Au was sputtered onto both surfaces of the pellets as blocking electrodes. High frequency impedance measurements were conducted from 100 MHz to 1 Hz at −20 °C using an analyzer (E4990A,"}, {"line": 44, "text": ""}, {"line": 45, "text": "![](images/d576a9a39289efdbf32d1b69f3467ff09ee787f6eba3b3a7d24fff74cfd949b1.jpg)  "}], "document_id": "_ Microstructure and conductivity of Al-substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 ceramics with different grain sizes", "line": 43, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Microstructure and conductivity of Al-substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 ceramics with different grain sizes.md", "submatches": [{"end": 382, "start": 365}], "text": "The powders were pressed into pellets with a diameter of 10.0 mm, and then isostatically cold pressed under a pressure of 120 MPa. They were sintered in Al2O3 crucibles for 20 h at 1230 °C in air. The heating rate was 10 °C min−1 from room temperature to 600 °C and 1 °C min−1 from 600 °C to 1230 °C. The cooling rate was 2 °C min−1. In order to avoid Li volatilization during sintering, the pellets were covered with mother powders with the same composition as the pellets. The mother powders also played a role in preventing mutual diffusion of elements between Al O crucibles and the LLZ pellets. The amount of the mother powders was 6 times larger in weight than that of LLZ pellets. After the sintering, LLZ surfaces were conditioned by an automated polisher (ML182, MKL-250, Maruto Instrument Co., Ltd.) using 9 μm, 3 μm, and 1 μm diamond paste in air. Immediately after polishing, the samples were transferred to an Ar glovebox. Apparent densities and bulk densities of LLZ sintered bodies were respectively determined by pycnometry (AccuPycII1340, SHIMADZU Corp.) and shape-measurement. Relative densities were calculated by dividing apparent densities or bulk densities by the reported theoretical density (5.1 g cm−3) [10]. The microstructures of the LLZ sintered bodies were observed by SEMEDX. The acceleration voltage ranged from 1 to 10 kV and XRD measurements (SmartLab, Rigaku Corp.) were performed with CuKα radiation. The conductivities of the LLZ ceramics were characterized by the AC impedance method using an impedance analyzer (SI-1260, Solartron) at 25–80 °C. Au was sputtered onto both surfaces of the pellets as blocking electrodes. High frequency impedance measurements were conducted from 100 MHz to 1 Hz at −20 °C using an analyzer (E4990A,"}, {"context": [{"line": 30, "text": "# 2.  Experiment procedures"}, {"line": 31, "text": ""}, {"line": 32, "text": "The LMZxTO ceramics were prepared by solid state reaction technic. The raw materials of Mg(OH)2·4MgCO3·5H2O (>99.5%), Li2CO3(99.9%), Ta2O5(99.9%), and ZnO(99.9%) were weighed according to the stoichiometry of Li3Mg2-xZnxTaO6 (x=0\\~0.5). These raw powders were ball milled in alcohol for 7h in nylon jars with zirconia balls. After drying, the mixtures were calcined at 1000ºC for 4h, then reground for 3h, dried and mixed with an 6wt% PVA as a binder. The resultant powders were axially pressed into cylinders with diameter of 15 mm and 7.5mm in thickness under the pressure of 20 MPa. To control Li volatilization, these pellets were muffled in ZrO2 powders and Li2CO3 were used to provide Li-rich atmosphere in high temperature circumstances. Then the pellets were sintered from 1230ºC to 1310ºC."}, {"line": 33, "text": ""}, {"line": 34, "text": "Phase composition was determined by X-ray diffraction (XRD) using CuKα radiation (Philips X’Pert Pro MPD, Netherlands) and the structure refinement were carried out by analyzing XRD data using the GSAS software [16]. Archimedes principle was employed to measure the apparent density (or bulk density) of the sintered samples. The relative density (D) was the ratio of the measured apparent density (ρo) to the calculated theoretical density (ρtheo):      . And the theoretical density (ρtheo) was obtained by: ρtheo =   , where Z is the number of molecules in the unit cell, A is the formula weight, V is the cell volume, N is Avogadro's number (N=6.022×1023/mol). The porosity of the ceramics: P=1-D. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM, FEI Inspect F, the United Kingdom) was employed to study the surface microstructure of the ceramics. And the average grain sizes were analyzed from SEM graphs using Image-Pro Plus software. The network analyzer (Agilent Technologies E5071C, the United States) and a temperature chamber (DELTA 9023, Delta Design, USA) were used to measure the microwave dielectric properties by the Hakki–Coleman dielectric resonator method using the TE011 mode [17]. The temperature coefficient of resonant frequency was"}], "document_id": "_ The effects of ZnO doping on microstructure and microwave dielectric properties of Li &lt;sub_&gt;", "line": 32, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> The effects of ZnO doping on microstructure and microwave dielectric properties of Li &lt;sub_&gt;.md", "submatches": [{"end": 617, "start": 600}], "text": "The LMZxTO ceramics were prepared by solid state reaction technic. The raw materials of Mg(OH)2·4MgCO3·5H2O (>99.5%), Li2CO3(99.9%), Ta2O5(99.9%), and ZnO(99.9%) were weighed according to the stoichiometry of Li3Mg2-xZnxTaO6 (x=0\\~0.5). These raw powders were ball milled in alcohol for 7h in nylon jars with zirconia balls. After drying, the mixtures were calcined at 1000ºC for 4h, then reground for 3h, dried and mixed with an 6wt% PVA as a binder. The resultant powders were axially pressed into cylinders with diameter of 15 mm and 7.5mm in thickness under the pressure of 20 MPa. To control Li volatilization, these pellets were muffled in ZrO2 powders and Li2CO3 were used to provide Li-rich atmosphere in high temperature circumstances. Then the pellets were sintered from 1230ºC to 1310ºC."}, {"context": [{"line": 68, "text": "Figure 6. Planar SEM images of Al-doped LLZO powder synthesized by the sol–gel method with Li addition: (a) Li 6.2 mol. (calculated average grain size  329 nm), (b) Li 7.13 mol. (grain size  429 nm), (c) Li 7.29 mol. (grain size 573 nm), and (d) Li 7.44 mol. (grain size 657 nm). Conditions: calcination temperature, 900 -C; Al doping, 0.4 mol."}, {"line": 69, "text": ""}, {"line": 70, "text": "Li addition. For the sol–gel LLZO powder, peaks corresponding to the cubic phase were mainly observed over the entire range of the Li addition amount, except for a small amount of Li-deficient secondary phases at 6.2 mol. The secondary phases can be correlated with the Li volatilization during the calcination. In contrast, the soli-state LLZO powder exhibited a tetragonal structure except for the highrdue University Librariesest Li addition of 7.44 mol. It is thought that the transformation to the cubic phase in the solid-state reaction requires more driving energy and Li addition amount."}, {"line": 71, "text": ""}, {"line": 72, "text": "To investigate the effects of the Li addition amount in detail, SEM images were taken for sol–gel and solid-state powders. Figure 6 shows the planar SEM images of the Al-doped LLZO powder synthesized by the sol–gel method with Li addition. The average grain sizes calculated by the linear intercept analysis method are included in the figure caption. A small grain structure is observed at the lowest Li addition of 6.2 mol. In this case, the calculated grain size is 329 nm. The grain size increases as the Delivered by Ingenta to:Li addition amount is increased. The calculated grain size IP: 95.85.80.74 On: Mis 657 nm in the LLZO powder with the highest Li addiCopyright: Americtion of 7.44 mol., in which some liquid marks of melting hysteresis are observed shown in Figure 6(d). Therefore, the increase in grain size with Li addition is related to the melting of excessive Li elements which can play a role in stabilizing the cubic phase."}], "document_id": "_ _ 10.1166_jnn.2016.13570", "line": 70, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> _ 10.1166_jnn.2016.13570.md", "submatches": [{"end": 289, "start": 272}], "text": "Li addition. For the sol–gel LLZO powder, peaks corresponding to the cubic phase were mainly observed over the entire range of the Li addition amount, except for a small amount of Li-deficient secondary phases at 6.2 mol. The secondary phases can be correlated with the Li volatilization during the calcination. In contrast, the soli-state LLZO powder exhibited a tetragonal structure except for the highrdue University Librariesest Li addition of 7.44 mol. It is thought that the transformation to the cubic phase in the solid-state reaction requires more driving energy and Li addition amount."}, {"context": [{"line": 53, "text": "Fig. 5 The bulk densities and the mass loss of (1 − x)LMTxCT-4 wt% LiF (0.05 ≤x≤ 0.2) ceramics as functions of sintering temperatures and composition x"}, {"line": 54, "text": ""}, {"line": 55, "text": "Figure 6 illustrates the ε values and Q × f values of (1 − x) LMT-xCT-4 wt% LiF (0.05 ≤ x ≤ 0.2) ceramics as function of sintering temperatures and composition x. As shown in Fig. 6a, εr values increased frst and then kept relatively stable with increasing temperature. Obviously, the ε values with sintering temperatures are not consistent with that of bulk densities especially when sintered at 850–1150 °C. The main reason that the ε values have not changed so much like bulk densities was because Li volatilization at elevated sintering temperature, resulted in an increase proportion of CT content. Since ε value of CT (ε \\~ 162) is much higher than that of LMT (ε \\~ 17.5), so, the ε values of composite ceramics did not decline in the range of 850–1150 °C. The ε values of 4 wt% LiF-doped (1 − x)LMT-xCT composite ceramics increased from 17.9 to 20.1 with increasing x from 0.05 to 0.2 at 850 °C. Furthermore, the interpolation table of Fig. 6a exhibits the measured ε values and calculated ε values of composite ceramics. It is obvious that the variation tendency of the calculated dielectric constant is in accordance with the measured ones. It can be seen in Fig. 6b that the Q × f values changed dramatically with the increase of sintering temperature and the maximum Q × f values were obtained at about 1100 °C for each composition. It should be noted that the Q × f values of the samples sintered around 1100 °C were approximate 4 times higher than those sintered at 850 °C. The increase of Q × f values with the sintering temperature was mainly attributed to grain growth, indicating that the grain size is the dominating factor to control Q × f values in LiF-doped LMT-CaTiO systems [28]. At 850 °C, Q × f values decreased from 32,840 to 21,736 GHz with x increased from 0.05 to 0.2, due to the lower Q × f values of CT than that of LMT."}, {"line": 56, "text": ""}, {"line": 57, "text": "![](images/2944bc10cca6684210b12ce16dd9304782a2c8514a9875f5e4ac110475362284.jpg)  "}], "document_id": "Low-firing Li4Mg3Ti2O9–CaTiO3 composite ceramics with temperature stable microwave dielectric proper", "line": 55, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Li4Mg3Ti2O9–CaTiO3 composite ceramics with temperature stable microwave dielectric proper.md", "submatches": [{"end": 535, "start": 518}], "text": "Figure 6 illustrates the ε values and Q × f values of (1 − x) LMT-xCT-4 wt% LiF (0.05 ≤ x ≤ 0.2) ceramics as function of sintering temperatures and composition x. As shown in Fig. 6a, εr values increased frst and then kept relatively stable with increasing temperature. Obviously, the ε values with sintering temperatures are not consistent with that of bulk densities especially when sintered at 850–1150 °C. The main reason that the ε values have not changed so much like bulk densities was because Li volatilization at elevated sintering temperature, resulted in an increase proportion of CT content. Since ε value of CT (ε \\~ 162) is much higher than that of LMT (ε \\~ 17.5), so, the ε values of composite ceramics did not decline in the range of 850–1150 °C. The ε values of 4 wt% LiF-doped (1 − x)LMT-xCT composite ceramics increased from 17.9 to 20.1 with increasing x from 0.05 to 0.2 at 850 °C. Furthermore, the interpolation table of Fig. 6a exhibits the measured ε values and calculated ε values of composite ceramics. It is obvious that the variation tendency of the calculated dielectric constant is in accordance with the measured ones. It can be seen in Fig. 6b that the Q × f values changed dramatically with the increase of sintering temperature and the maximum Q × f values were obtained at about 1100 °C for each composition. It should be noted that the Q × f values of the samples sintered around 1100 °C were approximate 4 times higher than those sintered at 850 °C. The increase of Q × f values with the sintering temperature was mainly attributed to grain growth, indicating that the grain size is the dominating factor to control Q × f values in LiF-doped LMT-CaTiO systems [28]. At 850 °C, Q × f values decreased from 32,840 to 21,736 GHz with x increased from 0.05 to 0.2, due to the lower Q × f values of CT than that of LMT."}, {"context": [{"line": 27, "text": "# 1 Introduction"}, {"line": 28, "text": ""}, {"line": 29, "text": "Ferrites are one of the most important soft magnetic materials which have many excellent properties, including high permeability, electrical resistivity, saturation magnetization, Curie temperature, and low eddy current loss [1–3]. They have been widely applied in electronic devices, such as multilayer chip inductor (MLCI) [4], biomedical systems [5], transformer cores [6], and gas sensing [5], due to their remarkable electrical and magnetic properties [7]. The rapid development of electronic information technology has accelerated electronic devices toward high frequency, high efficiency, and miniaturization [8]. It is important to produce the ferrites with high quality and low cost in the high-frequency field. Compared with other ferrites, Li–Zn ferrites are low-cost materials which have been widely applied in electronic devices for high-frequency applications [9–11]. However, pure Li–Zn ferrites are difficult to be densified sufficiently, because Li volatilization occurs at sintering temperature above 1160 °C [12]. Li volatilization can lead to the increase of dielectric loss tangent, the decrease of mechanical strength, and the change of electrical and magnetic properties for Li–Zn ferrites. In order to obtain excellent magnetic and electrical properties of Li–Zn ferrites, the doping is an effective approach to improve density at low sintering temperature and decrease Li volatilization for Li–Zn ferrites [12]. M. Mahmoudi et al. [13] have reported that the density of Li–Zn ferrites can be improved by doping SiO2 in the samples. C. Orozco et al. [14] synthesized the molybdenum-incorporated cobalt ferrite by the conventional solid-state reaction method. They reported that Mo incorporation can induce the agglomeration of particles, leading to larger particle size with increasing Mo content. Z.K. Heiba et al. [15] have shown the increase in saturation magnetization (Ms) in Mo-doped cobalt ferrite. These researches show that Mo doping can improve the soft magnetic properties of materials. However, the detailed structural and magnetic properties of Mo-doped Li–Zn ferrite have not been reported yet. Thus, the Mo-doped Li–Zn ferrites are investigated in this work."}, {"line": 30, "text": ""}, {"line": 31, "text": "The properties of ferrites are sensitive to the preparation method [16]. Different synthesis methods of Li–Zn ferrites have been reported in the literature, including sol–gel method [17], combustion synthesis method [18], chemical coprecipitation method [19], conventional ceramic method [12], etc. Among these methods, sol–gel auto-combustion method is the most convenient way to synthesize highly pure and uniform particles. The sol–gel auto-combustion method has been widely used in the synthesis of ferrite. S.K. Gurav et al. [20] have synthesized the Li0.5ZrxCoxFe2.5–2xO4 ferrites by the sol–gel auto-combustion technique. It has been reported by M. Arana et al. [21] that the Li0.5xZn0.6xMn0.4Fe2 +0.5xO4 ferrites were produced by the sol–gel auto-combustion method. The Ni-substituted Li–Zn ferrites have been fabricated by the sol–gel method, which has been employed to get nanosized particles and good magnetic properties [22]."}], "document_id": "_ Effect of Mo substitution on the structural and soft magnetic properties of Li–Zn ferrites _ 10.10", "line": 29, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Effect of Mo substitution on the structural and soft magnetic properties of Li–Zn ferrites _ 10.10.md", "submatches": [{"end": 988, "start": 971}, {"end": 1059, "start": 1042}, {"end": 1424, "start": 1407}], "text": "Ferrites are one of the most important soft magnetic materials which have many excellent properties, including high permeability, electrical resistivity, saturation magnetization, Curie temperature, and low eddy current loss [1–3]. They have been widely applied in electronic devices, such as multilayer chip inductor (MLCI) [4], biomedical systems [5], transformer cores [6], and gas sensing [5], due to their remarkable electrical and magnetic properties [7]. The rapid development of electronic information technology has accelerated electronic devices toward high frequency, high efficiency, and miniaturization [8]. It is important to produce the ferrites with high quality and low cost in the high-frequency field. Compared with other ferrites, Li–Zn ferrites are low-cost materials which have been widely applied in electronic devices for high-frequency applications [9–11]. However, pure Li–Zn ferrites are difficult to be densified sufficiently, because Li volatilization occurs at sintering temperature above 1160 °C [12]. Li volatilization can lead to the increase of dielectric loss tangent, the decrease of mechanical strength, and the change of electrical and magnetic properties for Li–Zn ferrites. In order to obtain excellent magnetic and electrical properties of Li–Zn ferrites, the doping is an effective approach to improve density at low sintering temperature and decrease Li volatilization for Li–Zn ferrites [12]. M. Mahmoudi et al. [13] have reported that the density of Li–Zn ferrites can be improved by doping SiO2 in the samples. C. Orozco et al. [14] synthesized the molybdenum-incorporated cobalt ferrite by the conventional solid-state reaction method. They reported that Mo incorporation can induce the agglomeration of particles, leading to larger particle size with increasing Mo content. Z.K. Heiba et al. [15] have shown the increase in saturation magnetization (Ms) in Mo-doped cobalt ferrite. These researches show that Mo doping can improve the soft magnetic properties of materials. However, the detailed structural and magnetic properties of Mo-doped Li–Zn ferrite have not been reported yet. Thus, the Mo-doped Li–Zn ferrites are investigated in this work."}], "ok": true, "query": "Li volatilization", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": true, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 7, "truncated": false}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:12:25.165608+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:34:{"duration_ms": 1475.26, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 2, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 12, "query": "Al contamination", "regex": false, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 337, "text": "# Virulence assays on the susceptible tomato cultivar LA3940"}, {"line": 338, "text": ""}, {"line": 339, "text": "Inoculations on the susceptible tomato cv. LA3940 with the wild-type P. fuligena strain (WT-Pf) as well as two ΔPf-Avr4 deletion mutants (ΔPf-Avr4-1 and ΔPf-Avr4-2) were performed with mycelial fragments, as described above in Methods (see section referring to the quantification of Pf-Avr4 expression levels). Briefly, mycelial fragments (105 fragments per mL) of each strain were sprayed on the leaves of three 6-week-old tomato plants, respectively. Three leaves from each inoculated plant were collected at 1, 3, 6, 9, 12, and 15 days post-inoculation (dpi) and were immediately frozen in liquid nitrogen, and then stored in -80°C until RNA extractions. Total RNA was isolated from inoculated leaves using the TRIzol reagent (Life Technologies, Carlsbad, CA) according to the manufacturer’s instructions and was subsequently treated with RNase-free DNase (ThermoFisher Scientific Inc., Walthan, MA) in order to remove any residual contaminations by genomic DNA. cDNA was synthesized according to the protocol of the iScript cDNA Synthesis kit (Bio-Rad Laboratories, Inc., Hercules, CA.). Quantitative real-time PCR (qPCR) was conducted using the SsoFast EvaGreen Supermix (Bio-Rad Laboratories, Inc., Hercules, CA), according to the manufacturer’s instructions, on a CFX96 Touch Real-Time PCR detection system (Bio-Rad Laboratories, Inc., Hercules, CA). First, expression of the fungal actin (Actin) under infection with the Pf-Avr4WT strain was quantified by qPCR, using primers PfActin-F and Pf-Actin-R (Supplemental Table 1). Expression of the tomato RPL2 (ribosomal protein large subunit 2: PL2T) gene was used as an endogenous control. RPL2 was amplified with primers RPL2-F and RPL2-R (Supplemental Table 1). Real-time PCR conditions included an initial 95°C denaturation step for 10 minutes, followed by denaturation for 15 seconds at 95°C, annealing for 30 seconds at 60°C, and extension for 30 seconds at 72°C for a total of 40 cycles. For relative quantification of the fungal biomass produced by the ΔPf-Avr4-1 and ΔPfAvr4-2 mutants as compared to the WT-Pf strain, the expression of Pf-Actin in leaves inoculated with the WT-Pf strain was set to 100% and the expression of Pf-Actin in leaves inoculated with the ΔPf-Avr4-1 and ΔPf-Avr4-2 mutants was calculated relative to the WT-Pf. Virulence assays were repeated three times and each qPCR was repeated in triplicate as well."}, {"line": 340, "text": ""}, {"line": 341, "text": "1011   "}], "document_id": "_ Structural analysis of an Avr4 effector ortholog offers insight into chitin-binding and recognitio", "line": 339, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Structural analysis of an Avr4 effector ortholog offers insight into chitin-binding and recognitio.md", "submatches": [{"end": 954, "start": 938}], "text": "Inoculations on the susceptible tomato cv. LA3940 with the wild-type P. fuligena strain (WT-Pf) as well as two ΔPf-Avr4 deletion mutants (ΔPf-Avr4-1 and ΔPf-Avr4-2) were performed with mycelial fragments, as described above in Methods (see section referring to the quantification of Pf-Avr4 expression levels). Briefly, mycelial fragments (105 fragments per mL) of each strain were sprayed on the leaves of three 6-week-old tomato plants, respectively. Three leaves from each inoculated plant were collected at 1, 3, 6, 9, 12, and 15 days post-inoculation (dpi) and were immediately frozen in liquid nitrogen, and then stored in -80°C until RNA extractions. Total RNA was isolated from inoculated leaves using the TRIzol reagent (Life Technologies, Carlsbad, CA) according to the manufacturer’s instructions and was subsequently treated with RNase-free DNase (ThermoFisher Scientific Inc., Walthan, MA) in order to remove any residual contaminations by genomic DNA. cDNA was synthesized according to the protocol of the iScript cDNA Synthesis kit (Bio-Rad Laboratories, Inc., Hercules, CA.). Quantitative real-time PCR (qPCR) was conducted using the SsoFast EvaGreen Supermix (Bio-Rad Laboratories, Inc., Hercules, CA), according to the manufacturer’s instructions, on a CFX96 Touch Real-Time PCR detection system (Bio-Rad Laboratories, Inc., Hercules, CA). First, expression of the fungal actin (Actin) under infection with the Pf-Avr4WT strain was quantified by qPCR, using primers PfActin-F and Pf-Actin-R (Supplemental Table 1). Expression of the tomato RPL2 (ribosomal protein large subunit 2: PL2T) gene was used as an endogenous control. RPL2 was amplified with primers RPL2-F and RPL2-R (Supplemental Table 1). Real-time PCR conditions included an initial 95°C denaturation step for 10 minutes, followed by denaturation for 15 seconds at 95°C, annealing for 30 seconds at 60°C, and extension for 30 seconds at 72°C for a total of 40 cycles. For relative quantification of the fungal biomass produced by the ΔPf-Avr4-1 and ΔPfAvr4-2 mutants as compared to the WT-Pf strain, the expression of Pf-Actin in leaves inoculated with the WT-Pf strain was set to 100% and the expression of Pf-Actin in leaves inoculated with the ΔPf-Avr4-1 and ΔPf-Avr4-2 mutants was calculated relative to the WT-Pf. Virulence assays were repeated three times and each qPCR was repeated in triplicate as well."}, {"context": [{"line": 21, "text": "![](images/0cc06a28ed13752cca40fed7da036036eee82675cc6f57dc48ae1dc0805fd3e2.jpg)"}, {"line": 22, "text": ""}, {"line": 23, "text": "The conventional solid-state method was used to prepare the Li-based garnet-type ceramic materials. As-received, high purity precursor powders including LiNO, (99%, Alfa Aesar), La2O3 (99%, Alfa Aesar), Gd2O3 (99%, Alfa Aesar) and Nb2O5 (99%, Alfa Aesar) were used. LaO3 and GdO3 were dried in a furnace at 900 C for 8h prior to the mixing of the materials. Stoichiometric amounts of the required precursors were used to prepare Gd-doped Lis+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). A 10 wt. % excess of Li salt was added to compensate for Li volatilization during the high temperature sintering stages. All syntheses were carried out in alumina crucibles, in air. The powders were mixed with isopropanol in zirconia bowls with zirconia balls, using a ball mill (Pullverisette, Frtisch, Germany) for 6h at 200 min'. The direction of rotation was changed every hour to ensure even mixing. Powders were dried in air, placed in crucibles and heated at 700 C for 6 h. Sintered powders were ball-milled once more under the previously mentioned conditions. After ball-milling, dried powders were pressed into pellets (\\~1cm diameter, \\~2 cm length) using an iso-static pressure of 200 kN for 5 minutes (isostatic press, P.O Weber). The pellets were covered with some of the powder to help reduce LiO volatilization, then sintered at 900 C for 24 h. Powder was also placed on the bottom of the alumina crucible to minimize Al contamination. A second sintering step (1100 C for 6 h) was used to improve the density of the samples."}, {"line": 24, "text": ""}, {"line": 25, "text": "# Characterization of Li5+2xLa3Nb2-xGd,O12 (0  x  0.45)"}], "document_id": "Evaluation on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like", "line": 23, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1421, "start": 1405}], "text": "The conventional solid-state method was used to prepare the Li-based garnet-type ceramic materials. As-received, high purity precursor powders including LiNO, (99%, Alfa Aesar), La2O3 (99%, Alfa Aesar), Gd2O3 (99%, Alfa Aesar) and Nb2O5 (99%, Alfa Aesar) were used. LaO3 and GdO3 were dried in a furnace at 900 C for 8h prior to the mixing of the materials. Stoichiometric amounts of the required precursors were used to prepare Gd-doped Lis+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). A 10 wt. % excess of Li salt was added to compensate for Li volatilization during the high temperature sintering stages. All syntheses were carried out in alumina crucibles, in air. The powders were mixed with isopropanol in zirconia bowls with zirconia balls, using a ball mill (Pullverisette, Frtisch, Germany) for 6h at 200 min'. The direction of rotation was changed every hour to ensure even mixing. Powders were dried in air, placed in crucibles and heated at 700 C for 6 h. Sintered powders were ball-milled once more under the previously mentioned conditions. After ball-milling, dried powders were pressed into pellets (\\~1cm diameter, \\~2 cm length) using an iso-static pressure of 200 kN for 5 minutes (isostatic press, P.O Weber). The pellets were covered with some of the powder to help reduce LiO volatilization, then sintered at 900 C for 24 h. Powder was also placed on the bottom of the alumina crucible to minimize Al contamination. A second sintering step (1100 C for 6 h) was used to improve the density of the samples."}, {"context": [{"line": 9, "text": "# 1. Introduction"}, {"line": 10, "text": ""}, {"line": 11, "text": "Avoiding the use of solvents in synthesis can reduce environmental contamination and even be more convenient than using solvent-based synthesis. It has also been said that “the best solvent is no solvent”. It is sometimes actually higher yield, easier and faster to synthesis some ligands and metal complexes by avoiding the use of solvents [1]."}, {"line": 12, "text": ""}, {"line": 13, "text": "Schiff base are the containing -HC=N- group. They are condensation product of ketones or aldehydes with primary amines and were reported by Hugo Schiff 1864. Metal complexes of Schiff base having played a central role in the development of coordination chemistry [2–4]."}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis, biological activity of a new long-chain di-Schiff base ligand and Ru(II) complex in sol", "line": 11, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis, biological activity of a new long-chain di-Schiff base ligand and Ru(II) complex in sol.md", "submatches": [{"end": 80, "start": 64}], "text": "Avoiding the use of solvents in synthesis can reduce environmental contamination and even be more convenient than using solvent-based synthesis. It has also been said that “the best solvent is no solvent”. It is sometimes actually higher yield, easier and faster to synthesis some ligands and metal complexes by avoiding the use of solvents [1]."}, {"context": [{"line": 27, "text": "Among the new technologies, nanotechnology is a technique used to convert simple materials into intelligent, efficient and functional materials. Titanium dioxide nanoparticles can be used for the realization of self-cleaning transparent coatings on different types of surfaces, with the aim of reducing cleaning and maintenance operations, and therefore, reducing relevant costs and improving the surface quality (Liu and Chen, 2014; Chen and Mao, 2007; Bourikas et al., 2014)."}, {"line": 28, "text": ""}, {"line": 29, "text": "The self-cleaning ability of titanium dioxide is due to the synergistic effect of its optical inductive property, which is activated with sunlight (Ollis, 2010; Diamanti et al., 2008; Guo et al., 2016; Fujishima and Zhang, 2006): super-hydrophilic and photo-catalytic. Titanium dioxide is super-hydrophilic under ultraviolet (UV) light, so a uniform water film is created on the prepared surfaces, which prevents the contact between the external contamination and the substrate, and eliminating the dust particles will be more easily. UV radiation can also activate the photo-catalytic property of titanium dioxide. This feature allows the photo analysis of organic and inorganic contaminants absorbed or deposited on titanium dioxide coatings through a sun-induced reaction. Titanium dioxide is one of the most widely used photocatalytic materials due to its high efficiency, low cost and compatibility with many substrates. The formation of water film on protected surfaces along with the decomposition of contaminants can result in the real self-cleaning effect."}, {"line": 30, "text": ""}, {"line": 31, "text": "In this way, the building industry inspired by nature, has recently shown great interest in creating surfaces, which are anti-pollution surfaces and can be easily cleaned using nanotechnology. In fact, some models of self-cleaning surfaces exist in nature, such as the surfaces of leaves of some plants and wings and body of some insects. It seems that transmission of these self-cleaning and anti-pollution effects by using nanoscale materials and their nanomorphological properties is possible."}], "document_id": "_ Self-cleaning treatment on historical stone surface via titanium dioxide nanocoatings _ 10.1108_pr", "line": 29, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Self-cleaning treatment on historical stone surface via titanium dioxide nanocoatings _ 10.1108_pr.md", "submatches": [{"end": 459, "start": 443}], "text": "The self-cleaning ability of titanium dioxide is due to the synergistic effect of its optical inductive property, which is activated with sunlight (Ollis, 2010; Diamanti et al., 2008; Guo et al., 2016; Fujishima and Zhang, 2006): super-hydrophilic and photo-catalytic. Titanium dioxide is super-hydrophilic under ultraviolet (UV) light, so a uniform water film is created on the prepared surfaces, which prevents the contact between the external contamination and the substrate, and eliminating the dust particles will be more easily. UV radiation can also activate the photo-catalytic property of titanium dioxide. This feature allows the photo analysis of organic and inorganic contaminants absorbed or deposited on titanium dioxide coatings through a sun-induced reaction. Titanium dioxide is one of the most widely used photocatalytic materials due to its high efficiency, low cost and compatibility with many substrates. The formation of water film on protected surfaces along with the decomposition of contaminants can result in the real self-cleaning effect."}, {"context": [{"line": 19, "text": "# Introduction"}, {"line": 20, "text": ""}, {"line": 21, "text": "Heavy metals have attracted particular interest within the framework of environmental studies. Industrial production, life cycle assessment, food pollution, and extraction of the highest amounts of heavy metals from surface and ground water are the most interesting topics for many researchers. Toxic elements in soil and transfer into crops are of great concern owing to the probability of toxic element introduction into the food chain and the influence of essential elements. Anthropogenic activities are considered as one of the most common sources of heavy metals in the environment, as are domestic activities (Ene et al., 2009). This toxic heavy metal contamination may affect the entire universe and all of humankind. The bioavailability of toxic metals causes the presence of metal ion speciation in the soil. Mercury (Hg) is one of the most hazardous metals that can be found worldwide and its toxic nature has been explored. The elimination of mercury ions from water and wastewater is essential. Therefore, the appropriate methods to control and determine mercury ions are important. However, direct evaluations of metal ions using different systematic methods (such as chemical precipitation, coagulation, ion exchange, flocculation, adsorption, etc.) are not sufficient due to high concentrations and the interfering matrix components (Babel and Kurniawan, 2003; Bailey et al., 1999). Thus, an effective separation procedure is required before metal ion determination for sensitive, accurate, and interference-free determination of metal ions. There are many treatment techniques for the separation of metal ions, including precipitation (Souza et al., 2011), liquid–liquid extraction (Akinobu et al., 1997), ion exchange (Hershey and Keliher, 1989), cloud point extraction (Manzoori et al., 2007), and solid-phase extraction (Ahmed, 2008; Montero Alvarez et al., 2007). Among the various treatments, the adsorption technique is the most frequently used method. Solvent extraction has been used as an efficient method owing to effective extraction, selective separation, and economical costs (Biparva and Hadjmohammadi, 2011; Dave et al., 2010; Diniz and Volesky, 2005; Hang et al., 2002; Jain et al., 2001; Jankowski et al., 2005; Jelinek et al., 2007; Kim et al., 2006; Lemos et al., 2008; Liang et al., 2005; Marwani et al., 2012a, 2012b; Mashhadizadeh et al., 2008; Pasinli et al., 2005; Rauf et al., 1993; Zhang et al., 2007). The use of adsorbents containing natural polymers has generated great interest, especially regarding polysaccharides such as chitin and its derivate, chitosan (Crini, 2005; Kurita, 2006; Varma et al., 2004). It is well known that the most abundant natural biopolymer is cellulose; the second is chitin, which can be found in the shells of crustaceans, skeletons of molluscs, and in the cell walls of some fungi (Gooday, 1990; Zhang et al., 2000). Chitin exists in nature as crystalline microfibrils. Depending on its source, three different crystalline polymorphic forms of chitin have been explored (Jang et al., 2004). Chitosan is a copolymer that is composed of (1!4) linkages of 2-amino-2-deoxy-D-glucopyranose units. Chitosan is found naturally in some fungal cell walls (Jayakumar et al., 2005; Synowiecki and Al-Khateeb, 2003). Chitosan has been widely used in several biological and medical applications (Brine et al., 1992; Colwell et al., 1985; Dutta et al., 2002; Kawamura et al., 1993; Muzzarelli, 1973). Scientists have been working on different methods to modify chitosan through physical or chemical methods to enhance its adsorption capacity (Guibal, 2004; Jeon and Holl, 2003; Matsumura et al., 1998). Cross-linking with € glutaraldehyde or epi-chlorohydrin is an example of chitosan chemical modification. Another chemical modification of chitosan derivatives can be obtained through functional group grafting onto the backbone of chitosan. Functional groups can affect different factors such as increasing the density of sorption sites, changing the pH range for metal sorption, and modifying the sorption sites in order to increase sorption selectivity for the metal (Donia et al., 2008; Elwakeel and Guibal, 2015; Kurita, 2006). The chemical methods of modification provide a wide range of derivatives with new properties, which can be applied in pharmaceutical and biomedical settings (Mourya and Inamdar, 2008). On the other hand, the important factor that explains why we should study the adsorption behaviour of chitosan in aqueous acid solutions is based on the presence of acetic acid as an acidic media. Acetic acid is predominantly used as a stimulator in the waste removal process, in which the pH of the dye solution is normally adjusted to 3–4. However, chitosan formed gels below pH 5.5 and could not be determined. The acid streaming could severely limit the use of chitosan as an adsorbent in removing dyes, metal ions, and other waste materials due to the dissolution tendency of chitosan in the acid effluent. Therefore, it is preferable to study the prepared sulfone-modified chitosan (SMC)/5–20 derivatives as surface adsorbents, which will be discussed in this work. Thus, b-ketosulfone derivatives (SMC) will be prepared from the reaction of chitosan with phenacyl bromide and sodium benzene sulfinate. Furthermore, the analytical potential of newly synthesized SMC towards a selective extraction of various metal ions, especially Hg(II), will be explored. Additionally, factors such as pH and the concentrations of metals that affect the selectivity and adsorption of SMC will be evaluated."}, {"line": 22, "text": ""}, {"li