). Cross-linking with € glutaraldehyde or epi-chlorohydrin is an example of chitosan chemical modification. Another chemical modification of chitosan derivatives can be obtained through functional group grafting onto the backbone of chitosan. Functional groups can affect different factors such as increasing the density of sorption sites, changing the pH range for metal sorption, and modifying the sorption sites in order to increase sorption selectivity for the metal (Donia et al., 2008; Elwakeel and Guibal, 2015; Kurita, 2006). The chemical methods of modification provide a wide range of derivatives with new properties, which can be applied in pharmaceutical and biomedical settings (Mourya and Inamdar, 2008). On the other hand, the important factor that explains why we should study the adsorption behaviour of chitosan in aqueous acid solutions is based on the presence of acetic acid as an acidic media. Acetic acid is predominantly used as a stimulator in the waste removal process, in which the pH of the dye solution is normally adjusted to 3–4. However, chitosan formed gels below pH 5.5 and could not be determined. The acid streaming could severely limit the use of chitosan as an adsorbent in removing dyes, metal ions, and other waste materials due to the dissolution tendency of chitosan in the acid effluent. Therefore, it is preferable to study the prepared sulfone-modified chitosan (SMC)/5–20 derivatives as surface adsorbents, which will be discussed in this work. Thus, b-ketosulfone derivatives (SMC) will be prepared from the reaction of chitosan with phenacyl bromide and sodium benzene sulfinate. Furthermore, the analytical potential of newly synthesized SMC towards a selective extraction of various metal ions, especially Hg(II), will be explored. Additionally, factors such as pH and the concentrations of metals that affect the selectivity and adsorption of SMC will be evaluated."}, {"context": [{"line": 73, "text": "Fig. 8. TG curves of different organic compounds with the heating rate of 10 °C min\u00011 : (a) DBS; (b) Rosin; (c) PHEMA."}, {"line": 74, "text": ""}, {"line": 75, "text": "10 °C min\u00011 under a flowing argon atmosphere. It can be observed that the majority of the above three organic compounds were removed in the temperature range from 300 to 400 °C. The difference lies in the residual mass after pyrolysis. Organic decomposition behavior for PHEMA was shown in Figure 8c. At the end of heat-treatment, the residual mass of the organics was about 13 wt%. Figure 8a and b showed the pyrolysis behavior of DBS and rosin, the residual mass were only 7 and 3 wt%, respectively. The differences in residual mass were attributed to the different degradation mechanisms of the organic compounds. PHEMA is a highmolecular weight organic compound prepared from polymerization of HEMA. During heat-treatment, PHEMA degrades by random chain scission. However, rosin is a low-molecular weight organic compound and DBS gel formats through the weak interaction force of hydrogen bond. During the pyrolysis process, rosin, and DBS could be removed by evaporating rather than chain scission. Therefore, in terms of decreasing the contamination content, DBS, and rosin gel system have an advantage over HEMA system. Additionally, the above TG analyses were carried out on the pure gel. The final contamination content in the bulk titanium would be investigated by chemical analysis on the sintered parts."}, {"line": 76, "text": ""}, {"line": 77, "text": "Moreover, another dehydriding procedure is indispensable while using titanium hydride as raw material to prepare titanium. In order to clarify the dehydriding behavior of titanium hydride, differential thermal analysis was used to investigate the thermal effects of pure titanium hydride powder during the pre-sintering stage. Figure 9 demonstrated the DTA curve of titanium hydride powder with the heating rate of 10 °C min\u00011 under a flowing argon atmosphere. It was observed that there were two exothermic peaks, which indicated that dehydriding carried out in two steps. It firstly dehydrided from TiH2 to TiHx, and then transformed to pure titanium.[18] Therefore, it was confirmed that the dehydriding temperature was higher than degreasing temperature. While setting the sintering procedure, enough holding time must be ensured at both stages."}], "document_id": "_ Low-Molecular Mass Organic Gelcasting of Titanium Hydride to Prepare Titanium _ 10.1002_adem.20140", "line": 75, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Low-Molecular Mass Organic Gelcasting of Titanium Hydride to Prepare Titanium _ 10.1002_adem.20140.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1222, "start": 1206}], "text": "10 °C min\u00011 under a flowing argon atmosphere. It can be observed that the majority of the above three organic compounds were removed in the temperature range from 300 to 400 °C. The difference lies in the residual mass after pyrolysis. Organic decomposition behavior for PHEMA was shown in Figure 8c. At the end of heat-treatment, the residual mass of the organics was about 13 wt%. Figure 8a and b showed the pyrolysis behavior of DBS and rosin, the residual mass were only 7 and 3 wt%, respectively. The differences in residual mass were attributed to the different degradation mechanisms of the organic compounds. PHEMA is a highmolecular weight organic compound prepared from polymerization of HEMA. During heat-treatment, PHEMA degrades by random chain scission. However, rosin is a low-molecular weight organic compound and DBS gel formats through the weak interaction force of hydrogen bond. During the pyrolysis process, rosin, and DBS could be removed by evaporating rather than chain scission. Therefore, in terms of decreasing the contamination content, DBS, and rosin gel system have an advantage over HEMA system. Additionally, the above TG analyses were carried out on the pure gel. The final contamination content in the bulk titanium would be investigated by chemical analysis on the sintered parts."}, {"context": [{"line": 26, "text": "# INTRODUCTION"}, {"line": 27, "text": ""}, {"line": 28, "text": "The discharge of heavy metals into aquatic ecosystems has become a matter of concern over the last few decades. The major sources of heavy metal contamination are the industrial effluents that adversely affect water resources, soil fertility, aquatic organisms and ecosystem integrity. Metal ions in wastewater can cause serious toxicological problems and thus are dangerous to the environment and human beings (Yu et al. ; Kargar et al. ). Hexavalent chromium (Cr(VI)) is highly toxic and carcinogenic. Waste containing Cr(VI) in solution may result from a variety of operations like steel manufacture, paint and pigment production, electroplating, leather tanning and chemical manufacturing (Sharma ; Hu et al. ). Hence the element has become a significant constituent of sewage and soil and is a potentially serious pollutant. The permissible limit of Cr(VI) for potable water is 0.05 mg/L, and 0.1 mg/L for discharge to inland surface water. Human health is highly affected when exposed to Cr(VI) in excess of the permissible limit. It affects the lungs, liver, nervous system and kidneys in mammals. Therefore, it is imperative to remove chromium from industrial effluent before discharging it into the water. Reduction-precipitation, coagulation, reverse osmosis and ion exchange are widely practiced techniques for Cr(VI) removal (Iqbal et al. ; Ali ). These conventional methods cannot completely remove toxic metals and moreover these techniques are expensive (Ali et al. ; Khan et al. ). Removal of the Cr(VI) from industrial wastewater through adsorption is an effective alternative due to its easy operation and economic viability. Materials already used for removal of hexavalent chromium include activated carbon prepared from ground nut husk, coconut sawdust, Hevea brasiliensis sawdust and rice husk ash (Periyasamy et al. ; Selvi et al. ; Karthikeyan et al. ; Singh &"}, {"line": 29, "text": ""}, {"line": 30, "text": "Singh ), as well as clay, pine needle powder and guar gum–nano zinc oxide biocomposite (Bhattacharya & Gupta ; Hadjmohammadi et al. ; Khan et al. ). However, there is some need to develop more efficient and locally available cheap adsorbents for the removal of Cr (VI)."}], "document_id": "_ Chromium (VI) removal from aqueous solutions using &lt;i&gt;Eichhornia&lt;_i&gt; as an adsorbent _", "line": 28, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Chromium (VI) removal from aqueous solutions using &lt;i&gt;Eichhornia&lt;_i&gt; as an adsorbent _.md", "submatches": [{"end": 158, "start": 142}], "text": "The discharge of heavy metals into aquatic ecosystems has become a matter of concern over the last few decades. The major sources of heavy metal contamination are the industrial effluents that adversely affect water resources, soil fertility, aquatic organisms and ecosystem integrity. Metal ions in wastewater can cause serious toxicological problems and thus are dangerous to the environment and human beings (Yu et al. ; Kargar et al. ). Hexavalent chromium (Cr(VI)) is highly toxic and carcinogenic. Waste containing Cr(VI) in solution may result from a variety of operations like steel manufacture, paint and pigment production, electroplating, leather tanning and chemical manufacturing (Sharma ; Hu et al. ). Hence the element has become a significant constituent of sewage and soil and is a potentially serious pollutant. The permissible limit of Cr(VI) for potable water is 0.05 mg/L, and 0.1 mg/L for discharge to inland surface water. Human health is highly affected when exposed to Cr(VI) in excess of the permissible limit. It affects the lungs, liver, nervous system and kidneys in mammals. Therefore, it is imperative to remove chromium from industrial effluent before discharging it into the water. Reduction-precipitation, coagulation, reverse osmosis and ion exchange are widely practiced techniques for Cr(VI) removal (Iqbal et al. ; Ali ). These conventional methods cannot completely remove toxic metals and moreover these techniques are expensive (Ali et al. ; Khan et al. ). Removal of the Cr(VI) from industrial wastewater through adsorption is an effective alternative due to its easy operation and economic viability. Materials already used for removal of hexavalent chromium include activated carbon prepared from ground nut husk, coconut sawdust, Hevea brasiliensis sawdust and rice husk ash (Periyasamy et al. ; Selvi et al. ; Karthikeyan et al. ; Singh &"}, {"context": [{"line": 78, "text": "A visual examination of the white Keronite PEO surface showed uniform and smooth appearance over the entire surface, without any rough speckles or loose particles with good coating retention at sharp corners. The nominal thickness was measured at 12  2 m, and had a good white appearance (Fig. 3). The PEO coating thickness is substantially thinner and more uniform than the white silicone RTV paint whose thickness typically scattered between 80 and 140 m. A scatter in thickness for the spray-deposition coating is typical. A highly uniform, thinner, and harder ceramic PEO coating attributes could result in improved wear resistance, handling resistance, and good thermal transfer response."}, {"line": 79, "text": ""}, {"line": 80, "text": "Surface roughness circa <Ra 1 m was typical for the as-prepared white PEO coating, indicating a relatively smooth and uniform coating surface. For example, Ra circa > 3 m was typical for the white RTV paint. Peel adhesion that was conducted on the asprepared PEO coating surface showed good adhesion and cohesion of the white PEO coating with the substrate with no particle pullout or loss. Coating was considered to have passed the Airbus requirements. Because the PEO coating is mostly conversion of the substrate, very high coating-adhesion values are often measured and are often reported to have at least twice the adhesion of anodized coating, thus avoiding the adhesion problems, interfacial contamination during surface preparations associated with deposited coatings [16,17]."}, {"line": 81, "text": ""}, {"line": 82, "text": "![](images/442e783ba4a22345377d1361fc7c2d34e440365194d84e2b7c1e02d6da267606.jpg)  "}], "document_id": "_ Inorganic White Thermal-Control Coatings for Extreme Space Environments. Journal of Spacecraft and", "line": 80, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Inorganic White Thermal-Control Coatings for Extreme Space Environments. Journal of Spacecraft and.md", "submatches": [{"end": 712, "start": 696}], "text": "Surface roughness circa <Ra 1 m was typical for the as-prepared white PEO coating, indicating a relatively smooth and uniform coating surface. For example, Ra circa > 3 m was typical for the white RTV paint. Peel adhesion that was conducted on the asprepared PEO coating surface showed good adhesion and cohesion of the white PEO coating with the substrate with no particle pullout or loss. Coating was considered to have passed the Airbus requirements. Because the PEO coating is mostly conversion of the substrate, very high coating-adhesion values are often measured and are often reported to have at least twice the adhesion of anodized coating, thus avoiding the adhesion problems, interfacial contamination during surface preparations associated with deposited coatings [16,17]."}, {"context": [{"line": 110, "text": "The focus of this study was to explore the use of maize flour, soybean flour and moringa leaf powder as potential food ingredients that can add value to complementary foods. Malnourished babies are at higher risk of death in infancy and are more likely to face lifelong cognitive and physical deficits and chronic health problems due to protein–energy malnutrition (PEM) (USAID/ ENGINE/Save the children, 2012). Children under 6 years of age are vulnerable to PEM due to poor attention (Joshi, Joshi, Singh, Joshi, & Khan, 2011)."}, {"line": 111, "text": ""}, {"line": 112, "text": "The moisture content of the blended flour is in the limit of FAO/WHO recommended value (<10%), which decreased significantly (p < 0.05.) from 8.7% in maize flour (control) to 5.8% in R55. Moisture has an implication in terms of the consistency/texture and microbiological quality of food (Makinde & Ladipo, 2012). Complementary foods should be free of microbial contamination that might prone children to diarrhea. Olaoye, Onilude, and Idowu (2006) reported that lower moisture content in food have an implication to prevent from microbial contaminations. This finding agreed to the study of Nwosu et al. (2014) and Abraham et al. (2013) that reported decreasing moisture content as moringa leaf powder level increased in their complementary food prepared from maize flour, soybean flour and Moringa leaf powder."}, {"line": 113, "text": ""}, {"line": 114, "text": "Table 3. Proximate and energy composition of the control and composite flour   "}], "document_id": "Proximate composition of complementary food prepared from maize (Zea mays), soybean (Glycine max) an", "line": 112, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> composition of complementary food prepared from maize (Zea mays), soybean (Glycine max) an.md", "submatches": [{"end": 375, "start": 359}, {"end": 553, "start": 537}], "text": "The moisture content of the blended flour is in the limit of FAO/WHO recommended value (<10%), which decreased significantly (p < 0.05.) from 8.7% in maize flour (control) to 5.8% in R55. Moisture has an implication in terms of the consistency/texture and microbiological quality of food (Makinde & Ladipo, 2012). Complementary foods should be free of microbial contamination that might prone children to diarrhea. Olaoye, Onilude, and Idowu (2006) reported that lower moisture content in food have an implication to prevent from microbial contaminations. This finding agreed to the study of Nwosu et al. (2014) and Abraham et al. (2013) that reported decreasing moisture content as moringa leaf powder level increased in their complementary food prepared from maize flour, soybean flour and Moringa leaf powder."}, {"context": [{"line": 13, "text": "# 1. INTRODUCTION"}, {"line": 14, "text": ""}, {"line": 15, "text": "Turmeric (Curuma long L.) is a yellow color herbaceous root which is extensively used as food spices and also for medicinal purposes. It is traded in different form such as turmeric powder, turmeric oil, turmeric root etc. Turmeric has long been used as food flavoring and food coloring agent in Asian diet  It is also utilized in home medicine and pharmaceutical applications. Turmeric is applied to fresh wounds, bruises and insect bites ] as home remedy. The medicinal value of turmeric is mainly due to the yellow pigment called curcumin (diferuloyl methane) content in it 3. Curcumin is also the source of yellowish orange color in turmeric. Curcumin which is present at varying concentration range in turmeric (0.3% to 8.6%) (3- is the main source of its medicinal value such as anti-flammatory , anti-cancer anti-oxidant I, and wound healing  Varying concentration of curcumin in turmeric is mainly due to nurient and acidity content in soi , ferilizer 2, sol ye , and cultivar  Low conceration of curumin n umeric, and isolation of curcumin from turmeric for medicinal purpose has motivated contamination of turmeric by chemicals as a substitute to curcumin ). Although whole, dried and fresh turmeric are free of any chemical contamination, turmeric powder is generally contaminated with yellow colored chemicals as a substitute for curcumin for color and weight. Curcuma zedoaria a wild relative of Curcuma long L., which has close resemblance to turmeric, is mixed with turmeric powder [15). Similarly, turmeric powder is reportedly adulterated with other chemicals such as metanil yellow as a substitute to curcumin for appearance ]. Metanil yellow (CigH4NNaOS) is a toxic azo dye which is classfied as CII category by Joint FAO/wHO Expert commitee on Food Additives. According to the study on rates, long term consumption of metanil yellow can cause neurotoxicity , petatocellular carcinoma 8), tumor (9, liver carcinogenesis [201, and lymphocytic leukemia 21]]"}, {"line": 16, "text": ""}, {"line": 17, "text": "Different laboratory based analytical methods are used for detection of chemical dyes such as metanil yellow in food powders. Bonan et al. (2013) used high- performance liquid chromatography coupled with diode- array detector for simultaneous detection of different synthetic dyes including metanil yellow in food stuffs 221. Similarly, liquid chromatography-electrospray tandem mass spectrometry was used to detect 40 different dyes (such as metanil yellow, orange II, Uranine ete) in soft drinks 231. Due to the operational complexities and sample destructive nature, such analytical methods are limited to laboratory purpose only. Optical method is an altemative for such conventional technique. Optical methods are gaining importance in agricultural and food research due to its simple and nondestructive characteristics [2+25. Diffrent optical methods are developed and utilized for detection of chemical contaminants in food and food products such as Raman technique for detection of melamine in milk 26, detection of pesticide residue in fruits [241, detection of starch adulterated garlic powder [27. Thistudy utilized FT-Raman and FT-IR spectroscopy as a complementary technique for detection of metanil yellow contamination in turmeric powder. FTRaman and FT-IR spectroscopy has been extensively used for detection of different types of adulterants and chemical contaminants in food and food products 2-30] FT-Raman and FT-IR spectrocopy have their respective advantages over the other and can be used as a complementary technique. Both spectroscopic techniques were utilized for spectral analysis of turmeric powder, metanil yellow and mixture of these two powders."}], "document_id": "_ Detection of metanil yellow contamination in turmeric using FT-Raman and FT-IR spectroscopy. Sensi", "line": 15, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Detection of metanil yellow contamination in turmeric using FT-Raman and FT-IR spectroscopy. Sensi.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1248, "start": 1232}], "text": "Turmeric (Curuma long L.) is a yellow color herbaceous root which is extensively used as food spices and also for medicinal purposes. It is traded in different form such as turmeric powder, turmeric oil, turmeric root etc. Turmeric has long been used as food flavoring and food coloring agent in Asian diet  It is also utilized in home medicine and pharmaceutical applications. Turmeric is applied to fresh wounds, bruises and insect bites ] as home remedy. The medicinal value of turmeric is mainly due to the yellow pigment called curcumin (diferuloyl methane) content in it 3. Curcumin is also the source of yellowish orange color in turmeric. Curcumin which is present at varying concentration range in turmeric (0.3% to 8.6%) (3- is the main source of its medicinal value such as anti-flammatory , anti-cancer anti-oxidant I, and wound healing  Varying concentration of curcumin in turmeric is mainly due to nurient and acidity content in soi , ferilizer 2, sol ye , and cultivar  Low conceration of curumin n umeric, and isolation of curcumin from turmeric for medicinal purpose has motivated contamination of turmeric by chemicals as a substitute to curcumin ). Although whole, dried and fresh turmeric are free of any chemical contamination, turmeric powder is generally contaminated with yellow colored chemicals as a substitute for curcumin for color and weight. Curcuma zedoaria a wild relative of Curcuma long L., which has close resemblance to turmeric, is mixed with turmeric powder [15). Similarly, turmeric powder is reportedly adulterated with other chemicals such as metanil yellow as a substitute to curcumin for appearance ]. Metanil yellow (CigH4NNaOS) is a toxic azo dye which is classfied as CII category by Joint FAO/wHO Expert commitee on Food Additives. According to the study on rates, long term consumption of metanil yellow can cause neurotoxicity , petatocellular carcinoma 8), tumor (9, liver carcinogenesis [201, and lymphocytic leukemia 21]]"}, {"context": [{"line": 90, "text": "The thickness variation of MOCVD GaN homo-epitaxial thick layers (∼10 μm) grown with the SinTaC and pBNcoated graphite heaters, respectively, was measured using an interference film thickness meter to determine the effect of heater material on the in-plane temperature distribution. Figure 5(c) shows the thickness variation estimated from the interference from a white light source for MOCVDgrown GaN epilayers obtained with the SinTaC and pBNcoated graphite heaters, respectively. The convex-shaped thickness distributions are similar. The σ/mean values of the thickness variation for epilayers obtained with the SinTaC and pBN-coated graphite heaters (obtained from data taken at 7473 locations throughout the 2 inch wafers) were 6.0% and 5.3%, respectively, which confirms that there was essentially no difference in thickness variation. Therefore, it is concluded that the in-plane temperature distribution obtained with the SinTaC heater was similar to that obtained with the conventional pBN-coated graphite heater. In addition, the PL spectra of the thick epilayers [inset in Fig. 5(c)] obtained for the two heaters exhibited no significant difference, which suggests that the types and densities of intrinsic defects and extrinsic defects (impurities) in the epilayers were almost identical."}, {"line": 91, "text": ""}, {"line": 92, "text": "To assess the impurity elements and their concentrations, we carried out SIMS analyzes. Figures 6(a) and 6(b) show the SIMS depth profiles (primary ion: Cs+) of impurity elements (H, C, O, and Si; major light-element impurities found in MOCVD-grown GaN epilayers) obtained from homo-epitaxial MOCVD GaN epilayers (layer stacking structures grown with various V/III source gas input ratios are shown in the figures) grown with the SinTaC and pBNcoated graphite heaters. The C concentration in a GaN epilayer is generally sensitive to both the V/III source gas input ratio and temperature.52,53) The layered samples in the experiments were grown with various V/III ratios at the same set temperatures. Figure 6(c) shows the average C concentration as a function of V/III ratio for samples grown with the two types of heater. The C concentration strongly depends on the V/III ratio. The C concentrations for samples grown with different heater elements are identical for a given V/III ratio. The results indicate no significant change in the growth temperature; this is consistent with the PL results (which suggested identical intrinsic/extrinsic defect densities). Furthermore, to confirm the absence of heavy-metal contamination from the TaC layer of the SinTaC heater, we measured the SIMS mass spectrum (primary ion: O2 ); the results are shown in Fig. 6(d). The significant peaks in the mass spectrum are all from GaN or incident ions (O and Cs); therefore, there was no significant contamination from the TaC layer. Thus, we can conclude that MOCVD GaN epilayers grown with the SinTaC heater have almost the same quality as that of those grown with a conventional heater."}, {"line": 93, "text": ""}, {"line": 94, "text": "# 4. Conclusions"}], "document_id": "_ Resistive heater element made of highly durable TaC-coated graphite for high-temperature and highl", "line": 92, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Resistive heater element made of highly durable TaC-coated graphite for high-temperature and highl.md", "submatches": [{"end": 1228, "start": 1212}], "text": "To assess the impurity elements and their concentrations, we carried out SIMS analyzes. Figures 6(a) and 6(b) show the SIMS depth profiles (primary ion: Cs+) of impurity elements (H, C, O, and Si; major light-element impurities found in MOCVD-grown GaN epilayers) obtained from homo-epitaxial MOCVD GaN epilayers (layer stacking structures grown with various V/III source gas input ratios are shown in the figures) grown with the SinTaC and pBNcoated graphite heaters. The C concentration in a GaN epilayer is generally sensitive to both the V/III source gas input ratio and temperature.52,53) The layered samples in the experiments were grown with various V/III ratios at the same set temperatures. Figure 6(c) shows the average C concentration as a function of V/III ratio for samples grown with the two types of heater. The C concentration strongly depends on the V/III ratio. The C concentrations for samples grown with different heater elements are identical for a given V/III ratio. The results indicate no significant change in the growth temperature; this is consistent with the PL results (which suggested identical intrinsic/extrinsic defect densities). Furthermore, to confirm the absence of heavy-metal contamination from the TaC layer of the SinTaC heater, we measured the SIMS mass spectrum (primary ion: O2 ); the results are shown in Fig. 6(d). The significant peaks in the mass spectrum are all from GaN or incident ions (O and Cs); therefore, there was no significant contamination from the TaC layer. Thus, we can conclude that MOCVD GaN epilayers grown with the SinTaC heater have almost the same quality as that of those grown with a conventional heater."}, {"context": [{"line": 35, "text": "# 1 Introduction"}, {"line": 36, "text": ""}, {"line": 37, "text": "Phosphogypsum (PG) is a waste by-product from the production of phosphoric acid by wet process in phosphorus fertilizer industry. The annual PG produced worldwide is about 250 million tonnes, of which over 80 million tonnes are from China.1-2 Currently, most of the PG generated is still disposed in vast stockpiles and/or into water bodies, resulting in considerable land occupation and severely environmental contamination to soil, water and atmosphere.3-5 Moreover, approximately 15% of PG is recycled as cement retarders, pavements, agricultural fertilizers and building materials.6-10 Therefore, the reutilization of PG is significantly insufficient and also not high-efficient. Thus, the efficient reutilization of PG is very important and urgent."}, {"line": 38, "text": ""}, {"line": 39, "text": "PG is mainly composed of calcium sulfate dihydrate (CsD), usually over 90%, in association with various amounts of silicon, fluorides, phosphates, residual acids, heavy-metal ions and organic substances.,11 TRrougn' dehydration reaction, the CsD in PG can transform into calcium sulfate hemihydrate (CSH) or anhydrite (AH). CSH has two crystalline forms: a-CSH and -CSH. Generally, a-CSH is superior to -CSH due to its higher mechanical properties, better workability and biocompatibility after rehydration and has been granted in construction industry and medical care.12-16 Hence, transforming PG to a-CSH is an effective way to reutilize this by-production. The producing of a-CSH in large scale usually use autoclave method which involves pretreatment of gypsum in an autoclave at an elevated temperature with high steam partial pressure. Salt solution method, which involves heating gypsum in boiling or near-boiling salt solutions under atmospheric pressure for several hours, has been also suggested for a-CSH preparation.1719 Commonly, salt mediums used contain inorganic acids or their salts such as HSO4, KCI, CaCl, MgCl2 and their mixed salts. Compared with autoclave method, the salt solution method with mild reaction conditions is more economic and preferred for transforming CSD into a-CSH.14,20 This phase transformation is considered to proceed preferentially though a dissolution and recrystallization process that CSD dissolves firstly and then a-CSH nucleates and crystallizes in the reaction system.21,22 The crystallization of a-CSH is related closely to some factors such as salt solutions, supersaturation, additives and temperature. Many works have been done to investigate the impacts of these factors and resolve the problems"}], "document_id": "Effects of phosphorus impurities on the preparation of α-calcium sulfate hemihydrate from waste phos", "line": 37, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> of phosphorus impurities on the preparation of α-calcium sulfate hemihydrate from waste phos.md", "submatches": [{"end": 424, "start": 408}], "text": "Phosphogypsum (PG) is a waste by-product from the production of phosphoric acid by wet process in phosphorus fertilizer industry. The annual PG produced worldwide is about 250 million tonnes, of which over 80 million tonnes are from China.1-2 Currently, most of the PG generated is still disposed in vast stockpiles and/or into water bodies, resulting in considerable land occupation and severely environmental contamination to soil, water and atmosphere.3-5 Moreover, approximately 15% of PG is recycled as cement retarders, pavements, agricultural fertilizers and building materials.6-10 Therefore, the reutilization of PG is significantly insufficient and also not high-efficient. Thus, the efficient reutilization of PG is very important and urgent."}], "ok": true, "query": "Al contamination", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 12, "truncated": true}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:12:26.654018+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
workspace/state/tool_calls.jsonl:36:{"duration_ms": 624.622, "request": {"case_sensitive": false, "context_lines": 2, "file_glob": "*.md", "max_results": 12, "query": "ionic conductivity", "regex": false, "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"]}, "response": {"matches": [{"context": [{"line": 8, "text": "Badajoz 06006, Spain"}, {"line": 9, "text": ""}, {"line": 10, "text": "The electrical conductivities of NaCrO2 powders synthesized from Na2CO3 and Cr2O3 reactants with and without mechanical activation at room temperature before the high temperature reaction are investigated in detail. The effect of holding time at the 900°C reaction on the electrical conductivity is studied as well. It is found that the impurities in the powders and structural defects of the resulting NaCrO2 crystals strongly depend on the synthesis conditions. The impurities and specially the crystal defects, in turn, influence the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 powders substantially. In particular, the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 is found to be mainly controlled by the degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in the Na sites of the O3-NaCrO2 layered structure. The bulk ionic conductivity is determined to be 9.8 × 10−7 S/cm when there is only 0.34% misplacement of Cr+3 ions in the Na sites, but drops to 1.7 × 10−7 S/cm when the misplacement increases to 9.97%. Such a strong correlation of more misplacement with lower electrical conductivity is attributed to blocking of Na ion transport on the Na plane by the misplaced Cr+3 ions in the O3-NaCrO2 layered structure."}, {"line": 11, "text": ""}, {"line": 12, "text": "© The Author(s) 2019. Published by ECS. This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License (CC BY, http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted reuse of the work in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. [DOI: 10.1149/2.0081915jes]"}], "document_id": "_ Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s", "line": 10, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s.md", "submatches": [{"end": 803, "start": 785}], "text": "The electrical conductivities of NaCrO2 powders synthesized from Na2CO3 and Cr2O3 reactants with and without mechanical activation at room temperature before the high temperature reaction are investigated in detail. The effect of holding time at the 900°C reaction on the electrical conductivity is studied as well. It is found that the impurities in the powders and structural defects of the resulting NaCrO2 crystals strongly depend on the synthesis conditions. The impurities and specially the crystal defects, in turn, influence the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 powders substantially. In particular, the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 is found to be mainly controlled by the degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in the Na sites of the O3-NaCrO2 layered structure. The bulk ionic conductivity is determined to be 9.8 × 10−7 S/cm when there is only 0.34% misplacement of Cr+3 ions in the Na sites, but drops to 1.7 × 10−7 S/cm when the misplacement increases to 9.97%. Such a strong correlation of more misplacement with lower electrical conductivity is attributed to blocking of Na ion transport on the Na plane by the misplaced Cr+3 ions in the O3-NaCrO2 layered structure."}, {"context": [{"line": 32, "text": "Structural analysis of the four NaCrO2 powders was conducted by X-ray diffractometry (XRD) using a Bruker D2 diffractometer with a Cu Kα source scanning from 10° to 90° with step of 0.02°. Each step takes 1 second, and the 0.19 volt filter is on to filter fluorescence. All samples were mixed with 5 wt% of micrometer-sized Si powder as the internal standard before the data collection, which was introduced to account for the instrumental shifts in the XRD patterns in order to measure lattice parameters precisely. The phases present were first identified with the aid of the PDF2 database, after which the XRD patterns were analyzed using the Rietveld and Pawley methods as described elsewhere.15,16"}, {"line": 33, "text": ""}, {"line": 34, "text": "Characterization of electrical conductivity.—The electrical conductivity of the four types of NaCrO2 powders was measured using EIS. The measurement procedure used is the one developed previously to measure the ionic conductivity of Na3MnCO3PO4 cathode material.17 Briefly, a special die with a plastic shell and two copper rods (upper and lower punches) was custom made for the EIS measurements. The diameter of the copper rod was 0.96 cm, and the grinded NaCrO2 powders were individually pressed into pellets inside the plastic shell by applying a pressure of 130 MPa to the copper rods. During the EIS measurement, the entire die was clamped with a specially designed vise to ensure a good contact between the pellet and the two copper rods. The EIS measurements were conducted in the frequency range of 100,000 Hz to 100 Hz with an amplitude of 10 mV using a frequency response analyzer (Parstat 4000, Princeton Applied Research). For the sake of comparison, EIS measurements were also performed directly on the as-synthesized BM-2h pellets without grinding into powder, i.e., on the “BM-2h pellet”."}, {"line": 35, "text": ""}, {"line": 36, "text": "# Results and Discussion"}], "document_id": "_ Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s", "line": 34, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s.md", "submatches": [{"end": 231, "start": 213}], "text": "Characterization of electrical conductivity.—The electrical conductivity of the four types of NaCrO2 powders was measured using EIS. The measurement procedure used is the one developed previously to measure the ionic conductivity of Na3MnCO3PO4 cathode material.17 Briefly, a special die with a plastic shell and two copper rods (upper and lower punches) was custom made for the EIS measurements. The diameter of the copper rod was 0.96 cm, and the grinded NaCrO2 powders were individually pressed into pellets inside the plastic shell by applying a pressure of 130 MPa to the copper rods. During the EIS measurement, the entire die was clamped with a specially designed vise to ensure a good contact between the pellet and the two copper rods. The EIS measurements were conducted in the frequency range of 100,000 Hz to 100 Hz with an amplitude of 10 mV using a frequency response analyzer (Parstat 4000, Princeton Applied Research). For the sake of comparison, EIS measurements were also performed directly on the as-synthesized BM-2h pellets without grinding into powder, i.e., on the “BM-2h pellet”."}, {"context": [{"line": 87, "text": "Figure 7. Schematic cross-sections of powder compacts with ionic transport length as a function of particle size during EIS measurement. See the text fo discussion."}, {"line": 88, "text": ""}, {"line": 89, "text": "Third, the following order of decreasing the ionic conductivity for the particle interface conductivity (σint) is observed: BM-2h pellet > BM-2h > BM-cool > SM-2h > SM-cool. This order indicates that NaCrO2 with high-energy ball milling before the 900°C reaction have higher σint than NaCrO2 without ball milling. As such, Na ion transport between NaCrO2 particles can be enhanced by mechanical activation before the high temperature reaction. Furthermore, holding at 900°C for 2 h also improves the particle interface conductivity for both BM and SM samples, suggesting that the defect structure or the content of defects on the surface of NaCrO2 particles have changed during 2-h holding at 900°C. It is worthy of pointing out that to the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that the particle interface conductivity has been reported."}, {"line": 90, "text": ""}, {"line": 91, "text": "Fourth, the order of the bulk ionic conductivity (σb) is slightly different from the particle interface conductivity. Specifically, the bulk ionic conductivity has the order of BM-2h > SM-2h > BM-cool > SM-cool. Although this order is slightly different from σint, it still reveals that (i) high-energy ball milling before the high temperature reaction enhances the ionic conductivity and (ii) holding at 900°C for 2 h improves the ionic conductivity when all other processing conditions are the same. It should be pointed out that the switching from σint (BM-cool) > σint (SM-2h) to σb (SM-2h) > σb (BM-cool) should be taken cautiously because it may not be a true phenomenon for the following reasons. First, the bulk conductivities of all samples have been underestimated when Eq. 1 was used to compute the bulk conductivity. This point can be explained with the aid of Figure 7, where powder compacts with different particle sizes are shown schematically. As seen in Eq. 1, the nominal length (L) and the nominal cross-sectional area (A) of a powder compact are used to convert the resistance (R) of the compact to the conductivity (σ) of the compact. However, actually the true length for electrical conduction is L1 for a powder compact with particle size of D (Figure 7a), and L > L because the presence of porosity in the powder compact leads to a tortuous pathway for electrical conduction. Furthermore, the true cross-sectional area A1 is smaller than the nominal one (A1 < A, not shown in Figure 7) because of the porosity in the powder compact. Thus, if A1 and L1 were known, their values should be inserted into Eq. 1 to compute the true bulk conductivity (σb1):"}], "document_id": "_ Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s", "line": 89, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s.md", "submatches": [{"end": 63, "start": 45}], "text": "Third, the following order of decreasing the ionic conductivity for the particle interface conductivity (σint) is observed: BM-2h pellet > BM-2h > BM-cool > SM-2h > SM-cool. This order indicates that NaCrO2 with high-energy ball milling before the 900°C reaction have higher σint than NaCrO2 without ball milling. As such, Na ion transport between NaCrO2 particles can be enhanced by mechanical activation before the high temperature reaction. Furthermore, holding at 900°C for 2 h also improves the particle interface conductivity for both BM and SM samples, suggesting that the defect structure or the content of defects on the surface of NaCrO2 particles have changed during 2-h holding at 900°C. It is worthy of pointing out that to the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that the particle interface conductivity has been reported."}, {"context": [{"line": 89, "text": "Third, the following order of decreasing the ionic conductivity for the particle interface conductivity (σint) is observed: BM-2h pellet > BM-2h > BM-cool > SM-2h > SM-cool. This order indicates that NaCrO2 with high-energy ball milling before the 900°C reaction have higher σint than NaCrO2 without ball milling. As such, Na ion transport between NaCrO2 particles can be enhanced by mechanical activation before the high temperature reaction. Furthermore, holding at 900°C for 2 h also improves the particle interface conductivity for both BM and SM samples, suggesting that the defect structure or the content of defects on the surface of NaCrO2 particles have changed during 2-h holding at 900°C. It is worthy of pointing out that to the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that the particle interface conductivity has been reported."}, {"line": 90, "text": ""}, {"line": 91, "text": "Fourth, the order of the bulk ionic conductivity (σb) is slightly different from the particle interface conductivity. Specifically, the bulk ionic conductivity has the order of BM-2h > SM-2h > BM-cool > SM-cool. Although this order is slightly different from σint, it still reveals that (i) high-energy ball milling before the high temperature reaction enhances the ionic conductivity and (ii) holding at 900°C for 2 h improves the ionic conductivity when all other processing conditions are the same. It should be pointed out that the switching from σint (BM-cool) > σint (SM-2h) to σb (SM-2h) > σb (BM-cool) should be taken cautiously because it may not be a true phenomenon for the following reasons. First, the bulk conductivities of all samples have been underestimated when Eq. 1 was used to compute the bulk conductivity. This point can be explained with the aid of Figure 7, where powder compacts with different particle sizes are shown schematically. As seen in Eq. 1, the nominal length (L) and the nominal cross-sectional area (A) of a powder compact are used to convert the resistance (R) of the compact to the conductivity (σ) of the compact. However, actually the true length for electrical conduction is L1 for a powder compact with particle size of D (Figure 7a), and L > L because the presence of porosity in the powder compact leads to a tortuous pathway for electrical conduction. Furthermore, the true cross-sectional area A1 is smaller than the nominal one (A1 < A, not shown in Figure 7) because of the porosity in the powder compact. Thus, if A1 and L1 were known, their values should be inserted into Eq. 1 to compute the true bulk conductivity (σb1):"}, {"line": 92, "text": ""}, {"line": 93, "text": "![](images/37eb78a86ded6750c4db1eafe3ece6c9e367bc93bb988a6e29c81749506a4844.jpg)"}], "document_id": "_ Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s", "line": 91, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s.md", "submatches": [{"end": 48, "start": 30}, {"end": 160, "start": 142}, {"end": 386, "start": 368}, {"end": 453, "start": 435}], "text": "Fourth, the order of the bulk ionic conductivity (σb) is slightly different from the particle interface conductivity. Specifically, the bulk ionic conductivity has the order of BM-2h > SM-2h > BM-cool > SM-cool. Although this order is slightly different from σint, it still reveals that (i) high-energy ball milling before the high temperature reaction enhances the ionic conductivity and (ii) holding at 900°C for 2 h improves the ionic conductivity when all other processing conditions are the same. It should be pointed out that the switching from σint (BM-cool) > σint (SM-2h) to σb (SM-2h) > σb (BM-cool) should be taken cautiously because it may not be a true phenomenon for the following reasons. First, the bulk conductivities of all samples have been underestimated when Eq. 1 was used to compute the bulk conductivity. This point can be explained with the aid of Figure 7, where powder compacts with different particle sizes are shown schematically. As seen in Eq. 1, the nominal length (L) and the nominal cross-sectional area (A) of a powder compact are used to convert the resistance (R) of the compact to the conductivity (σ) of the compact. However, actually the true length for electrical conduction is L1 for a powder compact with particle size of D (Figure 7a), and L > L because the presence of porosity in the powder compact leads to a tortuous pathway for electrical conduction. Furthermore, the true cross-sectional area A1 is smaller than the nominal one (A1 < A, not shown in Figure 7) because of the porosity in the powder compact. Thus, if A1 and L1 were known, their values should be inserted into Eq. 1 to compute the true bulk conductivity (σb1):"}, {"context": [{"line": 115, "text": "![](images/3d608b7bd96487d7aae5312e9eb00875d32ac1627af93be6ca4d4f42a75f16ff.jpg)"}, {"line": 116, "text": ""}, {"line": 117, "text": "Eqs. 5 and 6 are valid because comparisons are made with similar particle sizes either in the nanoscale or in the submicrometer range. Eqs. 7 and 8 are also valid because the ionic conductivities of BM samples are significantly underestimated but still larger than those of SM samples. Therefore, it can be concluded that both the bulk ionic conductivity and the conductivity at the particle interface increase with (i) high-energy ball milling before the high temperature reaction and (ii) holding at 900°C for 2 h."}, {"line": 118, "text": ""}, {"line": 119, "text": "Finally, it is noted that σb (BM-2h) and σb (BM-2h pellet) are similar but not identical. The similar values of these two bulk conductivities are expected because they both reflect the Na-ion conductivity within crystalline NaCrO2 particles synthesized at the same reaction temperature and holding time. However, their powder packing densities are different with one measured using the as-synthesized pellet (BM-2h pellet sample) and the other measured after grinding the as-synthesized pellet into powder and then pressing the powder into pellets again (BM-2h sample). The BM-2h pellet sample has a denser packing density than the BM-2h sample because the former is in a partially densified state and the latter is a powder compact with the same particle sizes.21 As such, the length for electrical conduction and the cross-section area for electrical conduction of the BM-2h pellet sample can be regarded to be similar to the nominal values of L and A in Eq. 1, respectively. In contrast, the length for electrical conduction of the BM-2h sample, L’, will be slight longer than L, while the cross-section area for electrical conduction of the BM-2h sample, A’, will be smaller than A because of the looser particle packing of the BM-2h sample (see, for example, Figure 7b). As shown in Eq. 1, the conductivity value depends on the L’/A’ ratio. Given that L’ is larger than L and A’ is smaller than A, one can expect the L’/A’ ratio to be deviated from the L/A ratio. Therefore, similar values of σb (BM-2h) and σ (BM-2h pellet), but not identical, are expected. This is indeed the case because the difference between σb (BM-2h) and σb (BM-2h pellet) is only 10% (Table II)."}], "document_id": "_ Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s", "line": 117, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s.md", "submatches": [{"end": 354, "start": 336}], "text": "Eqs. 5 and 6 are valid because comparisons are made with similar particle sizes either in the nanoscale or in the submicrometer range. Eqs. 7 and 8 are also valid because the ionic conductivities of BM samples are significantly underestimated but still larger than those of SM samples. Therefore, it can be concluded that both the bulk ionic conductivity and the conductivity at the particle interface increase with (i) high-energy ball milling before the high temperature reaction and (ii) holding at 900°C for 2 h."}, {"context": [{"line": 127, "text": "It is worthy of emphasizing again that the relationships 5, 6, 7 and 8 observed experimentally in this study are due to structural defects (particularly misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites), not by the presence of small amounts of P2-NaxCrO2 and unreacted Cr2O3 phases. As discussed above, the major effect of P2-NaxCrO2 on the measured electrical conductivity can be ruled out by comparing the quantities of the P2-phase in BM-2h and BM-cool samples. The quantities of the P2-phase in these two samples are very small (i.e., 0.4% vs. 1.0%, respectively) and similar, but BM-2h has ∼100% higher σb and ∼65% higher σint that BM-cool. These results clearly reveal that the P2-phase does not play a critical role in controlling the electrical conductivities of BM-2h and BM-cool samples. The major effect of P2-NaxCrO2 in controlling the electrical conductivity can also be ruled out by comparing the electrical conductivities of SM-2h and SM-cool samples. As shown in Table I, both SM-2h and SM-cool contain relatively high P2-NaxCrO2 phase (one being ∼5.7% and the other ∼4.1%). Thus, the quantities of the P2-phase in these two samples are similar, but the electrical conductivity of SM-2h is about 10 times that of SM-cool. It is unreasonable/unrealistic that ∼1.5% difference in the P2-phase has led to one order of magnitude change in the electrical conductivity between SM-2h and SM-cool since both contain relatively high P2-phase quantities. Therefore, the P2-phase does not play a critical role in controlling the measured electrical conductivity in this study. Similar reasoning related to the unreacted Cr2O3 can also easily rule out the possibility of the major effect of the unreacted Cr2O3 on the electrical conductivities of SM-2h and SM-cool samples. This discussion has been described in the paragraph above and, therefore, will not be repeated here."}, {"line": 128, "text": ""}, {"line": 129, "text": "Before closing, it is important to elucidate whether the electrical conductivity measured in this study is mainly ionic conductivity or electronic conductivity. It is proposed that the electrical conductivity measured here is mainly ionic conductivity. This hypothesis is supported by several reasons. First, O3-NaCrO2 has the same crystal structure as LiCoO2 (i.e., both have a rhombohedral (R3¯m) O3 type layered structure). It is well known that Li CoO is an electronic insulator when x is close to unity, but becomes a conductor when x adopts a lower value (< 1).22–24 If NaCrO2 behaves similarly as LiCoO2 and the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 is electronic-conductivity dominated, then NaxCrO2 (x < 1) would have higher electrical conductivities than NaCrO2. However, this is clearly not the case for the data listed in Tables I and II, which show that SM-cool and SM-2h powders have O3-NaxCrO2 phase with x 0.73 but lower electric conductivities than BM-cool and BM-2h powders (that have O3-NaxCrO2 phase with x 0.93). These results imply that either the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 is not electronic-conductivity dominated, or NaCrO2 has a very different behavior from LiCoO2 even though they have identical crystal structure. The second reason for the electric conductivity of NaCrO2 being dominated by the ionic conductivity is the good correlation between the degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites and the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2. Based on Tables I and II, BM-2h has the lowest degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites and the highest electric conductivity, while SMcool has the highest degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites and the lowest electric conductivity. Such correlations strongly suggest that the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 is dominated by ionic conductivity because high degrees of cation mixing result in blocking and interference of Na ion transport on the Na plane and thus low ionic conductivity."}, {"line": 130, "text": ""}, {"line": 131, "text": "# Concluding Remarks"}], "document_id": "_ Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s", "line": 129, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s.md", "submatches": [{"end": 132, "start": 114}, {"end": 251, "start": 233}, {"end": 1347, "start": 1329}, {"end": 1836, "start": 1818}, {"end": 1978, "start": 1960}], "text": "Before closing, it is important to elucidate whether the electrical conductivity measured in this study is mainly ionic conductivity or electronic conductivity. It is proposed that the electrical conductivity measured here is mainly ionic conductivity. This hypothesis is supported by several reasons. First, O3-NaCrO2 has the same crystal structure as LiCoO2 (i.e., both have a rhombohedral (R3¯m) O3 type layered structure). It is well known that Li CoO is an electronic insulator when x is close to unity, but becomes a conductor when x adopts a lower value (< 1).22–24 If NaCrO2 behaves similarly as LiCoO2 and the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 is electronic-conductivity dominated, then NaxCrO2 (x < 1) would have higher electrical conductivities than NaCrO2. However, this is clearly not the case for the data listed in Tables I and II, which show that SM-cool and SM-2h powders have O3-NaxCrO2 phase with x 0.73 but lower electric conductivities than BM-cool and BM-2h powders (that have O3-NaxCrO2 phase with x 0.93). These results imply that either the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 is not electronic-conductivity dominated, or NaCrO2 has a very different behavior from LiCoO2 even though they have identical crystal structure. The second reason for the electric conductivity of NaCrO2 being dominated by the ionic conductivity is the good correlation between the degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites and the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2. Based on Tables I and II, BM-2h has the lowest degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites and the highest electric conductivity, while SMcool has the highest degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites and the lowest electric conductivity. Such correlations strongly suggest that the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 is dominated by ionic conductivity because high degrees of cation mixing result in blocking and interference of Na ion transport on the Na plane and thus low ionic conductivity."}, {"context": [{"line": 138, "text": "4. The electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 is mainly controlled by the degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites. The more misplacement, the lower the electrical conductivity. Such a cor"}, {"line": 139, "text": ""}, {"line": 140, "text": "relation is due to blocking of Na ion transport on the Na plane by the misplaced Cr+3 ions in O3-NaCrO2 layered structure. 5. The good correlation between the degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites and the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 suggests that the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 determined through EIS measurement is dominated by the ionic conductivity."}, {"line": 141, "text": ""}, {"line": 142, "text": "# Acknowledgments"}], "document_id": "_ Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s", "line": 140, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Unraveling Processing-Structure-Electrical Conductivity Relationships of NaCrO &lt;sub_&gt;2&lt;_s.md", "submatches": [{"end": 374, "start": 356}], "text": "relation is due to blocking of Na ion transport on the Na plane by the misplaced Cr+3 ions in O3-NaCrO2 layered structure. 5. The good correlation between the degree of misplacement of Cr+3 ions in Na sites and the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 suggests that the electrical conductivity of NaCrO2 determined through EIS measurement is dominated by the ionic conductivity."}, {"context": [{"line": 3, "text": "G. B. Kunshinaa, \\*, V. I. Ivanenkoa, and I. V. Bocharovaa aTananaev Institute of Chemistry, Subdivision of Federal Research Center “Kola Scientific Center, Russian Academy of Sciences,” Apatity, Murmansk oblast, 184209 Russia \\*e-mail: kunshina@chemy.kolasc.net.ru Received October 3, 2018; revised November 21, 2018; accepted January 22, 2019"}, {"line": 4, "text": ""}, {"line": 5, "text": "Abstract—The method of solid-phase sintering was used to synthesize samples of lithium-conducting Li6.4Al0.2La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte with a garnet structure. Higher technological efficiency of the method of synthesis was achieved owing to using low-melting nitrate crystalline hydrates of ZrO(NO3)2 · 2H2O and La(NO3)3 · 6H2O as initial substances: formation of the melt considerably intensified the interaction, caused a decrease in the duration and energy consumption of the method, and provided the presence of only a single phase of powdered solid electrolyte with a high Li-ion conductivity. The excess of the lithium-containing component (Li2CO3) in the mix was varied to compensate lithium losses in the course of the high-temperature treatment. Specific ionic conductivity of Li6.4Al0.2La3Zr2O12 was determined using the impedance spectroscopy technique and was 2 × 10–4 S/cm at the room temperature."}, {"line": 6, "text": ""}, {"line": 7, "text": "Keywords: lithium-conducting solid electrolyte, lithium–lanthanum zirconate, synthesis, ion conductivity, electrochemical impedance"}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132", "line": 5, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132.md", "submatches": [{"end": 787, "start": 769}], "text": "Abstract—The method of solid-phase sintering was used to synthesize samples of lithium-conducting Li6.4Al0.2La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte with a garnet structure. Higher technological efficiency of the method of synthesis was achieved owing to using low-melting nitrate crystalline hydrates of ZrO(NO3)2 · 2H2O and La(NO3)3 · 6H2O as initial substances: formation of the melt considerably intensified the interaction, caused a decrease in the duration and energy consumption of the method, and provided the presence of only a single phase of powdered solid electrolyte with a high Li-ion conductivity. The excess of the lithium-containing component (Li2CO3) in the mix was varied to compensate lithium losses in the course of the high-temperature treatment. Specific ionic conductivity of Li6.4Al0.2La3Zr2O12 was determined using the impedance spectroscopy technique and was 2 × 10–4 S/cm at the room temperature."}, {"context": [{"line": 110, "text": "9. Il’ina, E.A., Andreev, O.L., Antonov, B.D., and Batalov, N.N., Morphology and transport properties of the solid electrolyte Li7La3Zr2O12 prepared by the solidstate and citrate-nitrate methods, J. Power Sources, 2012, vol. 201, p. 169.   "}, {"line": 111, "text": "10. Yoshida, T., Honda, A., and Sato, Y., Aluminiumdoped Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte and process for producing the same, EEC Patent no. EP 2159867 A1, 2010.   "}, {"line": 112, "text": "11. Shao, C., Liu, H., Yu, Z., Zheng, Z., Sun, N., and Diao, C., Structure and ionic conductivity of cubic Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by chemical co-precipitation method, Solid State Ionics, 2016, vol. 287, p. 13.   "}, {"line": 113, "text": "12. Zhao, P., Cao, G., Jin, Z., Ming, H., Wen, Y., Xu, Y., Zhu, X., Xiang, Y., and Zhang, S., Self-consolidation mechanism and its application in the preparation of Aldoped cubic Li7La3Zr2O12, Mater. Des., 2018, vol. 139, p. 65.   "}, {"line": 114, "text": "13. Weller, J.M., Whetten, J.A., and Chan, C.K., Synthesis of Fine Cubic Li La Zr O Powders in Molten LiCl– KCl Eutectic and Facile Densification by Reversal of Li+/H+ Exchange, ACS Appl. Energy Mater., 2018, vol. 1, no. 2, p. 552.   "}], "document_id": "_ Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132", "line": 112, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> Synthesis and Study of Conductivity of Al-Substituted Li7La3Zr2O12 _ 10.1134_S1023193519060132.md", "submatches": [{"end": 97, "start": 79}], "text": "11. Shao, C., Liu, H., Yu, Z., Zheng, Z., Sun, N., and Diao, C., Structure and ionic conductivity of cubic Li7La3Zr2O12 solid electrolyte prepared by chemical co-precipitation method, Solid State Ionics, 2016, vol. 287, p. 13.   "}, {"context": [{"line": 1, "text": "# Evaluation on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like LisLaNb2O12"}, {"line": 2, "text": ""}, {"line": 3, "text": "Dana Pinzaru and Venkataraman Thangadurai\\* Department of Chemistry, University of Calgary, 2500 University Drive NW, Calgary, AB, T2N 1N4, Canada \\*For correspondence ythangad@ucalgary.ca; phone: 1 403 210 8649."}], "document_id": "Evaluation on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like", "line": 1, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like.md", "submatches": [{"end": 87, "start": 69}], "text": "# Evaluation on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like LisLaNb2O12"}, {"context": [{"line": 3, "text": "Dana Pinzaru and Venkataraman Thangadurai\\* Department of Chemistry, University of Calgary, 2500 University Drive NW, Calgary, AB, T2N 1N4, Canada \\*For correspondence ythangad@ucalgary.ca; phone: 1 403 210 8649."}, {"line": 4, "text": ""}, {"line": 5, "text": "Abstract. There is a great interest in gaining a deeper understanding of the Li ion conductivity of Li stuffed garnets that result from the substitution at either the La or M sites in LisLaM2O12 (M = Nb, Ta), since these materials can be used as potential electrolytes in all solid state batteries.Here, we investigate the effect of replacing Nb5+ with Gd3+ and stuffing two Li\\* per Gd3+ in LisLaNbO12 on the structure and ionic conductivity of the new family of garnet-type compounds with the nominal formula Lis+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). The samples were prepared via conventional solid-state synthesis in air at elevated temperatures. Powder X-ray diffraction (PxRD) studies confirmed the formation of the garnet-type structure for Li5+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). Elemental mapping obtained using energy dispersive X-ray analysis (EDS) showed no evidence of a secondary phase being formed in Li5+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). Archimedes' density measurements confirmed that the density of the samples with x  0.25 is not improved. The porosity of the samples with x  0.3 was found to be in the 8- 16 % range Among the samples investigated in this work, the x = 0.45 member of Lis+2xLa3Nb2- xGd,O12 showed the highest ionic conductivity of 1.91 x 10- S cm' at room temperature, which is an order of magnitude higher than that of the parent compound LisLaNb2O12. The activation energy of this material was calculated to be 0.38 eV at 25 - 225 C and 0.28 eV at 225 - 325 C. The change in the activation energy at high temperature regime can be explained using the Lisite occupation model in the garnet-type structure."}, {"line": 6, "text": ""}, {"line": 7, "text": "Keywords: Li-stuffed garnets, Solid Li ion electrolytes, Lis+2xLa3Nb2-xGd,O12 (0  x  0.45), Conductivity"}], "document_id": "Evaluation on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like", "line": 5, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like.md", "submatches": [{"end": 442, "start": 424}, {"end": 1245, "start": 1227}], "text": "Abstract. There is a great interest in gaining a deeper understanding of the Li ion conductivity of Li stuffed garnets that result from the substitution at either the La or M sites in LisLaM2O12 (M = Nb, Ta), since these materials can be used as potential electrolytes in all solid state batteries.Here, we investigate the effect of replacing Nb5+ with Gd3+ and stuffing two Li\\* per Gd3+ in LisLaNbO12 on the structure and ionic conductivity of the new family of garnet-type compounds with the nominal formula Lis+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). The samples were prepared via conventional solid-state synthesis in air at elevated temperatures. Powder X-ray diffraction (PxRD) studies confirmed the formation of the garnet-type structure for Li5+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). Elemental mapping obtained using energy dispersive X-ray analysis (EDS) showed no evidence of a secondary phase being formed in Li5+2xLa3Nb2-xGdO12 (0  x  0.45). Archimedes' density measurements confirmed that the density of the samples with x  0.25 is not improved. The porosity of the samples with x  0.3 was found to be in the 8- 16 % range Among the samples investigated in this work, the x = 0.45 member of Lis+2xLa3Nb2- xGd,O12 showed the highest ionic conductivity of 1.91 x 10- S cm' at room temperature, which is an order of magnitude higher than that of the parent compound LisLaNb2O12. The activation energy of this material was calculated to be 0.38 eV at 25 - 225 C and 0.28 eV at 225 - 325 C. The change in the activation energy at high temperature regime can be explained using the Lisite occupation model in the garnet-type structure."}, {"context": [{"line": 15, "text": "LigLa3M2O12 (M = Nb, Ta) was first reported by Mazza in 1988 to crystallize in the garnet-like framework.1o What is unusual about these Li containing garnets is that there is a greater concentration of Li ions than the ideal garnet structure can accommodate in the tetrahedral (24d) sites. The extra Li ion ions reside in the octahedral (48g) sites. Not all of the octahedral sites are occupied by Li ions, leaving site vacancies, suggesting that these compounds may be able of conducting Li ions.'' Consequently, Thangadurai et al reported in 2003 a total (bulk + grain-boundary) Li ion conductivity of 106 S cm' at room temperature for LisLaM2O12.12 Since their discovery as Li ion conductors, there has been an increased interest in the development of garnet-type Li ion solid electrolytes for all solid-state LIBs. The Ta-based analog has been proven to be stable against chemical reaction with Li metal, as well as electrochemically stable up to 6 V vs. Li/Lit. These attributes, together with the good Li ion conductivity make them ideal electrolyte candidates for all solid-state LIBs. Researchers have attempted to further improve the conductivity by various substitutions at either the La site or the M site.8,9"}, {"line": 16, "text": ""}, {"line": 17, "text": "Partial substitution at the M-site of the pentavalent ion with a trivalent ion yields new members of the garnet-like family of compounds, with higher Li content. The larger ionic radius of Sc3+ (0.89 A), y3+ (0.90 A), Sm3+ (0.96 A) and In3+ (0.79 A), compared to the radii of Nb5+ (0.64 A) and Ta+ (0.64 A) resulted in an increased lattice parameter.13-18 The increase in Li content, together with the larger lattice parameters of the doped members, are expected to increase the total Li ion conductivity of the materials. For example, Li5.5LaNb1.5Ino.5O12 showed a total Li ion conductivity of 104 at 50 C, while the reported conductivity for Li6.25La3Nb1.375Sco.625012 is 3.7 x 104 S cm1.14,17 Recentl, our group stdied the effct of Y doping on Lis+2xLa?M2-xYxO12 (M = Nb, Ta). The x = 0.75 member of the Nb family of compounds displayed a high bulk ionic conductivity of 2.7 x 104 S cm' at room temperature, while the Ta analog with the same amount of Y-doping showed a bulk ionic conductivity of 1.8 x 104 S cm1 15,16 30 mol % Sm-doped Lis.LaNb1.7Smo.3012 showed a total Li ion conductivity of 5.8 x 10- S cm' at room temperature.' Recently, Ahmad and Al-Quaimi have investigated the origin of enhanced Li-ion conductivity in Gd-doped Lis+2xLa3Nb2-xGdxO12.1' However, only four levels of doping have been investigated and there was little discussion on the structural aspects of these compounds. Meanwhile, our group studied the same family of compounds in the 0 - 0.45 range, increasing the dopant level in 0.05 increments. An interesting trend was observed for dopant levels less than 0.10. In the present work, we investigate the effect of Gd and Li substitution in the parent phase on the structure and ionic conductivity."}, {"line": 18, "text": ""}, {"line": 19, "text": "# Experimental aspects"}], "document_id": "Evaluation on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like", "line": 17, "path": "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH> on the Effect of Gd-doping for Nb on the Morphology and Ionic Conductivity of Garnet-like.md", "submatches": [{"end": 870, "start": 852}, {"end": 996, "start": 978}, {"end": 1729, "start": 1711}], "text": "Partial substitution at the M-site of the pentavalent ion with a trivalent ion yields new members of the garnet-like family of compounds, with higher Li content. The larger ionic radius of Sc3+ (0.89 A), y3+ (0.90 A), Sm3+ (0.96 A) and In3+ (0.79 A), compared to the radii of Nb5+ (0.64 A) and Ta+ (0.64 A) resulted in an increased lattice parameter.13-18 The increase in Li content, together with the larger lattice parameters of the doped members, are expected to increase the total Li ion conductivity of the materials. For example, Li5.5LaNb1.5Ino.5O12 showed a total Li ion conductivity of 104 at 50 C, while the reported conductivity for Li6.25La3Nb1.375Sco.625012 is 3.7 x 104 S cm1.14,17 Recentl, our group stdied the effct of Y doping on Lis+2xLa?M2-xYxO12 (M = Nb, Ta). The x = 0.75 member of the Nb family of compounds displayed a high bulk ionic conductivity of 2.7 x 104 S cm' at room temperature, while the Ta analog with the same amount of Y-doping showed a bulk ionic conductivity of 1.8 x 104 S cm1 15,16 30 mol % Sm-doped Lis.LaNb1.7Smo.3012 showed a total Li ion conductivity of 5.8 x 10- S cm' at room temperature.' Recently, Ahmad and Al-Quaimi have investigated the origin of enhanced Li-ion conductivity in Gd-doped Lis+2xLa3Nb2-xGdxO12.1' However, only four levels of doping have been investigated and there was little discussion on the structural aspects of these compounds. Meanwhile, our group studied the same family of compounds in the 0 - 0.45 range, increasing the dopant level in 0.05 increments. An interesting trend was observed for dopant levels less than 0.10. In the present work, we investigate the effect of Gd and Li substitution in the parent phase on the structure and ionic conductivity."}], "ok": true, "query": "ionic conductivity", "roots": ["<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>", "<PRIVATE_MOUNT_PATH>"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 45.0, "total_returned": 12, "truncated": true}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T10:12:27.588766+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
